window.PAPERSETS = [{"id":"endterm","name":"End-of-term mock exam","short":"End-of-term mock","blurb":"The end-of-term mock, all 53 questions, copied across exactly as they were set: the question, the four options, the key and the worked explanation, with nothing added and nothing reworded. It walks the whole course in order, five on proportions, six on crosstabs, five on relationships between variables, eight on single and multiple regression, five on one-way ANOVA, six on two-factor ANOVA, eight on repeated measures and ten on research basics. Every number in it has been recomputed.","theme":0,"icon":"📝","group":"paper","questions":[{"n":1,"q":"For small samples, the \"plus four\" method is often used for a confidence interval for the difference between two population proportions. What does it do?","o":["It adds two successes and two failures to each sample, then applies the usual z interval to the adjusted counts.","It adds one success and one failure to each sample, then applies the usual z interval to the adjusted counts.","It adds four extra observations to the smaller sample only and replaces z* by a t* value.","It removes the four most extreme observations from each sample before applying the usual z interval."],"a":1,"pct":0,"typ":"","theme":2,"spot":"","plain":"","work":"Two-sample plus-four: add 1 success and 1 failure to each sample (4 pseudo-observations in total), then compute the usual interval from the adjusted counts. The version that adds 2 successes and 2 failures is the one-sample plus-four interval. The adjustment improves coverage for small samples without changing the z* critical value.","wrongs":["","","",""],"trap":"","src":"End-of-term mock, question 1  ·  Proportions"},{"n":2,"q":"School psychologist Petra screens a simple random sample of 200 primary-school children and finds that 24 of them show signs of dyslexia. She wants to report a 95% confidence interval for the population proportion. What is the margin of error?","o":["0.045","0.038","0.069","0.023"],"a":0,"pct":0,"typ":"","theme":2,"spot":"","plain":"","work":"p̂ = 24/200 = 0.12. SE = √(0.12 × 0.88 / 200) = 0.0230. Margin of error = z* × SE = 1.96 × 0.0230 = 0.045.\n0.023 is only the standard error (z* forgotten); 0.069 uses the planning value p* = 0.5 instead of the sample proportion; 0.038 uses z* = 1.645, which is for 90% confidence.","wrongs":["","","",""],"trap":"","src":"End-of-term mock, question 2  ·  Proportions"},{"n":3,"q":"A study advisor welcomes 200 new first-year students and learns that 29 of them have a diagnosed learning disability. She wants to test H0: p = 0.10 against Ha: p &gt; 0.10. What is the value of the z statistic?","o":["2.12","1.50","0.045","1.81"],"a":0,"pct":0,"typ":"","theme":2,"spot":"","plain":"","work":"p̂ = 29/200 = 0.145. In a test the standard error uses the hypothesised value p0: SE = √(0.10 × 0.90 / 200) = 0.0212. z = (0.145 - 0.10) / 0.0212 = 2.12 (one-sided p ≈ .017).\n1.81 comes from plugging p̂ into the standard error, which is the recipe for a confidence interval, not for the test. 0.045 is only the numerator.","wrongs":["","","",""],"trap":"","src":"End-of-term mock, question 3  ·  Proportions"},{"n":4,"q":"Researchers compare the proportion of pupils who read for pleasure every week at two schools. The 95% confidence interval for the difference in population proportions (School B minus School A) is (0.04, 0.22). The head teacher of School A, which has more pupils with learning difficulties, claims the two schools do not differ. What is the best conclusion?","o":["The claim is supported: a lower limit as small as 0.04 means the difference is essentially zero.","No conclusion is possible without the p-value, because a confidence interval cannot be used for testing.","The claim is contradicted: 0 lies outside the interval, so the schools differ significantly at the 5% level.","The claim is neither supported nor contradicted: the interval is wide, so the data are inconclusive."],"a":2,"pct":0,"typ":"","theme":2,"spot":"","plain":"","work":"A 95% confidence interval for a difference matches a two-sided test at α = .05 of H0: pB - pA = 0. Zero is not inside (0.04, 0.22), so H0 is rejected. Whether a difference of 0.04 to 0.22 is practically important is a separate question, but \"no difference\" is not compatible with these data.","wrongs":["","","",""],"trap":"","src":"End-of-term mock, question 4  ·  Proportions"},{"n":5,"q":"In a simple random sample at a large university, 20 of 50 girls and 21 of 45 boys said they plan to continue to a master's degree. Girls are group 1 and boys are group 2. Which interval is the 95% confidence interval for p1 - p2?","o":["(-0.266, 0.133)","(-0.234, 0.101)","(-0.133, 0.266)","(-0.199, 0.199)"],"a":0,"pct":0,"typ":"","theme":2,"spot":"","plain":"","work":"p̂1 = 0.400, p̂2 = 0.467, so the difference is -0.067. SE = √(0.4 × 0.6/50 + 0.467 × 0.533/45) = 0.1016. Margin = 1.96 × 0.1016 = 0.199. Interval: -0.067 ± 0.199 = (-0.266, 0.133).\n(-0.133, 0.266) is the interval for p2 - p1; (-0.234, 0.101) uses z* = 1.645 (90%); (-0.199, 0.199) forgets to centre on the observed difference. Because the correct interval contains 0, there is no significant difference at the 5% level.","wrongs":["","","",""],"trap":"","src":"End-of-term mock, question 5  ·  Proportions"},{"n":6,"q":"Using the table above, what is the expected count for the cell Women - Negative under independence?","o":["40.53","45.00","38.50","36.47"],"a":0,"pct":0,"typ":"","theme":3,"spot":"","plain":"","work":"Expected = row total × column total / n = 100 × 77 / 190 = 40.53. 45.00 is the observed count; 36.47 is the expected count for Men - Negative (90 × 77 / 190); 38.50 would be half the column total, which ignores that the groups differ in size.\nFor the whole table χ² = 8.15 with df = (2 - 1)(3 - 1) = 2, p ≈ .017.","wrongs":["","","",""],"trap":"","top":1,"src":"End-of-term mock, question 6  ·  Crosstabs","raw":"<table class=\"qt\"><caption>Main tone of gossip, by gender (counts)</caption><thead><tr><th></th><th>Positive</th><th>Negative</th><th>Neutral</th><th>Total</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Women</td><td>30</td><td>45</td><td>25</td><td>100</td></tr><tr><td>Men</td><td>18</td><td>32</td><td>40</td><td>90</td></tr><tr><td>Total</td><td>48</td><td>77</td><td>65</td><td>190</td></tr></tbody></table>"},{"n":7,"q":"In a study of 167 young people, a two-sample z test compares the proportion with high depressive symptoms between students with low and high school performance. It gives z = 2.5 (two-sided p = .012). The same data are put in a 2 × 2 table and analysed with a chi-square test of independence. What do you expect?","o":["χ² = 2.50 with 1 df and the same p-value, .012","χ² = 5.00 with 2 df and a p-value of .082","χ² = 6.25 with 1 df and a one-sided p-value of .006","χ² = 6.25 with 1 df and the same two-sided p-value, .012"],"a":3,"pct":0,"typ":"","theme":3,"spot":"","plain":"","work":"For a 2 × 2 table the chi-square statistic is the square of the two-sided z statistic: χ² = z² = 2.5² = 6.25, with df = (2 - 1)(2 - 1) = 1. Both tests are two-sided in effect and give the same p-value.","wrongs":["","","",""],"trap":"","src":"End-of-term mock, question 7  ·  Crosstabs"},{"n":8,"q":"Which condition must be met before the chi-square test for a two-way table can be trusted?","o":["The expected counts must be large enough (most at least 5, none below 1).","Both categorical variables must be approximately normally distributed in the population.","The row totals must be equal to the column totals, so that the table is balanced.","The observed count in every cell must be at least 5 before the test is run."],"a":0,"pct":0,"typ":"","theme":3,"spot":"","plain":"","work":"The chi-square approximation depends on the expected counts (row total × column total / n), not on the observed counts. Categorical variables have no normal distribution to check, and row and column totals are generally unequal.","wrongs":["","","",""],"trap":"","src":"End-of-term mock, question 8  ·  Crosstabs"},{"n":9,"q":"Using the table above, what is the relative risk of being a boy in the 8-10 group compared with the 11-13 group?","o":["0.15","0.67","0.30","1.50"],"a":1,"pct":0,"typ":"","theme":3,"spot":"","plain":"","work":"P(boy | 8-10) = 21/70 = 0.30. P(boy | 11-13) = 36/80 = 0.45. RR = 0.30 / 0.45 = 0.67.\n1.50 turns the ratio upside down (11-13 relative to 8-10); 0.15 is the difference in proportions; 0.30 is just one of the two risks.","wrongs":["","","",""],"trap":"","top":1,"src":"End-of-term mock, question 9  ·  Crosstabs","raw":"<table class=\"qt\"><caption>Children by age group and sex (counts)</caption><thead><tr><th></th><th>Boys</th><th>Girls</th><th>Total</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Age 8-10</td><td>21</td><td>49</td><td>70</td></tr><tr><td>Age 11-13</td><td>36</td><td>44</td><td>80</td></tr></tbody></table>"},{"n":10,"q":"A researcher classified 190 respondents by the main tone of their gossip. Which pair of hypotheses belongs to the chi-square test of independence for this table?","o":["H0: gossip tone and gender are independent. Ha: gossip tone and gender are associated.","H0: gossip tone and gender are independent. Ha: women gossip more positively than men.","H0: positive, negative and neutral gossip are equally common overall. Ha: they are not.","H0: women and men gossip equally often. Ha: women and men differ in how often they gossip."],"a":0,"pct":0,"typ":"","theme":3,"spot":"","plain":"","work":"The test of independence asks whether the distribution over tone is the same for women and men. Its alternative is simply \"associated\"; it has no direction, so a one-sided claim like \"women more positive\" is not what is tested. Gossip frequency was not measured, and \"equally common overall\" is the hypothesis of a goodness-of-fit test on one variable.","wrongs":["","","",""],"trap":"","top":1,"src":"End-of-term mock, question 10  ·  Crosstabs","raw":"<table class=\"qt\"><caption>Main tone of gossip, by gender (counts)</caption><thead><tr><th></th><th>Positive</th><th>Negative</th><th>Neutral</th><th>Total</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Women</td><td>30</td><td>45</td><td>25</td><td>100</td></tr><tr><td>Men</td><td>18</td><td>32</td><td>40</td><td>90</td></tr><tr><td>Total</td><td>48</td><td>77</td><td>65</td><td>190</td></tr></tbody></table>"},{"n":11,"q":"Developmental psychologist Jasper rolls a die 64 times. Faces 1 to 6 come up 8, 16, 13, 9, 11 and 7 times. Before he runs a goodness-of-fit test of H0: the die is fair, what is the value of the chi-square statistic?","o":["57.33","0.90","5.38","6.00"],"a":2,"pct":0,"typ":"","theme":3,"spot":"","plain":"","work":"Expected count per face = 64/6 = 10.67. Σ(O - E)²/E = (7.11 + 28.44 + 5.44 + 2.78 + 0.11 + 13.44)/10.67 = 57.33/10.67 = 5.375. With df = 6 - 1 = 5 the 5% critical value is 11.07, so H0 is not rejected (p ≈ .37).\n6.00 rounds the expected count to 10; 57.33 forgets to divide by E; 0.90 divides by n.","wrongs":["","","",""],"trap":"","src":"End-of-term mock, question 11  ·  Crosstabs"},{"n":12,"q":"A therapist records, for a sample of patients, the number of sessions attended (SD = 5.0) and the reduction in symptoms (SD = 10.0). The correlation is r = 0.45. What is the slope of the least-squares line for predicting symptom reduction from sessions?","o":["0.90","0.45","2.00","0.225"],"a":0,"pct":0,"typ":"","theme":4,"spot":"","plain":"","work":"b1 = r × sy/sx = 0.45 × 10.0/5.0 = 0.90. 0.225 uses sx/sy (the slope for predicting sessions from symptoms); 0.45 forgets the SDs; 2.00 is sy/sx without r.","wrongs":["","","",""],"trap":"","src":"End-of-term mock, question 12  ·  Relationships between variables"},{"n":13,"q":"Among children aged 6 to 12, the correlation between IQ score and dance performance is r = 0.72 (older children score higher on both). What correlation do you expect if only the 10-year-olds are analysed?","o":["Lower than 0.72, because age no longer drives both variables and the range is restricted.","Exactly 0.72, since a correlation does not depend on which subgroup of children is analysed.","Negative, since the effect of age on both variables reverses once age is held constant.","Higher than 0.72, since a more homogeneous group gives more precise measurements of both variables."],"a":0,"pct":0,"typ":"","theme":4,"spot":"","plain":"","work":"Two things push r down: age acts as a common cause that inflates the association across ages, and restricting the range of the variables (one age group) reduces r. A correlation is not a fixed property of two variables; it depends on the population and range sampled.","wrongs":["","","",""],"trap":"","src":"End-of-term mock, question 13  ·  Relationships between variables"},{"n":14,"q":"In a sample, the correlation between daily screen time and sleep quality is r = -0.60. Which statement is correct?","o":["Screen time causes poorer sleep in about 60% of the participants.","Sleep quality falls by 60% for every additional hour of screen time.","The relationship is weaker than a correlation of +0.40, because the sign is negative.","Screen time accounts for about 36% of the variation in sleep quality in this sample."],"a":3,"pct":0,"typ":"","theme":4,"spot":"","plain":"","work":"r² = (-0.60)² = 0.36, so about 36% of the variance in sleep quality is accounted for by the linear relationship with screen time. The sign gives only the direction; strength is |r|, so -0.60 is stronger than +0.40. r says nothing about percentages of participants or about causation.","wrongs":["","","",""],"trap":"","src":"End-of-term mock, question 14  ·  Relationships between variables"},{"n":15,"q":"In a study of 120 pupils, the correlation between weekly hours of outdoor play and an attention score is r = 0.34. The researcher recodes outdoor play from hours to minutes. What happens to the correlation?","o":["It decreases, because the spread of the x variable has become larger.","It becomes 0.34/60, because one unit of x is now 60 times smaller.","It increases, because minutes measure outdoor play more precisely.","It stays exactly 0.34."],"a":3,"pct":0,"typ":"","theme":4,"spot":"","plain":"","work":"r is unchanged by linear changes of units (adding a constant or multiplying by a positive constant) of either variable. The slope would change (it becomes 60 times smaller), but r does not.","wrongs":["","","",""],"trap":"","src":"End-of-term mock, question 15  ·  Relationships between variables"},{"n":16,"q":"Fatima has two data sets about the same 1,000 people. In data set A she correlates each person's IQ with their income: r = 0.27. In data set B she first sorts people into IQ categories and correlates each category's average IQ with its average income: r = 0.58. Why is the correlation in B so much higher?","o":["Averaging within categories removes individual-level scatter, so the group means line up more tightly than individuals do.","Data set B has far fewer observations, and correlations always become larger when the number of observations is smaller.","The relationship is causal at the group level but not at the individual level, so the correlation is larger in B.","Income and IQ are expressed on different scales in B, and changing the scale of measurement changes the value of r."],"a":0,"pct":0,"typ":"","theme":4,"spot":"","plain":"","work":"This is the classic ecological correlation: correlations between group averages are usually much higher than correlations between individuals because within-group variation (noise) has been averaged away. It does not mean the individual-level relationship is strong. r does not depend on the measurement scale, and small n makes r more variable, not systematically larger.","wrongs":["","","",""],"trap":"","src":"End-of-term mock, question 16  ·  Relationships between variables"},{"n":17,"q":"A multiple regression predicts sleep quality from caffeine intake (mg per day, b = -0.004) and anxiety (score from 0 to 80, b = -0.12). A student concludes that anxiety is the stronger predictor because |-0.12| &gt; |-0.004|. What is wrong with this reasoning?","o":["The coefficients can only be compared after both predictors have been log-transformed to a common scale.","Nothing is wrong: in multiple regression the larger absolute b always identifies the stronger predictor, whatever the units.","Unstandardized coefficients depend on measurement units; predictors on different scales are compared via standardized coefficients (β).","Negative coefficients cannot be compared with each other; only positive coefficients can be compared."],"a":2,"pct":0,"typ":"","theme":5,"spot":"","plain":"","work":"b is the change in y per one unit of x, so its size depends on how x is scaled (mg versus a 0-80 score). Standardized coefficients (β) express every predictor in SD units and are the appropriate way to compare predictors measured on different scales.","wrongs":["","","",""],"trap":"","src":"End-of-term mock, question 17  ·  Single &amp; multiple regression"},{"n":18,"q":"A psychologist inspects the residual plot of a regression (residuals against predicted values). The residuals are tightly clustered around zero for low predicted values and fan out increasingly for higher predicted values. Which assumption is violated, and what is the consequence?","o":["Linearity of the relationship; the fitted line systematically over- and under-predicts, so predictions are biased.","Independence of the residuals; the slope estimate is biased towards zero in every sample.","Normality of the residuals; the least-squares line can no longer be fitted to the data at all.","Constant variance of the residuals (homoscedasticity); standard errors and p-values become unreliable."],"a":3,"pct":0,"typ":"","theme":5,"spot":"","plain":"","work":"A fan or funnel shape means that the spread of the residuals changes with the predicted value: heteroscedasticity. The least-squares line can still be fitted, but the usual standard errors, confidence intervals and tests assume constant variance and become unreliable. A curved pattern would point to non-linearity.","wrongs":["","","",""],"trap":"","src":"End-of-term mock, question 18  ·  Single &amp; multiple regression"},{"n":19,"q":"Researchers randomly selected 15 cars and recorded each car's weight and mileage. The scatterplot showed a linear model is appropriate. Based on the SPSS output above, which statement is FALSE?","o":["A car that is 1,000 lbs heavier is predicted to get about 5.2 mpg less.","The correlation is positive, r = +0.662, as the Model Summary shows R = .662.","About 44% of the variation in mileage is explained by the weight of the car.","The slope test, t(13) = -3.18, gives the same p-value as the F test of the model."],"a":1,"pct":0,"typ":"","theme":5,"spot":"","plain":"","work":"SPSS prints R as a positive number (the multiple correlation); in simple regression the sign of r is the sign of the slope, which here is negative (Beta = -.662), so r = -0.662. The other statements are true: R² = .438; with one predictor F = t² (-3.18² ≈ 10.14), so the p-values match; and the slope -5.20 means 5.2 mpg less per 1,000 lbs.","wrongs":["","","",""],"trap":"","top":1,"src":"End-of-term mock, question 19  ·  Single &amp; multiple regression","raw":"<table class=\"qt\"><caption>Model summary</caption><thead><tr><th>R</th><th>R²</th><th>n</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>.662</td><td>.438</td><td>15</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"qt\"><caption>ANOVA</caption><thead><tr><th></th><th>df</th><th>F</th><th>Sig.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Regression</td><td>1</td><td>10.14</td><td>.007</td></tr><tr><td>Residual</td><td>13</td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"qt\"><caption>Coefficients (weight in 1,000 lbs; mileage in mpg)</caption><thead><tr><th></th><th>B</th><th>SE</th><th>Beta</th><th>t</th><th>Sig.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(Constant)</td><td>38.40</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Weight</td><td>-5.20</td><td>1.633</td><td>-.662</td><td>-3.18</td><td>.007</td></tr></tbody></table>"},{"n":20,"q":"In a multiple regression with 3 explanatory variables, SST = 1008, the model mean square MSM = 76 and the F statistic is 2.533. How many observations were used?","o":["26","30","27","29"],"a":1,"pct":0,"typ":"","theme":5,"spot":"","plain":"","work":"SSM = MSM × df_model = 76 × 3 = 228, so SSE = 1008 - 228 = 780. MSE = MSM/F = 76/2.533 = 30.0, so df_error = 780/30 = 26. With df_error = n - p - 1: n = 26 + 3 + 1 = 30. (Check: total df = 29 = n - 1.)","wrongs":["","","",""],"trap":"","src":"End-of-term mock, question 20  ·  Single &amp; multiple regression"},{"n":21,"q":"A simple regression predicts well-being from the number of therapy sessions: ŷ = 42 + 0.88x. A patient attended 25 sessions and has an observed well-being score of 58. What is this patient's residual?","o":["64.0","+6.0","16.0","-6.0"],"a":3,"pct":0,"typ":"","theme":5,"spot":"","plain":"","work":"Predicted: ŷ = 42 + 0.88 × 25 = 64.0. Residual = observed - predicted = 58 - 64 = -6.0. The patient scored 6 points below what the line predicts. +6.0 reverses the sign; 64.0 is the prediction itself.","wrongs":["","","",""],"trap":"","src":"End-of-term mock, question 21  ·  Single &amp; multiple regression"},{"n":22,"q":"A multiple regression predicts exam score from study hours and hours of sleep: ŷ = 31.0 + 2.4 × (study hours) + 1.1 × (sleep hours). How should the coefficient 2.4 be interpreted?","o":["Study hours explain 2.4% of the variance in exam scores once sleep has been taken into account.","One extra study hour raises a student's exam score by 2.4 points, whatever their amount of sleep.","Students who study 2.4 more hours are predicted to score exactly one point higher on the exam.","Among students with equal sleep, one extra study hour goes with a 2.4-point higher predicted exam score."],"a":3,"pct":0,"typ":"","theme":5,"spot":"","plain":"","work":"A coefficient in multiple regression is the predicted difference in y per unit of x with the other explanatory variables held constant. It describes association (predictions), not necessarily a causal effect, it is a slope rather than a number of hours per point, and it is not a percentage of explained variance.","wrongs":["","","",""],"trap":"","src":"End-of-term mock, question 22  ·  Single &amp; multiple regression"},{"n":23,"q":"A multiple regression with 4 explanatory variables is fitted on data from 60 participants. What are the degrees of freedom of the F test for the whole model?","o":["4 and 56","5 and 54","4 and 55","4 and 59"],"a":2,"pct":0,"typ":"","theme":5,"spot":"","plain":"","work":"df_model = p = 4. df_error = n - p - 1 = 60 - 4 - 1 = 55. So F(4, 55). (4, 59) uses the total df; (4, 56) forgets the intercept.","wrongs":["","","",""],"trap":"","src":"End-of-term mock, question 23  ·  Single &amp; multiple regression"},{"n":24,"q":"For a given value of x, a researcher computes both a 95% confidence interval for the mean response and a 95% prediction interval for one new observation. How do the two intervals compare?","o":["The confidence interval is wider, because it is based on an estimate of the mean rather than on a single observation.","Which one is wider depends on whether the slope of the regression line is positive or negative.","The confidence interval is narrower, because the prediction interval must also cover individual variation around the mean.","They are identical, because both intervals are centred on the same predicted value and use the same critical value."],"a":2,"pct":0,"typ":"","theme":5,"spot":"","plain":"","work":"Both are centred on ŷ, but the prediction interval adds the individual-level variability (residual SD) on top of the uncertainty in the estimated mean. It is therefore always wider; with a very large sample the confidence interval shrinks towards zero width while the prediction interval does not.","wrongs":["","","",""],"trap":"","src":"End-of-term mock, question 24  ·  Single &amp; multiple regression"},{"n":25,"q":"In a one-way ANOVA the mean square error is MSE = 52.9. What is the pooled estimate of the common standard deviation, rounded to two decimals?","o":["7.27","3.64","26.45","52.90"],"a":0,"pct":0,"typ":"","theme":6,"spot":"","plain":"","work":"The pooled SD is s_p = √MSE = √52.9 = 7.27. 52.90 is the pooled variance.","wrongs":["","","",""],"trap":"","src":"End-of-term mock, question 25  ·  1-factor ANOVA"},{"n":26,"q":"A one-way ANOVA compares three groups with sample standard deviations of 4.1, 6.0 and 9.4. A common rule of thumb says the equal-variance assumption is acceptable when the largest SD is less than twice the smallest. What do you conclude?","o":["The assumption concerns the sample means, not the SDs, so nothing needs to be checked.","The assumption is met, because the largest SD (9.4) is less than twice the middle SD (6.0).","The ratio 9.4/4.1 ≈ 2.3 exceeds 2, so the equal-variance assumption is doubtful.","The assumption is violated, so ANOVA cannot be used under any circumstances."],"a":2,"pct":0,"typ":"","theme":6,"spot":"","plain":"","work":"The rule compares the largest with the smallest SD: 9.4/4.1 = 2.29 &gt; 2, so the assumption of a common σ is questionable (especially with unequal group sizes). That calls for caution or a robust alternative, not an automatic ban on ANOVA.","wrongs":["","","",""],"trap":"","src":"End-of-term mock, question 26  ·  1-factor ANOVA"},{"n":27,"q":"Four teaching methods are compared with 15 students per method. The sum of squares between groups is SSG = 252 and the sum of squares within groups is SSE = 1680. What are the F statistic and its degrees of freedom?","o":["F(3, 59) = 2.95","F(3, 56) = 0.15","F(3, 56) = 2.80","F(4, 56) = 2.10"],"a":2,"pct":0,"typ":"","theme":6,"spot":"","plain":"","work":"MSG = SSG/(I - 1) = 252/3 = 84. MSE = SSE/(N - I) = 1680/56 = 30. F = 84/30 = 2.80 with df (3, 56) (p ≈ .048). F(3, 59) uses N - 1 for the error df; F(4, 56) uses I instead of I - 1; 0.15 is SSG/SSE, with no division by df.","wrongs":["","","",""],"trap":"","src":"End-of-term mock, question 27  ·  1-factor ANOVA"},{"n":28,"q":"Mark compares the exam scores of 4 teaching methods (15 students each) by running every pairwise t test, each at α = .05, without correction. If all null hypotheses are true and the tests are treated as independent, what is the family-wise error rate (the probability of at least one Type I error)?","o":["0.05","0.26","0.30","0.20"],"a":1,"pct":0,"typ":"","theme":6,"spot":"","plain":"","work":"With 4 groups there are 4 × 3/2 = 6 pairwise comparisons. P(at least one false positive) = 1 - (1 - .05)⁶ = 1 - 0.735 = 0.265. 0.20 (4 × .05) counts the wrong number of tests; 0.30 (6 × .05) is the Bonferroni upper bound, not the exact value. A one-way ANOVA (or Bonferroni-corrected comparisons) keeps the overall error rate at .05.","wrongs":["","","",""],"trap":"","src":"End-of-term mock, question 28  ·  1-factor ANOVA"},{"n":29,"q":"Forty participants are randomly divided into 5 groups of 8. Each group follows a different training programme for two months, after which the improvement in endurance is recorded. A one-way ANOVA gives p = 0.035, with α = 0.05. What is the appropriate conclusion?","o":["All five programmes differ from each other in their mean improvement after two months.","At least one programme differs in mean improvement; ANOVA does not say which.","The probability that the null hypothesis is true is 3.5%, given these data.","The programme with the highest sample mean is the most effective programme overall."],"a":1,"pct":0,"typ":"","theme":6,"spot":"","plain":"","work":"H0: all five population means are equal; Ha: not all are equal. p &lt; α rejects H0: at least one mean differs (F has df 4 and 35). Locating the differences requires follow-up comparisons with a multiplicity correction. A p-value is not the probability that H0 is true.","wrongs":["","","",""],"trap":"","src":"End-of-term mock, question 29  ·  1-factor ANOVA"},{"n":30,"q":"The plot above shows the cell means of a 2 × 3 design. Assume differences of this size are large compared with the error variance. Which pattern does it show?","o":["A main effect of B only; no main effect of A and no interaction.","A main effect of A and an A × B interaction, but no main effect of B.","A main effect of A and a main effect of B, but no interaction.","An A × B interaction only; neither main effect."],"a":2,"pct":0,"typ":"","theme":7,"spot":"","plain":"","work":"The lines are parallel, so the effect of B is the same at both levels of A: no interaction. The lines are separated (A2 is 2 points above A1 everywhere): main effect of A. Both lines rise from B1 to B3: main effect of B.","wrongs":["","","",""],"trap":"","top":1,"src":"End-of-term mock, question 30  ·  2-factor ANOVA","raw":"<svg viewBox=\"0 0 460 250\" role=\"img\" width=\"100%\" aria-label=\"Cell means for a two by three design\" style=\"max-width:460px;font-family:Instrument Sans,system-ui,sans-serif\"><line x1=\"52.0\" y1=\"208.0\" x2=\"412.0\" y2=\"208.0\" stroke=\"#e4e0d6\"/><text x=\"44.0\" y=\"212.0\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#5d6472\">0</text><line x1=\"52.0\" y1=\"170.0\" x2=\"412.0\" y2=\"170.0\" stroke=\"#e4e0d6\"/><text x=\"44.0\" y=\"174.0\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#5d6472\">2</text><line x1=\"52.0\" y1=\"132.0\" x2=\"412.0\" y2=\"132.0\" stroke=\"#e4e0d6\"/><text x=\"44.0\" y=\"136.0\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#5d6472\">4</text><line x1=\"52.0\" y1=\"94.0\" x2=\"412.0\" y2=\"94.0\" stroke=\"#e4e0d6\"/><text x=\"44.0\" y=\"98.0\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#5d6472\">6</text><line x1=\"52.0\" y1=\"56.0\" x2=\"412.0\" y2=\"56.0\" stroke=\"#e4e0d6\"/><text x=\"44.0\" y=\"60.0\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#5d6472\">8</text><line x1=\"52.0\" y1=\"18.0\" x2=\"412.0\" y2=\"18.0\" stroke=\"#e4e0d6\"/><text x=\"44.0\" y=\"22.0\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#5d6472\">10</text><line x1=\"52.0\" y1=\"208.0\" x2=\"412.0\" y2=\"208.0\" stroke=\"#1d2433\"/><line x1=\"52.0\" y1=\"18.0\" x2=\"52.0\" y2=\"208.0\" stroke=\"#1d2433\"/><text x=\"52.0\" y=\"228.0\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#1d2433\">B1</text><text x=\"232.0\" y=\"228.0\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#1d2433\">B2</text><text x=\"412.0\" y=\"228.0\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#1d2433\">B3</text><text x=\"232.0\" y=\"242.0\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#5d6472\">Factor B</text><text transform=\"translate(14,113.0) rotate(-90)\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#5d6472\">Mean score</text><polyline points=\"52.0,151.0 232.0,113.0 412.0,75.0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#4a6aa8\" stroke-width=\"2.2\"/><circle cx=\"52.0\" cy=\"151.0\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#4a6aa8\"/><circle cx=\"232.0\" cy=\"113.0\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#4a6aa8\"/><circle cx=\"412.0\" cy=\"75.0\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#4a6aa8\"/><text x=\"420.0\" y=\"79.0\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#4a6aa8\">A1</text><polyline points=\"52.0,113.0 232.0,75.0 412.0,37.0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#a8474a\" stroke-width=\"2.2\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\"/><rect x=\"47.5\" y=\"108.5\" width=\"9\" height=\"9\" fill=\"#a8474a\"/><rect x=\"227.5\" y=\"70.5\" width=\"9\" height=\"9\" fill=\"#a8474a\"/><rect x=\"407.5\" y=\"32.5\" width=\"9\" height=\"9\" fill=\"#a8474a\"/><text x=\"420.0\" y=\"41.0\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#a8474a\">A2</text></svg>"},{"n":31,"q":"Emma runs a 2 (feedback: positive vs negative) × 3 (reward: none, small, large) between-subjects ANOVA with N = 60 (10 per cell). Main effect of feedback: F(1, 54) = 12.4, p = .001. Main effect of reward: F(2, 54) = 1.8, p = .18. Interaction: F(2, 54) = 18.9, p &lt; .001. In her plot, feedback lowers performance when there is no reward but raises it when the reward is large. How should she interpret this?","o":["The interaction shows that feedback and reward are correlated with each other, so one factor should be dropped from the design.","The interaction qualifies the main effects: the effect of feedback depends on reward level, so she should examine simple effects.","Since the main effect of feedback is significant, the interaction can safely be ignored when interpreting the results.","Feedback matters (significant main effect) and reward does not (p = .18), so she can conclude that reward has no influence on performance."],"a":1,"pct":0,"typ":"","theme":7,"spot":"","plain":"","work":"With opposite feedback effects at different reward levels, the effects partly cancel in the marginal means, so the main effect of reward looks absent even though reward clearly matters. A significant interaction qualifies the main effects: describe how the effect of one factor changes across levels of the other (simple effects / plot). An interaction is not a correlation between factors; in a balanced design they are independent by construction.","wrongs":["","","",""],"trap":"","src":"End-of-term mock, question 31  ·  2-factor ANOVA"},{"n":32,"q":"Jasmin runs a 2 × 3 between-subjects ANOVA with 25 participants in each cell (N = 150). Main effect A: SS = 418, df = 1. Main effect B: SS = 840, df = 2. Interaction: SS = 360, df = 2. Error: SS = 21,600, df = 144. What is the F statistic for main effect B?","o":["2.80","1.20","5.60","0.04"],"a":0,"pct":0,"typ":"","theme":7,"spot":"","plain":"","work":"MSB = 840/2 = 420. MSE = 21,600/144 = 150. FB = 420/150 = 2.80, with df (2, 144) (p ≈ .064, not significant at .05). 5.60 divides SSB by MSE without dividing by its df; 1.20 is the F for the interaction (180/150); 0.04 is SSB/SSE.","wrongs":["","","",""],"trap":"","src":"End-of-term mock, question 32  ·  2-factor ANOVA"},{"n":33,"q":"For the data above, what is the sum of squares error (SSE)?","o":["56","40","120","3.33"],"a":1,"pct":0,"typ":"","theme":7,"spot":"","plain":"","work":"SSE is the sum of squared deviations of the scores from their own cell mean. Cell means: 4, 8, 6, 10. Cell sums of squares: 6, 14, 14, 6, so SSE = 40 (df = 16 - 4 = 12, MSE = 3.33). 120 is SST; 56 is SST - SSB (it still contains SSA, the interaction and error variance).","wrongs":["","","",""],"trap":"","top":1,"src":"End-of-term mock, question 33  ·  2-factor ANOVA","raw":"<table class=\"qt\"><caption>Scores in a 2 × 2 between-subjects design (4 participants per cell)</caption><thead><tr><th>A1 B1</th><th>A1 B2</th><th>A2 B1</th><th>A2 B2</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>2</td><td>6</td><td>4</td><td>8</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>7</td><td>5</td><td>10</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>8</td><td>6</td><td>11</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>11</td><td>9</td><td>11</td></tr></tbody></table>"},{"n":34,"q":"The plot above shows the cell means of another 2 × 3 design. Which statement describes it best?","o":["An A × B interaction; the marginal means of A and of B are all equal, so no main effects.","A main effect of B only, because the lines have a steep slope across levels of B.","A main effect of A only, because the two lines are far apart at B1 and B3.","No effects at all, because the lines cross and therefore cancel each other out."],"a":0,"pct":0,"typ":"","theme":7,"spot":"","plain":"","work":"The lines are not parallel (they cross), so the effect of B depends on the level of A: a clear interaction. The marginal mean of A1 and A2 is 5 for both, and the marginal mean of every B level is 5, so no main effects. Crossing lines do not mean \"no effect\"; they are the strongest visual sign of an interaction.","wrongs":["","","",""],"trap":"","top":1,"src":"End-of-term mock, question 34  ·  2-factor ANOVA","raw":"<svg viewBox=\"0 0 460 250\" role=\"img\" width=\"100%\" aria-label=\"Cell means for a two by three design\" style=\"max-width:460px;font-family:Instrument Sans,system-ui,sans-serif\"><line x1=\"52.0\" y1=\"208.0\" x2=\"412.0\" y2=\"208.0\" stroke=\"#e4e0d6\"/><text x=\"44.0\" y=\"212.0\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#5d6472\">0</text><line x1=\"52.0\" y1=\"170.0\" x2=\"412.0\" y2=\"170.0\" stroke=\"#e4e0d6\"/><text x=\"44.0\" y=\"174.0\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#5d6472\">2</text><line x1=\"52.0\" y1=\"132.0\" x2=\"412.0\" y2=\"132.0\" stroke=\"#e4e0d6\"/><text x=\"44.0\" y=\"136.0\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#5d6472\">4</text><line x1=\"52.0\" y1=\"94.0\" x2=\"412.0\" y2=\"94.0\" stroke=\"#e4e0d6\"/><text x=\"44.0\" y=\"98.0\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#5d6472\">6</text><line x1=\"52.0\" y1=\"56.0\" x2=\"412.0\" y2=\"56.0\" stroke=\"#e4e0d6\"/><text x=\"44.0\" y=\"60.0\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#5d6472\">8</text><line x1=\"52.0\" y1=\"18.0\" x2=\"412.0\" y2=\"18.0\" stroke=\"#e4e0d6\"/><text x=\"44.0\" y=\"22.0\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#5d6472\">10</text><line x1=\"52.0\" y1=\"208.0\" x2=\"412.0\" y2=\"208.0\" stroke=\"#1d2433\"/><line x1=\"52.0\" y1=\"18.0\" x2=\"52.0\" y2=\"208.0\" stroke=\"#1d2433\"/><text x=\"52.0\" y=\"228.0\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#1d2433\">B1</text><text x=\"232.0\" y=\"228.0\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#1d2433\">B2</text><text x=\"412.0\" y=\"228.0\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#1d2433\">B3</text><text x=\"232.0\" y=\"242.0\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#5d6472\">Factor B</text><text transform=\"translate(14,113.0) rotate(-90)\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#5d6472\">Mean score</text><polyline points=\"52.0,151.0 232.0,113.0 412.0,75.0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#4a6aa8\" stroke-width=\"2.2\"/><circle cx=\"52.0\" cy=\"151.0\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#4a6aa8\"/><circle cx=\"232.0\" cy=\"113.0\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#4a6aa8\"/><circle cx=\"412.0\" cy=\"75.0\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#4a6aa8\"/><text x=\"420.0\" y=\"79.0\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#4a6aa8\">A1</text><polyline points=\"52.0,75.0 232.0,113.0 412.0,151.0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#a8474a\" stroke-width=\"2.2\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\"/><rect x=\"47.5\" y=\"70.5\" width=\"9\" height=\"9\" fill=\"#a8474a\"/><rect x=\"227.5\" y=\"108.5\" width=\"9\" height=\"9\" fill=\"#a8474a\"/><rect x=\"407.5\" y=\"146.5\" width=\"9\" height=\"9\" fill=\"#a8474a\"/><text x=\"420.0\" y=\"155.0\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#a8474a\">A2</text></svg>"},{"n":35,"q":"A study compares 3 levels of factor A and 3 levels of factor B, with 10 observations in each cell. What are the degrees of freedom of the F statistic that tests the A × B interaction?","o":["2 and 87","4 and 81","4 and 89","9 and 81"],"a":1,"pct":0,"typ":"","theme":7,"spot":"","plain":"","work":"dfinteraction = (a - 1)(b - 1) = 2 × 2 = 4. dferror = N - ab = 90 - 9 = 81 (equivalently 9 cells × (10 - 1)). So F(4, 81).","wrongs":["","","",""],"trap":"","src":"End-of-term mock, question 35  ·  2-factor ANOVA"},{"n":36,"q":"Twenty-four participants each complete a memory task in a quiet room and in a noisy room, once in the morning and once in the afternoon. The dependent variable is the memory score. Which analysis is appropriate?","o":["Mixed ANOVA (noise within subjects, time of day between subjects)","Two-way independent ANOVA (both factors between subjects, 2 × 2)","Two-way repeated measures ANOVA (both factors within subjects)","Paired-samples t test applied to all four conditions at once"],"a":2,"pct":0,"typ":"","theme":8,"spot":"","plain":"","work":"Two factors, each with two levels, and each participant is measured in all four cells: a 2 × 2 design with both factors repeated, i.e. a two-way repeated measures ANOVA. It would be mixed only if one of the factors varied between participant groups.","wrongs":["","","",""],"trap":"","src":"End-of-term mock, question 36  ·  Repeated measures ANOVA"},{"n":37,"q":"A nutritionist tests whether eating cheese before bed gives bad dreams. Each participant sleeps three nights: eating nothing, eating a plate of cheese, or drinking a glass of milk (another dairy product). The order of the three nights is counterbalanced across participants. Heart rate during dreaming is measured. Which analysis is appropriate?","o":["Two-way repeated measures ANOVA","One-way independent (between-subjects) ANOVA","Paired-samples t test","One-way repeated measures ANOVA"],"a":3,"pct":0,"typ":"","theme":8,"spot":"","plain":"","work":"There is one factor (bedtime food) with three levels, and every participant takes part in all three levels: a one-way repeated measures design. A paired t test only handles two conditions; a two-way design would need a second factor.","wrongs":["","","",""],"trap":"","src":"End-of-term mock, question 37  ·  Repeated measures ANOVA"},{"n":38,"q":"A psychologist measures 8 people at 4 points in time. SST = 450, the between-person sum of squares is 180, SSW (within persons) = 270 and SSM (model) = 120. What is SSR (residual)?","o":["60","330","270","150"],"a":3,"pct":0,"typ":"","theme":8,"spot":"","plain":"","work":"SSW = SSM + SSR, so SSR = 270 - 120 = 150. (Same result from SST - SSbetween persons - SSM = 450 - 180 - 120.) 330 is SST - SSM; 60 is 180 - 120; 270 is SSW itself. With k = 4 and n = 8: F = (120/3)/(150/21) = 5.6.","wrongs":["","","",""],"trap":"","src":"End-of-term mock, question 38  ·  Repeated measures ANOVA"},{"n":39,"q":"In a one-way repeated measures ANOVA with three conditions, Mauchly's test is significant (p = .01). What does this mean and what do you do?","o":["The three conditions differ significantly in their means; report the F test as it is.","The assumption of equal variances between participants is violated; switch to an independent ANOVA.","The assumption of sphericity is violated; use a corrected F test such as Greenhouse–Geisser.","The assumption of sphericity is met; continue with the uncorrected F test."],"a":2,"pct":0,"typ":"","theme":8,"spot":"","plain":"","work":"Mauchly's test tests H0: the variances of the differences between all pairs of conditions are equal (sphericity). A significant result means sphericity does not hold; the F test is then too liberal, so use a correction (Greenhouse–Geisser, or Huynh–Feldt) that reduces the degrees of freedom.","wrongs":["","","",""],"trap":"","src":"End-of-term mock, question 39  ·  Repeated measures ANOVA"},{"n":40,"q":"Twenty participants are tested in two conditions and analysed with a paired-samples t test: t(19) = 2.50. If the same data were analysed with a one-way repeated measures ANOVA, what would you expect?","o":["F(1, 19) = 6.25, with the same p-value","F(1, 20) = 1.58, with the same p-value","F(2, 38) = 6.25, with a smaller p-value","F(1, 19) = 2.50, with the same p-value"],"a":0,"pct":0,"typ":"","theme":8,"spot":"","plain":"","work":"With two conditions, the repeated measures F is the square of the paired t: F(1, n - 1) = t² = 2.50² = 6.25, with the same p-value. The paired t test is the within-subjects counterpart of the independent-samples t test, just as repeated measures ANOVA is the counterpart of independent one-way ANOVA.","wrongs":["","","",""],"trap":"","src":"End-of-term mock, question 40  ·  Repeated measures ANOVA"},{"n":41,"q":"In a one-way repeated measures ANOVA with n = 10 participants and k = 3 conditions, SSM = 84 and SSR = 108. What are the F statistic and its degrees of freedom?","o":["F(2, 27) = 10.50","F(2, 18) = 0.14","F(2, 18) = 7.00","F(2, 9) = 3.50"],"a":2,"pct":0,"typ":"","theme":8,"spot":"","plain":"","work":"dfM = k - 1 = 2, dfR = (k - 1)(n - 1) = 2 × 9 = 18. MSM = 84/2 = 42, MSR = 108/18 = 6, F = 42/6 = 7.00. F(2, 27) uses the between-subjects error df (N - k); F(2, 9) uses n - 1; 0.14 is the ratio upside down.","wrongs":["","","",""],"trap":"","src":"End-of-term mock, question 41  ·  Repeated measures ANOVA"},{"n":42,"q":"In a within-subjects study with 30 patients and three medication conditions, a researcher finds no significant effect, F(2, 58) = 2.1, p = .13. She sees a clear pattern in the means, and a power analysis gives power = .42 for the effect size she expected. What is the best conclusion?","o":["The medication works, because the pattern in the means is clear even though p is above .05.","The medication has no effect, because the p-value is larger than .05 and the sample size was reasonable.","A Type I error occurred, because a clear pattern in the means turned out not to be significant.","Inconclusive: with power .42 a real effect could easily be missed (Type II error)."],"a":3,"pct":0,"typ":"","theme":8,"spot":"","plain":"","work":"A power of .42 means that even if the effect exists, the study would detect it less than half of the time. Failing to reject H0 is not proof of H0. A Type I error is a false positive; the clear-looking pattern is not an argument for an effect without a significant test. (Note the df: (k - 1)(n - 1) = 2 × 29 = 58, matching 30 participants in a repeated measures design.)","wrongs":["","","",""],"trap":"","src":"End-of-term mock, question 42  ·  Repeated measures ANOVA"},{"n":43,"q":"For the data above, what is the total sum of squares (SST)?","o":["72","102","30","104"],"a":3,"pct":0,"typ":"","theme":8,"spot":"","plain":"","work":"The grand mean is 96/12 = 8. SST = Σ(y - 8)² = 25 + 9 + 1 + 16 + 0 + 9 + 4 + 1 + 9 + 1 + 4 + 25 = 104. For reference: SSM = 72, SSbetween persons = 30, SSR = 2. 72 is only SSM; 102 is SSM + between-persons (forgetting SSR); 30 is the between-persons part.","wrongs":["","","",""],"trap":"","top":1,"src":"End-of-term mock, question 43  ·  Repeated measures ANOVA","raw":"<table class=\"qt\"><caption>Scores of 4 participants in 3 conditions</caption><thead><tr><th></th><th>Cond. 1</th><th>Cond. 2</th><th>Cond. 3</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>P1</td><td>3</td><td>5</td><td>9</td></tr><tr><td>P2</td><td>4</td><td>8</td><td>11</td></tr><tr><td>P3</td><td>6</td><td>9</td><td>11</td></tr><tr><td>P4</td><td>7</td><td>10</td><td>13</td></tr></tbody></table>"},{"n":44,"q":"A researcher manipulates feedback (positive/negative), task difficulty (easy/medium/hard) and group size (alone/with a partner) in a fully crossed design. How many conditions does this design have, and what does crossing the factors allow that three separate one-factor experiments do not?","o":["12 conditions; the only gain is that fewer participants are needed than in three separate studies","12 conditions; whether the effect of one factor depends on the level of another (interactions)","7 conditions; whether the effect of one factor depends on the level of another (interactions)","6 conditions; interactions between the first two factors only, as the third is a nuisance variable"],"a":1,"pct":0,"typ":"","theme":1,"spot":"","plain":"","work":"A fully crossed 2 × 3 × 2 design has 2 × 3 × 2 = 12 cells (not 2 + 3 + 2 = 7). Its key advantage is that interactions can be tested; separate one-factor experiments can show main effects only. Efficiency is a side benefit, not the only one.","wrongs":["","","",""],"trap":"","src":"End-of-term mock, question 44  ·  Research basics"},{"n":45,"q":"Jan tests whether background music improves reading speed. Participants in group 1 (music) are tested in the morning; participants in group 2 (silence) are tested in the afternoon. Group 1 reads faster. Which statement is correct?","o":["Each participant is tested once, so the study is within-subjects and confounds cannot occur.","The main problem is low statistical power, not a confound, since the groups were tested separately.","There is no problem: confounds only occur in correlational studies, never in experiments.","Time of day is confounded with music, so the difference cannot be attributed to music alone."],"a":3,"pct":0,"typ":"","theme":1,"spot":"","plain":"","work":"Music and time of day vary together, so they cannot be separated. Confounds can occur in experiments too whenever something other than the manipulated variable differs systematically between conditions. Testing each participant once makes this a between-subjects design.","wrongs":["","","",""],"trap":"","src":"End-of-term mock, question 45  ·  Research basics"},{"n":46,"q":"Which statement correctly distinguishes random sampling from random assignment?","o":["Random sampling supports internal validity (causal claims); random assignment supports external validity (generalising).","Random assignment supports internal validity (causal claims); random sampling supports external validity (generalising).","Random assignment is only needed in within-subjects designs; random sampling is only needed in surveys.","Both mainly support external validity, because both are about who ends up taking part in the study."],"a":1,"pct":0,"typ":"","theme":1,"spot":"","plain":"","work":"Random assignment makes the groups equivalent on average before treatment, so differences afterwards can be attributed to the manipulation (internal validity). Random sampling makes the sample representative of the population, which supports generalisation (external validity). Random assignment is mainly needed in between-subjects designs.","wrongs":["","","",""],"trap":"","src":"End-of-term mock, question 46  ·  Research basics"},{"n":47,"q":"Which of the following studies is a true experiment?","o":["Pupils who volunteered for a mindfulness course are compared on attention with pupils who did not volunteer.","Attention scores of pupils are correlated with the number of mindfulness sessions each pupil attended.","Pupils from one school get a mindfulness course, pupils from a neighbouring school do not; attention is measured in both schools.","Pupils are assigned by lottery to a mindfulness course or a waiting list; attention is measured in both groups afterwards."],"a":3,"pct":0,"typ":"","theme":1,"spot":"","plain":"","work":"A true experiment needs (1) manipulation of the independent variable by the researcher and (2) random assignment to conditions. In the other three, who receives the course (or how much of it) is determined by the pupils or the school, which opens the door to confounds such as motivation.","wrongs":["","","",""],"trap":"","src":"End-of-term mock, question 47  ·  Research basics"},{"n":48,"q":"In a within-subjects experiment, participants complete conditions A, B and C. What is the purpose of counterbalancing the order of the conditions across participants?","o":["To make sure that the participants in each condition are matched on age, IQ and other background variables.","To remove practice and fatigue effects from the data completely, so order no longer matters at all.","To increase the number of participants who can be tested in each condition within the same time.","To spread order effects (practice, fatigue, carry-over) evenly over the conditions, so no condition is favoured."],"a":3,"pct":0,"typ":"","theme":1,"spot":"","plain":"","work":"Counterbalancing does not eliminate order effects; it balances them across conditions so they are not confounded with the manipulation. They then add noise but do not systematically favour any one condition.","wrongs":["","","",""],"trap":"","src":"End-of-term mock, question 48  ·  Research basics"},{"n":49,"q":"Statement 1: To conclude that X influences Y, it is sufficient that X and Y covary and that X occurs before Y.\nStatement 2: If X and Y covary but the direction of influence is unclear (bidirectionality), we may still conclude that X caused Y.\nWhich option is correct?","o":["Both statements are false.","Only statement 1 is true.","Both statements are true.","Only statement 2 is true."],"a":0,"pct":0,"typ":"","theme":1,"spot":"","plain":"","work":"Causal inference needs three things: covariation, temporal precedence and the elimination of alternative explanations (third variables). Statement 1 omits the third criterion. Statement 2 contradicts the second criterion: with unclear direction, temporal order has not been established.","wrongs":["","","",""],"trap":"","src":"End-of-term mock, question 49  ·  Research basics"},{"n":50,"q":"A study advisor finds that students who attend more lectures pass the course more often (p = .018). A colleague suggests that motivated students both attend more often and study harder, which is why they pass more often. What is the colleague's suggestion an example of?","o":["A Type I error: the association is a false-positive finding","Regression to the mean after extreme scores on one variable","A third variable offering an alternative explanation","Reverse causality: passing makes students attend more"],"a":2,"pct":0,"typ":"","theme":1,"spot":"","plain":"","work":"Motivation is a third variable that affects both attendance and passing, so it can produce the association without attendance causing anything. Reverse causality would be \"passing makes students attend more\"; a Type I error is a false positive; regression to the mean is about extreme scores moving towards the average on remeasurement.","wrongs":["","","",""],"trap":"","src":"End-of-term mock, question 50  ·  Research basics"},{"n":51,"q":"Which statement about within-subjects and between-subjects designs is TRUE?","o":["Within-subjects designs never need counterbalancing, because each participant acts as their own control.","Within-subjects designs need far more participants for the same power, because each person counts once.","Within-subjects designs usually have more power, but they are open to order and carry-over effects.","Between-subjects designs are generally more sensitive, because every participant is tested only once."],"a":2,"pct":0,"typ":"","theme":1,"spot":"","plain":"","work":"In a within-subjects design stable differences between people (IQ, baseline heart rate) are removed from the error term, which increases sensitivity and reduces the required number of participants. The price is that earlier conditions may influence later ones (practice, fatigue, carry-over), which is why the order is counterbalanced.","wrongs":["","","",""],"trap":"","src":"End-of-term mock, question 51  ·  Research basics"},{"n":52,"q":"Psychologist Tom surveys 450 young people. Of those who game at least 4 hours a day, 60% report sleep problems, versus 34% of non-gamers (p &lt; .001). Tom concludes: \"Excessive gaming causes sleep problems.\" What is the main problem with this conclusion?","o":["He should have used a within-subjects design, which by itself would have proven causation.","The data are correlational, so reverse causation and third variables are not ruled out.","With 450 participants the p-value will always be significant, so the result is meaningless.","He compared percentages instead of means, which cannot show a difference between groups."],"a":1,"pct":0,"typ":"","theme":1,"spot":"","plain":"","work":"Gaming was not manipulated, so the groups may differ in other ways (stress, age, screen use at night), and sleep problems could also lead to gaming. Association is not causation. A significant p-value does not change this, and a within-subjects design is not by itself a guarantee of causal inference.","wrongs":["","","",""],"trap":"","src":"End-of-term mock, question 52  ·  Research basics"},{"n":53,"q":"A school offers an optional mindfulness course. Pupils decide for themselves whether to take it. Afterwards, participants score higher on attention than non-participants. Which threat to internal validity is most obvious here?","o":["Maturation: pupils simply become older and more able to concentrate during the course period.","Selection: pupils who opt in may already differ, for example in motivation.","Instrumentation: the attention test changed in difficulty between the measurements.","History: an unrelated event, such as an exam period, happened during the course period."],"a":1,"pct":0,"typ":"","theme":1,"spot":"","plain":"","work":"Participants selected themselves, so the groups likely differed before the course (selection). Maturation and history would affect both groups similarly, and no information suggests the test changed.","wrongs":["","","",""],"trap":"","src":"End-of-term mock, question 53  ·  Research basics"}]},{"id":"tut1","name":"Practice set 1","short":"Practice set 1","blurb":"The practice paper with the official answer key, all 16 questions, transcribed exactly as it was handed out. It walks the whole course in order: one-way ANOVA and post-hoc logic, repeated measures, two-way designs, correlation and regression, chi-square, proportions and confidence intervals, and it closes with two true or false statement pairs on research design. Every number in the key has been recomputed. Question 1 had an output image that could not be read, so that table is rebuilt from the official solution.","theme":0,"icon":"📘","group":"paper","questions":[{"n":1,"q":"In a study boys and girls are compared with respect to their assertiveness scores. This results in the following output. What is the F-value for this ANOVA?","o":["2.62","3.73","1.92","2.31"],"a":0,"pct":50,"typ":"f_from_table","theme":0,"spot":"HINT. A table of sums of squares and degrees of freedom, and the question asks for F. You never need the raw scores. F always comes from the MS column, so the only real work is turning each SS into an MS first.","plain":"In plain words: how big is the difference between boys and girls compared with the ordinary spread of pupils inside each group? F is that comparison, written as a ratio.","work":"Step 1. Turn the between row into a mean square. MS between = SS between / df between = 14.45 / 1 = 14.45. With one df the SS and the MS are the same number, which is why the table looks like it repeats itself.\nStep 2. Turn the within row into a mean square. MS within = SS within / df within = 99.3 / 18 = 5.5167.\nStep 3. Divide. F = 14.45 / 5.5167 = 2.62.\nStep 4. Sense check on the design. df between = 1 means 2 groups. df within = N - k = 18, so N = 20 pupils, 10 boys and 10 girls. df total = 19, and 1 + 18 = 19, so the table balances.\nStep 5. Sense check on the size of F. An F near 1 means the groups differ about as much as pupils inside a group do. 2.62 is bigger than 1 but the cut-off for df = 1, 18 at the .05 level is 4.41, so this result is not significant. The exact p-value is .123.\nMemory hook: never divide an SS by an SS. Climb down to MS first, then divide.","wrongs":["","This is a cut-off, not a result. The critical value for df = 1, 18 is 3.01 at the .10 level and 4.41 at the .05 level, so 3.73 sits between the two tabled cut-offs. It is a decoy built to look like a number from the F table. No row of this output produces it.","An F under 2 cannot come out of this table. To get 1.92 you would have to divide 14.45 by about 7.5, and the largest mean square you could even argue for here is MS total = 113.75 / 19 = 5.99, which still gives 2.41. If your F is far below the other options, check that you divided by the WITHIN row.","This is the total row creeping in. Using SS total instead of SS within gives 14.45 / (113.75 / 18) = 2.29, or 14.45 / (113.75 / 19) = 2.41. This option sits in that region. The denominator of F is the error row only, never the total row."],"trap":"Dividing 14.45 by 99.3 and getting 0.15. Sums of squares are not comparable until each one is divided by its own degrees of freedom.","tri":"Symptom: an ANOVA table with SS and df, and a question asking for F. -&gt; Diagnosis: build F from mean squares. -&gt; Treatment: MS = SS / df for each row, then F = MS between / MS within.","src":"Practice set 1, question 1","recon":"Reconstructed: the printed output was an image the text could not read, so I rebuilt the table from the official solution, which states SS between = 14.45 with df = 1, SS within = 99.3 with df = 18 and MS within = 5.5167; those numbers force the rest of the table (SS total 113.75, df total 19, so 20 pupils, 10 per group) and they are the only values that give the printed answer of 2.62.","out":{"cap":"Reconstructed one-way ANOVA table: assertiveness, boys versus girls","h":["Source","SS","df","MS","F"],"r":[["Between groups","14.45","1","14.45","?"],["Within groups","99.30","18","5.52",""],["Total","113.75","19","",""]]}},{"n":2,"q":"Under what circumstances are post-hoc tests necessary?","o":["When you reject the null hypothesis in a study with 2 different treatments","When you do not reject the null hypothesis in a study with more than 2 different treatments","When you do not reject the null hypothesis in a study with 2 different treatments","When you reject the null hypothesis in a study with more than 2 different treatments"],"a":3,"pct":50,"typ":"posthoc_when","theme":0,"spot":"HINT. The words \"post-hoc\" plus \"when\" or \"under what circumstances\" means the question is about two conditions at once, not about any calculation. Every option here is a pairing of a decision (reject or not) with a number of groups. So check both halves of each option separately.","plain":"In plain words: when is it worth hunting for WHICH groups differ? Only when the overall test has already told you that somewhere there is a difference, and only when there are enough groups for \"which ones\" to be a real question.","work":"Step 1. Take the first condition. A significant F says \"not all of these population means are equal\". It does not say which pair. That unanswered question is the whole reason post-hoc tests exist. If F is not significant you have nothing to follow up, so you stop.\nStep 2. Take the second condition. With exactly 2 groups there is only one possible pair. A significant F has already told you those two differ, so a follow-up test would just repeat it. With 3 or more groups there are several pairs, so \"which ones\" is a genuine question.\nStep 3. Put them together. Reject the null AND more than 2 treatments. That is option d.\nStep 4. Sense check. Why not just run all the pairwise t tests from the start? Because with k = 3 groups there are m = 3 pairs and the chance of at least one false alarm is 1 - .95 to the power 3 = .14, not .05. Post-hoc tests are built to keep that error rate under control.\nMemory hook: F says SOMETHING differs, post-hoc says WHICH. No \"something\", nothing to ask \"which\" about.","wrongs":["The first half is right and the second half is wrong. With only 2 treatments a significant F already names the difference, because there is only one pair to name. There is nothing left to follow up.","Both halves are wrong in the same direction. A non-significant F means you have no evidence of any difference, so there is nothing to localise. Running post-hoc tests after a non-significant F is fishing.","Both halves are wrong. No significant result, and only one possible pair. This is the option that gets picked when you read \"post-hoc\" as \"something you always do afterwards\" rather than as a follow-up that needs a signal first.",""],"trap":"Treating post-hoc tests as a routine extra step. They are conditional. No significant overall F means no post-hoc tests, whatever the number of groups.","tri":"Symptom: a question asking when post-hoc tests are needed. -&gt; Diagnosis: the two conditions for follow-up testing. -&gt; Treatment: you need a significant overall F AND more than two groups. Both halves, or no post-hoc.","src":"Practice set 1, question 2"},{"n":3,"q":"After performing a Repeated Measures ANOVA a researcher finds a F-ratio with df = 2, 12. How many people participated in this study?","o":["13","15","7","14"],"a":2,"pct":50,"typ":"backout_n_rm","theme":0,"spot":"HINT. You are given a df pair and asked for a number of PEOPLE. That is always a work-backwards question. The one thing you must notice first is the words \"Repeated Measures\", because the error df formula is different from the independent-groups one, and the whole answer hangs on which formula you use.","plain":"In plain words: the two df values are a fingerprint of the design. The first number tells you how many conditions there were, the second tells you how many people were measured in all of them. Read the fingerprint backwards.","work":"Step 1. Read the numerator. df model = k - 1 = 2, so k = 3 conditions. Every person was measured three times.\nStep 2. Write down the right error formula. In repeated measures, df error = (n - 1)(k - 1), where n is the number of PERSONS. Here that is (n - 1) x 2 = 12.\nStep 3. Solve. (n - 1) x 2 = 12, so n - 1 = 6, so n = 7. Seven people.\nStep 4. Sense check by rebuilding the table. With n = 7 and k = 3 there are 21 measurements, so df total = 20. Split that up: df conditions = 2, df persons = n - 1 = 6, df error = 12, and 2 + 6 + 12 = 20. It balances, which is the proof that 7 is right.\nStep 5. Notice the power point. Only 7 people, yet 12 error df. That is why repeated measures designs are efficient: the differences between people are pulled out into their own row instead of sitting in the error term.\nMemory hook: in repeated measures the error df is a RECTANGLE, (n - 1) times (k - 1). In independent groups it is a SUBTRACTION, N - k.","wrongs":["This used df error = n - 1, the one-sample rule, and read 12 as n - 1 to get n = 13. That formula has no (k - 1) in it, so it quietly assumes each person gave one score, which is the opposite of a repeated measures design.","This is the independent-groups reading. Treating the pair as (k - 1, N - k) gives N = 12 + 3 = 15 separate people. It is the right arithmetic for the wrong design, and it is the mistake the question is built to catch.","","This reads 12 as N - k with k = 2, giving 14. Two wrongs at once: the subtraction rule belongs to independent groups, and the numerator already told you there were 3 conditions, not 2."],"trap":"Using N - k because that is the ANOVA df rule you learned first. The words \"Repeated Measures\" in the stem are there to switch you to (n - 1)(k - 1). Read the design before you reach for a formula.","tri":"Symptom: a repeated measures df pair and a question about the number of participants. -&gt; Diagnosis: unpack n from df error = (n - 1)(k - 1). -&gt; Treatment: 1 get k from df model = k - 1, 2 put k into (n - 1)(k - 1) = df error, 3 solve for n.","src":"Practice set 1, question 3"},{"n":4,"q":"A repeated measures ANOVA has more power than an ANOVA for independent groups, because one variance component is calculated differently. Which variance component is this?","o":["The Sum of Squares Total","The Sum of Squares Model","The Sum of Squares Residual","None of the above"],"a":2,"pct":50,"typ":"rm_power","theme":0,"spot":"HINT. The question says \"more power\" and \"variance component\". Power questions are nearly always questions about the DENOMINATOR of the test statistic, because that is where the noise lives. So look for the error term among the options.","plain":"In plain words: the same people are measured in every condition, so you can measure how much of the mess is just \"some people score high and some score low\" and take it out. What is left over as error is smaller, so the signal stands out more.","work":"Step 1. Remember what makes F big. F = MS model / MS error. You can raise F by making the top bigger or the bottom smaller. The design cannot change the real size of the treatment effect, so the gain has to come from the bottom.\nStep 2. See what is in the bottom in each design. In independent groups, the error term holds everything that makes two people in the same condition differ, including stable differences between people.\nStep 3. See what changes with repeated measures. Each person appears in every condition, so their personal level can be estimated and given its own row, the between-persons row. That chunk is subtracted out of the residual.\nStep 4. Read the consequence. SS total and SS model are unchanged in the way they are defined. SS residual is what is recomputed, and it is smaller. Smaller MS error, bigger F, more power. That is option c.\nStep 5. Sense check with the df. The residual df also changes, from N - k to (n - 1)(k - 1), which is exactly what happens when a new row is carved out of the old error row.\nExplain it to a friend: you stop blaming the error term for the fact that people are simply different from each other.","wrongs":["SS total is just the total spread of all the scores around the grand mean. The same scores give the same total however you analyse them. What changes is how that total is CUT UP, not how big it is.","SS model is the between-conditions part, built from the condition means. Those means are the signal, and they are computed the same way in both designs. The power gain is not on this side of the ratio.","","There is a specific right answer here, so \"none of the above\" is wrong. Use this option only when you have actually ruled all three out, not when you are unsure."],"trap":"Thinking the gain comes from a bigger effect. It comes from a smaller error term. Repeated measures does not strengthen the treatment, it cleans up the comparison.","tri":"Symptom: a question about why repeated measures has more power. -&gt; Diagnosis: the error term is computed differently. -&gt; Treatment: name the residual, because the person-to-person variation is removed from it, which shrinks MS error and raises F.","src":"Practice set 1, question 4"},{"n":5,"q":"In an independent measures study with two factors A and B, a 2-factor ANOVA is performed. In the 2-factor ANOVA the FA for factor A has df = 2, 36 and the FB for factor B has df = 3, 36. Based on this information, what are the df for the interaction AxB?","o":["df = 6, 72","df = 5, 72","df = 5, 36","df = 6, 36"],"a":3,"pct":75,"typ":"df_interaction","theme":0,"spot":"HINT. Two df pairs given, one df pair asked for. Notice at once that both given pairs end in the SAME second number, 36. That is the paper telling you that every F in a two-way ANOVA shares one error term. Half the answer is already on the page.","plain":"In plain words: factor A has some number of levels, factor B has some number of levels, and the interaction asks whether the effect of A changes across the levels of B. Its df is the two level-counts minus one each, multiplied. The error df does not move.","work":"Step 1. Unpack the levels. df A = a - 1 = 2, so factor A has 3 levels. df B = b - 1 = 3, so factor B has 4 levels. The design is 3 x 4, which is 12 cells.\nStep 2. Build the interaction df by MULTIPLYING. df AxB = (a - 1)(b - 1) = 2 x 3 = 6.\nStep 3. Leave the error df alone. All three F-ratios in a two-way ANOVA have the same denominator, MS error, so they all carry the same error df. Both given pairs say 36, so the interaction pair is 6, 36. That is option d.\nStep 4. Sense check the whole table. Error df = N - ab = 36 with ab = 12, so N = 48 people, 4 per cell. Then df total = N - 1 = 47 and the rows add up: 2 + 3 + 6 + 36 = 47. It balances.\nStep 5. Spot the pattern worth keeping. Main effect df add, interaction df multiply. 2 + 3 = 5 is never an interaction df.\nMemory hook: an interaction is a GRID of combinations, so its df is a product, not a sum.","wrongs":["The 6 is right and the 72 is invented. Nothing doubles when you move to the interaction row. Error df is a property of the whole table, not of each effect, and both printed pairs already show 36.","Wrong twice: 2 + 3 = 5 adds the df instead of multiplying them, and 72 doubles an error df that does not change.","The error df is right, the interaction df is the classic addition slip. 2 + 3 = 5. Interaction df is (a - 1)(b - 1), a product. With a 3 x 4 design the interaction has 6 df.",""],"trap":"Adding 2 and 3 to get 5. Main effects add up to the interaction only in your head, never in the arithmetic. Multiply.","tri":"Symptom: main-effect df given, interaction df wanted. -&gt; Diagnosis: two-way ANOVA degrees of freedom. -&gt; Treatment: 1 levels from a - 1 and b - 1, 2 interaction df = (a - 1)(b - 1), 3 keep the error df exactly as printed.","src":"Practice set 1, question 5"},{"n":6,"q":"The 2-factor ANOVA gives:","o":["two different F-ratios","an overall F-ratio followed by the required post hoc tests","three different F-ratios","four different F-ratios."],"a":2,"pct":50,"typ":"two_way_concept","theme":0,"spot":"HINT. A one-line question that just asks what the output contains. Count the effects a two-factor design has, and you have counted the F-ratios, because each effect gets its own line in the table.","plain":"In plain words: with two factors there are three separate questions you can ask: does A matter, does B matter, and does A behave differently depending on B. Three questions, three F-ratios.","work":"Step 1. List the effects. Main effect of A. Main effect of B. Interaction AxB. Three rows of interest.\nStep 2. Remember how the table is built. Each of those rows has its own SS and df, so its own MS, and each MS is divided by the SAME MS error. Three numerators, one denominator, three F-ratios.\nStep 3. Rule out the extra row. The error row and the total row appear in the table but they do not get an F of their own, which is what makes \"four\" wrong.\nStep 4. Sense check against the previous question. There the printed output had FA with df = 2, 36 and FB with df = 3, 36, and the question asked for the df of the third F. Three F-ratios is exactly what that paper showed.\nMemory hook: two factors, three questions, three F-ratios. The interaction is always the one people forget to count.","wrongs":["This counts the two main effects and forgets the interaction, which is usually the most interesting row of the three. If a design has two factors, it always has an interaction to test.","This is the one-way ANOVA picture: one overall F, then follow-up tests. A two-way ANOVA does not have a single overall F. Post-hoc tests may follow a significant main effect with 3 or more levels, but they are not what the analysis \"gives\".","","This counts a row too many, usually by giving the error or total row an F-ratio. Those rows have no F. Only effects are tested, and there are three effects."],"trap":"Forgetting the interaction. Two factors do not give two tests, they give three, and the third one is often the point of running the study.","tri":"Symptom: how many F-ratios does a two-factor ANOVA produce. -&gt; Diagnosis: count the effects. -&gt; Treatment: main effect of A, main effect of B, interaction AxB. That is 3.","src":"Practice set 1, question 6"},{"n":7,"q":"If we want to determine the strength of a relation using a scatterplot, we pay attention to:","o":["How closely the points fall on a straight line.","How close the points are together.","Whether the points show an increasing or a decreasing relation","None of the above"],"a":0,"pct":50,"typ":"scatter_strength","theme":0,"spot":"HINT. The word \"strength\" with a scatterplot. A scatterplot shows three things at once: form, direction and strength. The question names one of them, so answer about that one only and ignore the options that describe the other two.","plain":"In plain words: strength means how tightly the cloud of points hugs a straight line. A thin cigar shape is strong, a round blob is weak. Which way it tilts is a different question.","work":"Step 1. Separate the three readings of a scatterplot. Form: is the pattern straight or curved. Direction: does it go up or down. Strength: how closely do the points cluster around that pattern.\nStep 2. Match strength to the right option. Only option a talks about closeness TO A LINE. That is the definition of strength, and it is what the size of r measures.\nStep 3. Connect it to r. r near 1 or near -1 means the points sit almost on a line, so the relation is strong. r near 0 means a shapeless cloud. The sign of r carries the direction, the size of r carries the strength. Two separate jobs for one number.\nStep 4. Sense check with an example. Points at (1, 10), (2, 20), (3, 30) are far apart on the page but sit perfectly on a line, so r = 1. Strong, even though the points are not close TOGETHER. That is the difference between option a and option b.\nMemory hook: strength is about closeness to the LINE, not closeness to each OTHER.","wrongs":["","This is the tempting one. Points can be spread right across the plot and still be a perfect straight line, and they can be bunched in a tight round blob with r = 0. Crowding on the page is not strength. Zooming a plot in or out would change crowding without changing r at all.","Increasing or decreasing is DIRECTION, the sign of r. A relation can be decreasing and extremely strong, like r = -0.95. Direction tells you which way, not how much.","Option a is a correct definition of strength, so \"none of the above\" cannot be right. Keep this option for when you have genuinely eliminated all three."],"trap":"Reading \"strength\" as \"how close the dots are to each other\". It means how close they are to a straight line.","tri":"Symptom: a scatterplot and the word strength. -&gt; Diagnosis: strength is tightness around a line, not tilt and not crowding. -&gt; Treatment: say how closely the points fall on a straight line.","src":"Practice set 1, question 7"},{"n":8,"q":"The null hypothesis used in hypothesis testing with the Pearson correlation is:","o":["the correlation is 0 in the population","the correlation is not equal to 0 in the sample.","the correlation is not equal to 0 in the population.","the correlation is 0 in the sample."],"a":0,"pct":50,"typ":"corr_hyp","theme":0,"spot":"HINT. A hypothesis-wording question. Every option here is one of two choices crossed with another: equal to zero or not equal to zero, and sample or population. So screen the options on two features, not on how they sound. The null is always the \"no effect\" version, and hypotheses are always about the population.","plain":"In plain words: a hypothesis is a claim about the world, not about your spreadsheet. You already know what your sample did. The question is whether that pattern exists out there, and the null says it does not.","work":"Step 1. Apply the first filter: null or alternative. A null hypothesis is always the statement of nothing happening. For a correlation that is \"no linear relationship\", which means the correlation is 0. That throws out both the \"not equal to 0\" options at once.\nStep 2. Apply the second filter: sample or population. You calculated r from your sample, so there is nothing left to hypothesise about it. Hypotheses are about the unknown population value, written rho. That throws out the remaining sample option.\nStep 3. What survives. The correlation is 0 in the population. Option a.\nStep 4. Sense check on how the test then works. You assume rho = 0, ask how often a sample of this size would produce an r as far from 0 as yours, and that is the p-value. The logic only makes sense if the null is about the population and the data are the sample.\nMemory hook: hypotheses are always about rho, never about r. Greek letter for the claim, Latin letter for the data.","wrongs":["","Two mistakes in one line. It states a difference, which makes it an alternative rather than a null, and it makes a claim about the sample, which is already observed.","This is the ALTERNATIVE hypothesis, correctly worded about the population. It is the statement you accept if you reject the null. Picking it means you read past the word \"null\" in the stem.","Right idea of \"no relationship\", wrong target. Your sample r is a known number, probably not exactly 0, so claiming it is 0 is not a hypothesis, it is a false statement about data you can see."],"trap":"Writing the hypothesis about r instead of rho. The test asks about the population; the sample is the evidence, not the claim.","tri":"Symptom: which hypothesis belongs to a Pearson correlation test. -&gt; Diagnosis: null equals no relationship, stated about the population. -&gt; Treatment: rho = 0 in the population.","src":"Practice set 1, question 8"},{"n":9,"q":"The Pearson correlation between X1 and Y is r = 0.40 and sY2 = 100. When we add a second variable, X2, to the regression model we get R2 = 0.25. How much additional variance is explained by the addition of X2 compared to the regression model with only X1?","o":["0.15 or 15%","0.09 or 9%","0.40 or 40%","0.25 or 25%"],"a":1,"pct":50,"typ":"r2_change","theme":0,"spot":"HINT. The word \"additional\" with one r and one R squared in the stem. Additional always means a subtraction, and the two things you subtract must be in the same unit. One of the numbers given is a correlation and the other is already a proportion of variance, so one of them has to be squared before you can subtract anything.","plain":"In plain words: the first predictor already explained part of Y. The two predictors together explain more. The question is how much the second one added on top, so you need both amounts as proportions of variance, then take the difference.","work":"Step 1. Convert the one-predictor model into explained variance. With a single predictor R squared is just r squared, so R squared = 0.40 x 0.40 = 0.16, that is 16 per cent.\nStep 2. Read off the two-predictor model. R squared = 0.25, that is 25 per cent.\nStep 3. Subtract. 0.25 - 0.16 = 0.09, so X2 adds 9 per cent of the variance in Y. Option b.\nStep 4. Deal with the spare number. sY2 = 100 is the variance of Y, and it is not needed for the proportion. If you did want the answer in score units: 9 per cent of 100 = 9 units of variance. The paper puts sY2 there to see whether you know what the question is asking for.\nStep 5. Sense check. Adding a predictor can never lower R squared, so the gain must be 0 or more. 0.09 is positive and smaller than 0.25, which is the most it could possibly be. It passes.\nMemory hook: r is a direction with a size, r squared is a share of the variance. Only shares can be added and subtracted.","wrongs":["This subtracted r from R squared: 0.40 - 0.25 = 0.15. It mixes units, like subtracting metres from square metres, and it comes out backwards too, since the one-predictor model cannot explain more than the two-predictor model.","","This is just r, the correlation you started with. It is not a share of variance and it is not a gain. Squaring is the step that was skipped.","This is the whole R squared of the two-predictor model, not the extra bit. It is what X1 and X2 explain TOGETHER. The word \"additional\" asks you to take off what X1 was already explaining."],"trap":"Forgetting to square the 0.40. r and R squared are different kinds of number, and the subtraction is only legal once both are proportions of variance.","tri":"Symptom: an r for one predictor, an R squared for two, and the word additional. -&gt; Diagnosis: change in R squared. -&gt; Treatment: 1 square the r, 2 subtract it from the R squared of the bigger model.","src":"Practice set 1, question 9"},{"n":10,"q":"The regression equation is determined by minimizing:","o":["the total squared error (residual) between the predicted and the observed Y values.","the total absolute error (residual) between the predicted and the observed Y value.","the total absolute error (residual) between the values of X and Y.","the total squared error (residual) between the values of X and Y."],"a":0,"pct":50,"typ":"least_squares","theme":0,"spot":"HINT. A definition question with four options that cross two features: squared or absolute, and \"predicted versus observed Y\" or \"X versus Y\". Tick both features for each option. The name of the method, least squares, gives you the first feature for free.","plain":"In plain words: of all the straight lines you could draw through the cloud of points, the regression line is the one that makes the up-and-down misses as small as possible overall. Misses are measured on Y, and they are squared before adding up.","work":"Step 1. Define a residual properly. A residual is observed Y minus predicted Y, for one case. It is a vertical distance, measured in the units of Y. It is never a distance between X and Y, which are different variables in different units.\nStep 2. Say why they get squared. Residuals above and below the line have opposite signs and would cancel out if you simply added them. Squaring makes every miss positive and penalises a big miss more than several small ones.\nStep 3. Name the criterion. Choose the intercept and slope that make the sum of the squared residuals as small as possible. That is \"least squares\", and it is option a.\nStep 4. Sense check against something you already use. SS error in the regression ANOVA table IS this minimised quantity, and MSE comes from it. The whole output is built on this one criterion.\nMemory hook: least SQUARES, and the squares are vertical misses in Y.","wrongs":["","Absolute instead of squared. Minimising absolute errors is a real method, called least absolute deviations, but it is not what ordinary regression does and it is not what the output you read in this course is built from.","Wrong on both counts: absolute instead of squared, and a distance between X and Y, which is not a residual. X and Y are different variables, often in different units, so the gap between them is not an error of prediction.","The squaring is right but the quantity is wrong. The line is not trying to make X and Y close to each other. It is trying to make the PREDICTED Y close to the OBSERVED Y."],"trap":"Reading the residual as a gap between X and Y. It is always a gap within Y: what you saw minus what the line said.","tri":"Symptom: what does the regression line minimise. -&gt; Diagnosis: the least squares criterion. -&gt; Treatment: the sum of squared differences between observed Y and predicted Y.","src":"Practice set 1, question 10"},{"n":11,"q":"In a Chi-square test of independence the term “expected counts” refers to:","o":["The counts that one deems known for the population under study","The counts observed in the sample data.","The counts observed in the population under study","The counts that can be calculated based on the null hypothesis."],"a":3,"pct":50,"typ":"expected_concept","theme":0,"spot":"HINT. A definition question about the word \"expected\". Three of the options use the word \"observed\" or \"known\", which are the opposite idea. Expected counts are never data. They are what the null hypothesis predicts.","plain":"In plain words: expected counts are the numbers you would get if the two variables had nothing to do with each other, worked out from the row and column totals of your own table. They are a prediction to compare the real data against.","work":"Step 1. Remember what the null hypothesis says. In a test of independence it says the two variables are unrelated, so the split on one variable is the same in every category of the other.\nStep 2. Turn that claim into numbers. If the variables are unrelated, each cell should hold expected = row total x column total / grand total. Every piece of that formula is a MARGIN of your table, never a cell of it. That is why the expected counts are a prediction rather than data.\nStep 3. Match to an option. Calculated on the basis of the null hypothesis. Option d.\nStep 4. See how they are used. Chi-square adds up (observed - expected) squared / expected across the cells. If the null is right, observed and expected sit close together and chi-square is small. A big chi-square is the data arguing against the null.\nStep 5. Sense check. Expected counts rarely come out as whole numbers, like 12, 18, 28, 42, or even 22.5. A count of people can never be 22.5 in reality, which is a daily reminder that these are predictions, not observations. Keep them unrounded.\nMemory hook: OBSERVED is what happened, EXPECTED is what the null predicted.","wrongs":["\"Deems known\" suggests you bring the numbers in from outside, from theory or from an earlier study. In a test of independence you do not: you derive them from the margins of this very table.","This is the definition of the OBSERVED counts. If expected and observed were the same thing, every (observed - expected) would be zero and chi-square would always be 0.","You never observe a population in this design. You observe a sample. And population counts would be data too, not a prediction.",""],"trap":"Rounding the expected counts to whole numbers because counts of people should be whole. They are predicted values, so leave them as they are, or your chi-square will drift off.","tri":"Symptom: what are expected counts. -&gt; Diagnosis: the counts predicted by the null hypothesis of independence. -&gt; Treatment: expected = row total x column total / grand total, computed under the null and kept unrounded.","src":"Practice set 1, question 11"},{"n":12,"q":"In the following study we want to know how 100 “PvdA”+”VVD” voters feel about the party they voted for at the moment. Calculate the Chi-square.","o":["chi-square = 1.14","chi-square = 0.86","chi-square = 0.79","chi-square = 0.68"],"a":2,"pct":75,"typ":"chi_calc","theme":0,"spot":"HINT. A small table of counts and the word \"calculate\". Two categorical variables crossed in a grid means a chi-square test of independence, and the work is always the same three moves: totals, expected counts, then one sum. The numbers are easy; the marks are lost in the bookkeeping.","plain":"In plain words: if feeling positive had nothing to do with which party you voted for, how many people would sit in each of the four boxes? Compare that with what really happened, and add up the mismatches.","work":"Step 1. Get the margins. Positive row = 10 + 20 = 30. Negative row = 30 + 40 = 70. PvdA column = 10 + 30 = 40. VVD column = 20 + 40 = 60. Grand total = 100, which matches the stem.\nStep 2. Expected counts, row total x column total / 100.\nPositive and PvdA: 30 x 40 / 100 = 12.\nPositive and VVD: 30 x 60 / 100 = 18.\nNegative and PvdA: 70 x 40 / 100 = 28.\nNegative and VVD: 70 x 60 / 100 = 42.\nCheck: 12 + 18 + 28 + 42 = 100, and the expected margins are 30, 70, 40, 60, the same as the observed ones. They always are, which is a free check on your arithmetic.\nStep 3. Each cell misses by the same 2, because in a two-by-two table the four differences are always the same size. (10 - 12) = -2, (20 - 18) = 2, (30 - 28) = 2, (40 - 42) = -2.\nStep 4. Add up (observed - expected) squared / expected.\n4 / 12 = 0.3333\n4 / 18 = 0.2222\n4 / 28 = 0.1429\n4 / 42 = 0.0952\nTotal chi-square = 0.7937, so 0.79. Option c.\nStep 5. Decide. df = (r - 1)(c - 1) = (2 - 1)(2 - 1) = 1. The critical value at .05 with df = 1 is 3.84, and 0.79 is nowhere near it. The exact p-value is .373, so do not reject the null. Feeling and party look independent here.\nMemory hook: squared difference divided by EXPECTED, every time. Expected is the yardstick, so expected goes on the bottom.","wrongs":["Too big for this table. The four contributions are each 4 divided by an expected count of 12 or more, so the largest possible single term is 0.33 and the total cannot reach 1. If your chi-square is over 1 here, check your expected counts: shrinking them is what inflates the total.","This is the shape of answer you get by dividing by the OBSERVED count instead of the expected one, which gives 4/10 + 4/20 + 4/30 + 4/40 = 0.83, right beside this option. The expected count is the denominator, because it is the standard you are measuring against.","","Too small. This is the region you land in if you apply a correction or drop a cell. With Yates continuity correction, for instance, each difference shrinks from 2 to 1.5 and the total falls to 0.45. This course uses the uncorrected formula, which gives 0.79."],"trap":"Dividing by the observed count. The formula is (observed - expected) squared divided by expected. Write the expected count under each term before you start adding.","tri":"Symptom: a two-by-two table of counts, calculate chi-square. -&gt; Diagnosis: chi-square test of independence. -&gt; Treatment: 1 row and column totals, 2 expected = row x column / grand total, 3 add (observed - expected) squared / expected over all cells.","src":"Practice set 1, question 12","out":{"cap":"Voted","h":["","PvdA","VVD"],"r":[["Positive","10","20"],["Negative","30","40"]]}},{"n":13,"q":"In a random sample of 60 households from City 1, 45 decorate their house during the holidays. In a random sample of 50 households from nearby City 2, 40 households decorate their house. We want to estimate the difference in the proportion of households that decorate their house using a 95% confidence interval. What is the large sample standard error of the difference in sample proportions?","o":["0.040","0.0795","0.0802","0.1125"],"a":1,"pct":50,"typ":"se_2p","theme":0,"spot":"HINT. Two independent samples, two proportions, and the words \"standard error of the difference\". The stem also says ESTIMATE and confidence interval, not test. That one word decides which of the two formulas you use: for an interval you keep the two proportions separate, you do not pool them.","plain":"In plain words: each city gives a proportion, and each proportion wobbles. The standard error of the difference measures how much the GAP between them wobbles from sample to sample. Wobbles combine through their squares, not by adding the wobbles themselves.","work":"Step 1. Get the two proportions. p1 = 45 / 60 = 0.75 in City 1. p2 = 40 / 50 = 0.80 in City 2.\nStep 2. Variance of each proportion. p1 q1 / n1 = 0.75 x 0.25 / 60 = 0.003125. p2 q2 / n2 = 0.80 x 0.20 / 50 = 0.0032.\nStep 3. Add the variances, because the samples are independent. 0.003125 + 0.0032 = 0.006325. Variances add even though the proportions get subtracted, and that is the one thing to remember here: uncertainty always piles up, it never cancels.\nStep 4. Take the square root. SE = square root of 0.006325 = 0.0795. Option b.\nStep 5. Sense check. Each single proportion has a standard error of about 0.056, so the difference must be noisier than either one alone, but less than the two added together (0.112). 0.0795 sits between them, which is right.\nStep 6. Finish the job the stem started. The difference is 0.80 - 0.75 = 0.05, and 0.05 plus or minus 1.96 x 0.0795 runs from -0.106 to 0.206. It contains zero, so these two cities are not shown to differ.\nMemory hook: for an INTERVAL, keep the two proportions apart. Pooling belongs to a TEST, where the null says the two are the same.","wrongs":["This pools the two samples into one and divides by the combined size: pooled p = 85 / 110 = 0.7727, and the square root of 0.7727 x 0.2273 / 110 = 0.040. It treats 110 households as one big sample, which throws away the fact that you are comparing two separate ones. Far too small.","","This is the POOLED standard error: pooled p = 85 / 110 = 0.7727, then the square root of 0.7727 x 0.2273 x (1/60 + 1/50) = 0.0802. It is the right formula for a hypothesis TEST, where the null assumes the two proportions are equal, so you may share information between them. An interval makes no such assumption, so it uses the unpooled version. Note how close the two answers are, 0.0795 against 0.0802, which is why you must choose on the basis of the word \"estimate\", not the size of the number.","This added the two standard errors instead of adding the variances: 0.0559 + 0.0566 = 0.1125. Standard errors never add directly. Square them, add, then take the root."],"trap":"Pooling out of habit. Pool for a test of equal proportions, keep them separate for a confidence interval. The word \"estimate\" in the stem is the signal.","tri":"Symptom: two independent proportions and a confidence interval for their difference. -&gt; Diagnosis: unpooled standard error of a difference in proportions. -&gt; Treatment: SE = square root of (p1 q1 / n1 + p2 q2 / n2), with each sample proportion used as it is.","src":"Practice set 1, question 13"},{"n":14,"q":"A sociologist determines that out of 200 couples that had children within 2 years of getting married, 80 were divorced after 5 years. If p is the population proportion of couples that had children within 2 years of getting married and were divorced after 5 years, what is the 90% confidence interval for this proportion?","o":["0.400 ± 0.035","0.400 ± 0.057","0.400 ± 0.067","0.400 ± 0.088"],"a":1,"pct":50,"typ":"ci1p","theme":0,"spot":"HINT. One sample, a count out of a total, and a confidence level. That is a confidence interval for a single proportion. Because all four options start with the same 0.400, the whole question is really about the margin of error, so go straight to the multiplier and the standard error.","plain":"In plain words: 40 per cent of these couples divorced. If you repeated the study you would get a slightly different percentage. The interval says how far off the true figure could plausibly be, with 90 per cent confidence.","work":"Step 1. Sample proportion. p-hat = 80 / 200 = 0.400. That matches the centre of every option, so it is just a check that you read the stem.\nStep 2. Standard error. The square root of 0.400 x 0.600 / 200 = the square root of 0.0012 = 0.0346.\nStep 3. Multiplier. Proportions use z*, not t*, because there is no sample standard deviation to estimate: the spread of a proportion is fixed by the proportion itself. For 90 per cent confidence, z* = 1.645. Keep the three you need: 1.645 for 90, 1.96 for 95, 2.576 for 99.\nStep 4. Margin of error. 1.645 x 0.0346 = 0.057. So the interval is 0.400 plus or minus 0.057, which is 0.343 to 0.457. Option b.\nStep 5. Sense check. 90 per cent confidence must give a NARROWER interval than 95 per cent. At 95 per cent the margin would be 1.96 x 0.0346 = 0.068. So the right answer has to be smaller than 0.068, which rules out two options immediately.\nMemory hook: proportion means z*, mean with s means t*. And 90 per cent is 1.645, the number people reach for least and need most.","wrongs":["This is the standard error on its own, 0.0346, with the multiplier left off. A margin of error is z* times the standard error. Without the multiplier you have an interval of only about 68 per cent confidence, not 90.","","This used z* = 1.96, the 95 per cent multiplier: 1.96 x 0.0346 = 0.068. The stem asks for 90 per cent. More confidence always costs width, so if you want less confidence the interval must get narrower, not wider.","This is the 99 per cent region: z* = 2.576 gives 2.576 x 0.0346 = 0.089, this option to the last digit but one. Reaching for the biggest multiplier makes the interval nearly twice as wide as the one that was asked for."],"trap":"Using 1.96 on autopilot. Read the confidence level first and pick the matching z*. 90 per cent is 1.645.","tri":"Symptom: a single sample proportion and a confidence level. -&gt; Diagnosis: confidence interval for one proportion. -&gt; Treatment: p-hat plus or minus z* times the square root of p-hat (1 - p-hat) / n, with z* = 1.645 for 90 per cent.","src":"Practice set 1, question 14"},{"n":15,"q":"Read the following statements:\n\nStatement I:\nOverall, between-subjects designs require more participants than within-subjects designs.\n\nStatement II:\nA single-factor experimental design must include at least two conditions.","o":["Both statements are true","I is true, II is false","I is false, I is true","Both are false"],"a":0,"pct":50,"typ":"design_statements","theme":0,"spot":"HINT. Two statements and four combinations. Never judge the pair as a whole. Mark statement I true or false on its own, write the letter down, then do statement II, then read off the option that matches. Guessing the \"feel\" of the pair is how marks vanish here.","plain":"In plain words: statement I says you need more people when each person is in only one condition. Statement II says an experiment with one factor still needs at least two conditions to compare. Decide on each separately.","work":"Step 1. Statement I. In a between-subjects design each person gives data in one condition only, so with 3 conditions and 20 people per condition you need 60 people. Within-subjects, the same 20 people do all three conditions, so 20 people are enough. True.\nStep 2. Why that is true, in one line. Within-subjects designs reuse participants, and they also remove the differences between people from the error term, so they need fewer people for the same power.\nStep 3. Statement II. A factor is a variable that is manipulated, and you cannot manipulate anything with one setting. With a single condition there is nothing to compare it against. So at least two levels are needed. True.\nStep 4. Combine. True and true, so option a.\nStep 5. Sense check against the statistics. A single-factor design with two conditions is analysed with a t test or a one-way ANOVA with k = 2. With one condition df between = k - 1 = 0, so there is no test at all. The arithmetic agrees with the design logic.\nMemory hook: between-subjects spends PEOPLE, within-subjects spends each person's TIME. And one condition is not an experiment, it is an observation.","wrongs":["","This marks statement II false. But a factor with only one level offers nothing to compare, and df between would be 0. Two conditions is the minimum for any experiment.","Note the typo in this option: it should read \"I is false, II is true\". Either way it marks statement I false, and statement I is true. Between-subjects designs need more people because nobody is reused.","Both statements are standard, correct textbook points, so this is the option to pick when you have mixed up both halves at once."],"trap":"Reading the two statements as a package and answering on the overall feel. Mark them one at a time, in writing, before you look at the options.","tri":"Symptom: two design statements, four true or false combinations. -&gt; Diagnosis: judge each statement alone. -&gt; Treatment: mark I, mark II, then match the option. Here both are true.","src":"Practice set 1, question 15"},{"n":16,"q":"Read the following statements:\n\nStatement I:\nSimply by taking the same test twice, participants’ mean score improves. This is an instrumentation effect.\n\nStatement II:\nEven in a randomized experiment, the potential for attrition remains a concern.","o":["Both statements are true","I is true, II is false","I is false, I is true","Both are false"],"a":2,"pct":50,"typ":"validity_threats","theme":0,"spot":"HINT. Two statements about threats to internal validity, and one of them puts a NAME on an effect. When a statement names a term, the trick is usually the name, not the effect. Ask whether the label fits the thing described.","plain":"In plain words: statement I describes people getting better at a test just by having sat it before, but gives it the wrong label. Statement II says people dropping out still matters even when you randomised at the start. Judge each one on its own.","work":"Step 1. Statement I, the effect itself. Scores going up because people have already taken the test is real and common. People remember items and get used to the format.\nStep 2. Statement I, the label. That is a TESTING effect, also called a practice effect. INSTRUMENTATION means the measuring instrument itself changed: a different questionnaire version, a recalibrated machine, an observer who has become stricter over time. Here the test did not change, the participants did. Wrong label, so statement I is false.\nStep 3. Statement II. Randomisation balances the groups at the START. It cannot control who walks away afterwards. If the people who drop out of the treatment group are the ones it was not helping, the groups are no longer comparable at the end. So attrition is still a threat. True.\nStep 4. Combine. I false, II true. Option c, keeping in mind that the option is printed with a typo: \"I is false, I is true\" must mean \"I is false, II is true\".\nStep 5. Memory hook: instrumentation is the RULER changing, testing is the PERSON changing. And randomisation protects the start of a study, never the end.","wrongs":["This marks statement I true, which means accepting the label \"instrumentation\" for what is really a testing or practice effect. The effect described is real; the name given to it is not.","Both halves are wrong. Statement I is mislabelled, and statement II is a correct and important point: random assignment does nothing about people leaving the study later.","","This marks statement II false, which would mean randomisation protects you from dropout. It does not. It only makes the groups comparable at the moment of assignment."],"trap":"Marking a statement true because the effect it describes is real, without checking the name attached to it. Instrumentation and testing effects are different threats.","tri":"Symptom: two statements on validity threats, one carrying a technical label. -&gt; Diagnosis: check the label against the definition, then judge the second claim. -&gt; Treatment: I is mislabelled so it is false, II is true, which gives the \"I false, II true\" option.","src":"Practice set 1, question 16"}]},{"id":"tut2","name":"Practice set 2","short":"Practice set 2","blurb":"The second practice paper, all 14 questions, transcribed exactly as it was handed out. Its instruction line reads: unless otherwise stated, use a significance level of .05 and assume that the relevant statistical assumptions are met. This paper leans on interpretation rather than arithmetic: what a significant F does and does not license, how hypotheses are worded, which analysis fits which design, reading a confidence interval for a difference in proportions, and what a p-value means. No answer key was printed with it, so the key here was derived and every number verified. Question 11 had a sphericity output image that could not be read, so that table is rebuilt.","theme":0,"icon":"📗","group":"paper","questions":[{"n":1,"q":"A psychologist investigates whether three different interventions (mindfulness, cognitive behavioural therapy, and a control condition) influence anxiety scores. A one-way ANOVA produces a statistically significant F-ratio. What can the psychologist conclude?","o":["The null hypothesis is rejected so all three population means must differ significantly from one another.","The null hypothesis is rejected and at least one population mean differs from another.","The null hypothesis is rejected because the F-ratio is significant for every pair of groups.","The null hypothesis is rejected and we do not need further post-hoc testing."],"a":1,"pct":75,"typ":"anova_conclude","theme":0,"spot":"HINT. A significant F and the words \"what can you conclude\". No calculation. Scan the options for over-claiming: \"all\", \"every pair\", \"no further testing\". The correct option after a significant F is always the modest one.","plain":"In plain words: the test has told you the three groups are not all the same. It has not told you which ones differ. \"Not all equal\" is a much weaker statement than \"all different\", and it is the only one you have earned.","work":"Step 1. Write out the null. H0: mu1 = mu2 = mu3, all three population means equal.\nStep 2. Negate it carefully. The opposite of \"all equal\" is \"not all equal\", which means at least one mean differs from at least one other. It is NOT \"all three differ from each other\". One odd group out of three is enough to make the null false.\nStep 3. Match the option. \"At least one population mean differs from another\" is option b.\nStep 4. Sense check with a picture. Suppose mindfulness and CBT both average 20 and the control averages 30. The null is false, F can easily be significant, and yet two of the three means are identical. So \"all three differ\" cannot follow from a significant F.\nStep 5. What comes next. With k = 3 there are m = k(k - 1)/2 = 3 pairs to compare, so post-hoc tests are needed to locate the difference while keeping the family-wise error rate near .05.\nMemory hook: F is a smoke alarm. It tells you there is a fire somewhere, not which room.","wrongs":["This is the classic over-claim. \"Not all equal\" does not mean \"all different\". Two of the three means could be identical and F would still come out significant.","","A one-way ANOVA produces ONE F for the whole set of groups. There is no F per pair, so this reason does not exist. Pairs are compared afterwards, with post-hoc tests.","Backwards. A significant F with 3 groups is exactly when post-hoc testing becomes necessary, because the overall test cannot say which groups differ."],"trap":"Upgrading \"at least one differs\" into \"they all differ\". The ANOVA null is a chain of equals signs, and breaking one link is enough to break the chain.","tri":"Symptom: significant one-way F with k = 3, asking what follows. -&gt; Diagnosis: what rejecting the ANOVA null does and does not license. -&gt; Treatment: conclude that at least one population mean differs from another, then run post-hoc tests to find out which.","src":"Practice set 2, question 1"},{"n":2,"q":"A researcher measures the concentration levels of eight participants under four different background-noise conditions. The researcher performs a repeated-measures ANOVA. What is the appropriate hypothesis for this test?","o":["The alternative hypothesis states that the four population means are equal.","The null hypothesis states that the four population means are equal.","The null hypothesis states that at least 1 population mean is different.","The alternative hypothesis states that at least 3 population means are different."],"a":1,"pct":50,"typ":"rm_hyp","theme":0,"spot":"HINT. A hypothesis-wording question. Each option pairs a LABEL (null or alternative) with a CLAIM (all equal, or some differ). Check the pairing, not the claim alone. The null is always the \"nothing is going on\" claim, however many conditions there are and whether or not the same people are measured twice.","plain":"In plain words: the null says background noise makes no difference, so the four average concentration levels are the same. Everything else is the alternative.","work":"Step 1. Write the null for an ANOVA with k = 4 conditions. H0: mu1 = mu2 = mu3 = mu4. Noise condition makes no difference to mean concentration.\nStep 2. Note what repeated measures does NOT change. The hypotheses are the same as for independent groups. What changes is how the error term and its df are built, (n - 1)(k - 1) with n = persons, not the question being asked.\nStep 3. Match the option. \"The null hypothesis states that the four population means are equal\" is option b.\nStep 4. Write the alternative properly. Ha: at least one of the four population means differs from another. Not \"all four differ\", and not a specific number of them.\nStep 5. Sense check with the df. k = 4 conditions and n = 8 persons gives df model = 3 and df error = (8 - 1)(4 - 1) = 21. A test is being run on four means at once, which only makes sense if the null ties all four together.\nMemory hook: the null is the boring story, and it always comes with equals signs.","wrongs":["Right claim, wrong label. \"All four means are equal\" is the NULL. The alternative is the statement of difference. This option simply swaps the two names over.","","Wrong label again, the other way round. \"At least one mean is different\" is the ALTERNATIVE. A null hypothesis never asserts a difference, because it is the claim you try to knock down.","The label is right but the claim is invented. The alternative says at least ONE mean differs from another. \"At least 3\" is a made-up threshold, and nothing in ANOVA counts how many means differ."],"trap":"Checking whether the claim sounds correct without checking which hypothesis it is pinned to. Two of these options state true things under the wrong name.","tri":"Symptom: which hypothesis belongs to a repeated measures ANOVA. -&gt; Diagnosis: null equals all means equal. -&gt; Treatment: H0: mu1 = mu2 = mu3 = mu4; Ha: not all four are equal.","src":"Practice set 2, question 2"},{"n":3,"q":"A psychologist investigates the effects of therapy type (CBT versus schema therapy) and stress level (low, moderate, and high) on depression scores. Which statement about the analysis is correct?","o":["The two-way ANOVA analysis tests the main effect therapy type and the main effect for stress level. We cannot test for an interaction effect.","The one-way ANOVA analysis produces one overall F-ratio that determines whether both factors and their interaction are significant.","The two-way ANOVA analysis tests the main effect of therapy type, the main effect of stress level, and the interaction.","The independent t-test analysis tests whether therapy type and stress level are correlated so depression scores can be ordinal."],"a":2,"pct":50,"typ":"which_test","theme":0,"spot":"HINT. Count the grouping variables in the stem before you read the options. Two categorical predictors and one continuous outcome means a two-way ANOVA, and a two-way ANOVA always has three effects. Then just find the option that says all three.","plain":"In plain words: two things were varied, therapy type and stress level, so you can ask three questions: does therapy matter, does stress level matter, and does therapy work differently at different stress levels. The third question is the interaction.","work":"Step 1. Name the design. Therapy type has 2 levels, stress level has 3 levels, depression score is the continuous outcome. That is a 2 x 3 between-groups design, so a two-way ANOVA.\nStep 2. List what it tests. Main effect of therapy type. Main effect of stress level. Interaction of therapy type and stress level. Three effects, three F-ratios, all sharing one MS error.\nStep 3. Match the option. Only option c lists all three.\nStep 4. Sense check on why the interaction is the interesting one. CBT might beat schema therapy at low stress and lose at high stress. Looking only at the main effect of therapy would average those two stories together and show nothing. That is exactly what you would miss with option a.\nStep 5. df check for the table. Therapy df = 1, stress df = 2, interaction df = 1 x 2 = 2, error df = N - 6.\nMemory hook: two factors in, three F-ratios out. The interaction is free, and it is usually the point.","wrongs":["The first sentence is right, the second is wrong. With both factors crossed and people in every combination, the interaction is testable, and it is the main reason for running a two-way design instead of two separate one-way ones.","A one-way ANOVA handles ONE factor. It cannot separate two factors, and no single F-ratio can tell you which of three effects is significant. One F-ratio answers one question.","","Wrong on every count. An independent t test compares two means, it does not test correlation between two predictors, and the measurement level of the outcome is not something an analysis decides for you."],"trap":"Counting only the main effects. A crossed two-factor design always carries an interaction, and leaving it out is the mistake the first option is built from.","tri":"Symptom: two categorical factors, one continuous outcome. -&gt; Diagnosis: two-way ANOVA. -&gt; Treatment: it gives three F-ratios, two main effects and the interaction.","src":"Practice set 2, question 3"},{"n":4,"q":"A researcher investigates the relationship between perceived stress and self-esteem in 50 psychology students. Pearson's correlation is (r = -0.60), with (p &lt; .05). Which conclusion is most appropriate?","o":["Higher perceived stress causes lower self-esteem because the correlation is negative.","There is a statistically significant negative linear relationship between perceived stress and self-esteem.","Exactly 60% of the variance in self-esteem is explained by perceived stress.","The negative correlation indicates that the variables do not have a linear relationship."],"a":1,"pct":50,"typ":"corr_interpret","theme":0,"spot":"HINT. A correlation with a p-value and \"which conclusion\". Three traps live in these options every time: causation, r read as a percentage of variance, and a negative sign read as \"no relationship\". Find the option that only says what r and p actually say.","plain":"In plain words: students with more perceived stress tend to have lower self-esteem, the link is fairly strong, and it is unlikely to be a fluke. That is all. Which one causes which is not something a correlation can tell you.","work":"Step 1. Read the sign. r = -0.60 is negative, so as perceived stress goes up, self-esteem tends to go down. Negative means opposite directions, never \"no relationship\".\nStep 2. Read the size. 0.60 is a fairly strong linear association. r squared = 0.36, so 36 per cent of the variance in self-esteem is shared with perceived stress, not 60 per cent.\nStep 3. Read the p-value. p &lt; .05 means a correlation this far from zero would be unlikely if the population correlation really were zero. So reject H0: rho = 0.\nStep 4. Say only what you may. A significant negative linear relationship. Option b.\nStep 5. Why causation is blocked. This is an observational study. The link could run the other way, low self-esteem makes stress feel worse, or a third variable such as workload could drive both. No random assignment, no causal claim.\nMemory hook: r gives you a direction and a strength. Causation needs an experiment, and share of variance needs a squaring.","wrongs":["Correlation read as causation, the most common error in the whole course. The sign of r tells you the direction of the association, not which variable is driving the other. With 50 students measured as they are, no design feature rules out reverse causation or a third variable.","","This reads r as a share of variance. The share is r SQUARED: 0.60 x 0.60 = 0.36, so 36 per cent, and even that is \"shared variance\", not \"explained by\" in a causal sense. The word \"exactly\" is a second warning sign, since this is a sample estimate.","This confuses the sign with the form. A strong linear relationship can run downhill. If the relation were truly non-linear, a Pearson r could be near zero while the variables were still closely connected, which is the opposite situation."],"trap":"Letting the word \"because\" in option a smuggle in causation. An observational correlation, however strong and however significant, cannot tell you what caused what.","tri":"Symptom: a significant correlation and a conclusion to pick. -&gt; Diagnosis: what r and p license. -&gt; Treatment: report direction (negative), form (linear) and significance, and stop there.","src":"Practice set 2, question 4"},{"n":5,"q":"John wants to predict test anxiety from perceived stress using simple linear regression. The correlation between perceived stress and test anxiety is (r = .50). The psychologist then adds sleep quality as a second predictor. The multiple regression model has (R^2 = .40). How much additional variance in test anxiety is explained by adding sleep quality, compared with the model containing perceived stress alone?","o":["15 percentage points.","25 percentage points.","40 percentage points.","65 percentage points."],"a":0,"pct":50,"typ":"r2_change","theme":0,"spot":"HINT. One r, one R squared, and the word \"additional\". That combination always means: square the r first, then subtract. The two numbers in the stem are in different units until you square the correlation.","plain":"In plain words: perceived stress alone already explained part of test anxiety. With sleep quality added the model explains more. The question is the size of the step up.","work":"Step 1. Small model. One predictor, so R squared = r squared = 0.50 x 0.50 = 0.25, that is 25 per cent.\nStep 2. Big model. R squared = 0.40, that is 40 per cent.\nStep 3. Subtract. 0.40 - 0.25 = 0.15, so 15 percentage points. Option a.\nStep 4. Mind the wording. \"Percentage points\" is the right unit for a difference between two percentages. Going from 25 per cent to 40 per cent is a rise of 15 percentage points, which is a 60 per cent increase in relative terms. The paper wants the 15.\nStep 5. Sense check. Adding a predictor can never reduce R squared, so the step must be between 0 and 0.40. 0.15 fits. If you had got a negative number, you subtracted the wrong way round.\nMemory hook: square before you subtract. r is not a share of variance until it has been squared.","wrongs":["","This is r squared, the variance the FIRST model already explained. It is the starting point, not the gain. The subtraction step was skipped.","This is the total R squared of the two-predictor model. It is what both predictors explain together, so it still includes everything perceived stress was already doing.","This added instead of subtracting: 25 + 40 = 65. It also double counts perceived stress, and no model can explain more than 100 per cent, so adding shares like this is never the move."],"trap":"Treating the .50 as if it were already a proportion of variance. Square it to 0.25 first, then take the difference.","tri":"Symptom: r for one predictor, R squared for two, asking for the extra. -&gt; Diagnosis: change in R squared. -&gt; Treatment: 1 R squared for the small model = r squared, 2 subtract it from the R squared of the big model.","src":"Practice set 2, question 5"},{"n":6,"q":"A psychologist investigates whether study preference (studying alone or in a group) is associated with exam outcome (pass or fail). The observed frequencies are: Which statement is correct?","o":["The expected count for students who study alone and pass is 25, and the chi-square test has (df = 1).","The expected count for students who study alone and pass is 30, and the chi-square test has (df = 2).","The expected count for students who study alone and pass is 20, and the chi-square test has (df = 4).","The expected count for students who study alone and pass is 25, and the chi-square test has (df = 10)"],"a":0,"pct":50,"typ":"expected_df","theme":0,"spot":"HINT. Each option pairs an expected count with a df. That means two small calculations, and you can often kill half the options with the easier one. df for a two-by-two table is always 1, so compute that first and only then check the count.","plain":"In plain words: if study preference had nothing to do with passing, how many of the 40 students who study alone would you expect to pass? And how many free choices does a two-by-two table really have?","work":"Step 1. Totals. Study alone = 40, study in a group = 40. Pass column = 30 + 20 = 50, fail column = 10 + 20 = 30. Grand total = 80. Check: 50 + 30 = 80 and 40 + 40 = 80.\nStep 2. Expected count for \"study alone and pass\". Row total x column total / grand total = 40 x 50 / 80 = 2000 / 80 = 25.\nStep 3. Degrees of freedom. The table has r = 2 rows and c = 2 columns, so df = (2 - 1)(2 - 1) = 1. Not 80 - 1, and nothing to do with the sample size. Once you fix the margins, filling in ONE cell fills in the other three, and that is literally what the 1 counts.\nStep 4. Match. Expected 25 and df = 1 is option a.\nStep 5. Sense check. Overall 50 out of 80, that is 62.5 per cent, passed. Apply that rate to the 40 who study alone: 0.625 x 40 = 25. Same answer by a different route, which is a good habit for expected counts.\nStep 6. Finish the test, since you are already here. The other expected counts are 15, 25 and 15. Chi-square = 5.333 with df = 1, and p = .021, so you would reject the null at .05 and conclude that study preference and exam outcome are associated.\nMemory hook: expected = row times column over total. df = (rows - 1)(columns - 1).","wrongs":["","The 30 is the OBSERVED count in that cell, read straight off the table instead of computed, and df = 2 comes from treating the number of categories as the df, as in a goodness of fit test. Both halves are wrong.","The 20 is another observed count, from the wrong row, and df = 4 counts the CELLS of the table. Cells are not degrees of freedom; with the margins fixed only one cell is free.","The expected count is right, so this option is close. df = 10 is invented: no formula in the chi-square family produces it from a two-by-two table. When two options share a value, the other half of the option is where the question is decided."],"trap":"Reading the expected count off the table. Expected counts are never observed data; they are computed from the margins under the null.","tri":"Symptom: a two-by-two table of counts, with expected count and df on offer. -&gt; Diagnosis: chi-square test of independence. -&gt; Treatment: expected = row total x column total / grand total, and df = (r - 1)(c - 1).","src":"Practice set 2, question 6","out":{"cap":"Observed frequencies","h":["","Pass","Fail","Total"],"r":[["Study alone","30","10","40"],["Study in a group","20","20","40"]]}},{"n":7,"q":"A researcher compares the proportion of participants who report experiencing panic attacks in two independent groups: Group A: 40 out of 100 participants. Group B: 60 out of 100 participants. The researcher calculates a 95% confidence interval for the difference between the population proportions. The interval is approximately ([-0.33,-0.07]). Which interpretation is correct?","o":["The sample proportions are equal and they do not differ significantly because the confidence interval contains zero.","The population proportion in Group A is estimated to be between 7% and 33% higher than that in Group B.","Because zero is not included, the difference is statistically significant at the corresponding 95% level.","There is a 5% probability that the observed sample proportions lie between -0.33 and -0.07."],"a":2,"pct":75,"typ":"ci2p_interpret","theme":0,"spot":"HINT. An interval for a DIFFERENCE and the word \"interpretation\". Do two things before reading the options: check whether zero is inside, and check the sign of the interval. Those two facts answer almost every question of this type.","plain":"In plain words: the whole plausible range for the gap between the two groups sits below zero. So Group A really does report fewer panic attacks than Group B, and \"no difference\" is not in the plausible range.","work":"Step 1. Rebuild the interval so you trust it. p-hat A = 40 / 100 = 0.40, p-hat B = 60 / 100 = 0.60, difference A minus B = -0.20. SE = the square root of (0.40 x 0.60 / 100 + 0.60 x 0.40 / 100) = the square root of 0.0048 = 0.0693. Margin = 1.96 x 0.0693 = 0.136. Interval = -0.20 plus or minus 0.136, so -0.336 to -0.064, which is the printed -0.33 to -0.07 to about one hundredth.\nStep 2. Check for zero. Both ends are negative, so zero is not in the interval.\nStep 3. Translate that. A 95 per cent interval holds the values of the difference that the data find plausible. Zero is not among them, so \"no difference\" is ruled out at the 5 per cent level. The difference is statistically significant. Option c.\nStep 4. Read the direction. The interval is entirely below zero and the difference was computed as A minus B, so Group A has the LOWER proportion, by somewhere between about 6 and 34 percentage points.\nStep 5. Sense check with a test. Pooled p = 100 / 200 = 0.50, pooled SE = 0.0707, z = -0.20 / 0.0707 = -2.83, p = .005. Significant, which agrees with the interval.\nMemory hook: for a DIFFERENCE, zero is the boring value. Zero outside means something is going on. For a RATIO you would look at one instead.","wrongs":["Two errors. The interval does not contain zero, it runs from -0.33 to -0.07, both negative. And the sample proportions are plainly not equal, 0.40 against 0.60. This option is picked by reading a negative interval as if it straddled zero.","The direction is backwards. The interval was computed as A minus B and it is negative, so Group A is LOWER than Group B, not higher. If you ever flip the subtraction, flip the signs of both ends with it.","","This misreads what the 95 per cent refers to. The confidence level is about the long-run performance of the PROCEDURE for capturing the unknown population difference. It is not a probability statement about the sample values you already observed, and the 5 per cent is not attached to the inside of the interval."],"trap":"Checking only whether the numbers look big, instead of asking whether zero is inside. Zero in means not significant, zero out means significant.","tri":"Symptom: a confidence interval for a difference in proportions, asking for the interpretation. -&gt; Diagnosis: does the interval contain zero, and which way does it point. -&gt; Treatment: zero excluded means a significant difference at the matching level; the sign tells you the direction.","src":"Practice set 2, question 7"},{"n":8,"q":"N = 120 participants were asked to identify the dominant emotion in an ambiguous facial expression. Each participant selects one of four categories: happiness, sadness, anger, or fear. The researcher wants to test whether all four responses are equally likely in the population. Which statistical test and hypothesis are appropriate?","o":["A chi-square test of independence, with the null hypothesis that emotion and response time are independent.","A one-way ANOVA, with the null hypothesis that the mean frequency of each emotion is equal.","A Pearson correlation, with the null hypothesis that the correlation between emotion categories and participant responses is zero.","A chi-square goodness-of-fit test, with the null hypothesis that each category has a population proportion of .25."],"a":3,"pct":50,"typ":"gof_which","theme":0,"spot":"HINT. Count the variables. ONE categorical variable with several categories and a claim about how the counts should be spread means goodness of fit. TWO categorical variables crossed in a table means a test of independence. That one count decides the whole question.","plain":"In plain words: there is only one thing being measured here, which emotion each person picked. The claim to be tested is that the four options are chosen equally often, so a quarter each.","work":"Step 1. Count the variables. One: the chosen emotion, with four categories. Nothing is crossed with anything, so no two-way table exists.\nStep 2. Name the test. One categorical variable compared against a claimed set of proportions is a chi-square goodness of fit test.\nStep 3. Write the null in proportions. Equally likely with 4 categories means each proportion is 1/4 = .25. So H0: p1 = p2 = p3 = p4 = .25. That is the wording in option d.\nStep 4. Expected counts and df. Expected = 120 x .25 = 30 for every category. Check: 4 x 30 = 120. df = number of categories - 1 = 3. Note df does not involve the 120.\nStep 5. Sense check on what would follow. You would add (observed - 30) squared / 30 across the four categories and compare with the critical value for df = 3 at .05, which is 7.81.\nMemory hook: ONE variable against a claim is goodness of FIT. TWO variables in a grid is INDEPENDENCE.","wrongs":["Right family, wrong member, and the null names a variable that was never measured. Response time does not appear in the study. Independence needs TWO categorical variables, and here there is one.","ANOVA compares MEANS of a numeric outcome across groups. Here the data are counts of people in categories, and there is no numeric score to average. \"Mean frequency of each emotion\" is not a quantity this design produces.","A Pearson correlation needs two numeric variables. Emotion category is nominal, with no order and no spacing, so r cannot be computed on it in any meaningful way.",""],"trap":"Reaching for the test of independence because the word chi-square triggers the idea of a table. Count the variables first: one variable means goodness of fit.","tri":"Symptom: one categorical variable, counts across categories, a claim of equal likelihood. -&gt; Diagnosis: chi-square goodness of fit. -&gt; Treatment: null gives each category a proportion of 1/4 = .25, expected = 120 / 4 = 30 per category, df = 4 - 1 = 3.","src":"Practice set 2, question 8"},{"n":9,"q":"A psychologist conducts a two-way ANOVA to investigate the effects of treatment type (three levels) and participant age group (four levels) on memory scores. There are 48 participants, with equal numbers assigned to each of the 12 combinations of conditions. What are the degrees of freedom for the interaction between treatment type and age group, and for the residual error?","o":["Interaction: (df = 6); residual error: (df = 36).","Interaction: (df = 7); residual error: (df = 36).","Interaction: (df = 6); residual error: (df = 47).","Interaction: (df = 12); residual error: (df = 35)."],"a":0,"pct":75,"typ":"df_interaction","theme":0,"spot":"HINT. Levels of two factors and a total sample size given, two df wanted. This is pure bookkeeping, so write the design down as a x b first. Interaction df is a product of the two \"minus one\" counts; error df is a subtraction from N.","plain":"In plain words: how many independent pieces of information go into testing whether treatment works differently for different age groups, and how many are left over for measuring plain noise.","work":"Step 1. Write the design. a = 3 treatment types, b = 4 age groups, so ab = 12 cells. N = 48 people, which is 48 / 12 = 4 per cell.\nStep 2. Interaction df. (a - 1)(b - 1) = (3 - 1)(4 - 1) = 2 x 3 = 6. Multiply, never add.\nStep 3. Error df. N - ab = 48 - 12 = 36. Each cell contributes n - 1 = 3 df, and 12 cells x 3 = 36. Same number by a second route.\nStep 4. Match. Interaction 6, error 36. Option a.\nStep 5. Sense check on the whole table. df total = N - 1 = 47. Rows: treatment 2, age 3, interaction 6, error 36. 2 + 3 + 6 + 36 = 47. It balances, so the two numbers are right.\nMemory hook: interaction df MULTIPLY, error df SUBTRACTS from N. Then check the rows add up to N - 1.","wrongs":["","The error df is right but the interaction df is wrong. 7 is 12 - 5, or 6 plus one, and neither is a df rule. Interaction df is (a - 1)(b - 1) = 6.","The interaction df is right but 47 is df TOTAL, N - 1. The error row is what is left after every effect has taken its share: 47 - 2 - 3 - 6 = 36.","This used the number of CELLS, 12, as the interaction df, and then N - 1 - 12 = 35 for the error. Cells are not degrees of freedom. The 12 cells carry 11 df between them, and those 11 split as 2 + 3 + 6."],"trap":"Using ab = 12 as the interaction df. The interaction df is (a - 1)(b - 1) = 6; the 12 is how many cells there are, and it belongs in the error df as N - ab.","tri":"Symptom: a two-way design with a levels, b levels and N given, asking for interaction and error df. -&gt; Diagnosis: two-way ANOVA df. -&gt; Treatment: interaction df = (a - 1)(b - 1); error df = N - ab.","src":"Practice set 2, question 9"},{"n":10,"q":"A researcher tests whether a short mindfulness exercise changes mean stress scores of participants. The null hypothesis states that the population mean stress score is the same before and after the exercise. The statistical test produces (p = .03). Which statement is correct at (α = .05)?","o":["There is a 3% probability that the H0 is true and 97% probability that Ha is true.","The H0 must be true because the p-value is less than .05 and we can reject Ha.","The H0 is rejected because the result is statistically significant, but this does not tell us how large the change is.","The H0 is rejected so the intervention has no effect on mean stress scores."],"a":2,"pct":50,"typ":"pvalue_mean","theme":0,"spot":"HINT. A p-value and an alpha, with four interpretations. Two things are being tested at once: can you compare p with alpha, and do you know what the p-value is a probability OF. The right option is usually the careful one that also says what the test does NOT tell you.","plain":"In plain words: the result is unlikely enough under \"no change\" that you stop believing \"no change\". But a p-value says nothing about how big the change is, only that it is probably not zero.","work":"Step 1. Make the decision. p = .03 is smaller than alpha = .05, so reject H0. Decision rule: small p, reject.\nStep 2. Say what p is a probability of. It is the probability of getting a result at least this extreme IF the null were true. It is not the probability that the null is true. The null is either true or not; it does not have a probability in this framework.\nStep 3. Say what significance leaves open. With a large enough sample, a change of half a point on a stress scale can come out significant. So significance tells you a change is probably there, not that it matters. For size you need the mean difference itself, a confidence interval around it, or an effect size.\nStep 4. Match. Reject H0, and it does not tell us how large the change is. Option c.\nStep 5. Sense check on how to report it properly. \"Stress scores dropped significantly, p = .03, with a mean decrease of X points, 95 per cent CI from A to B.\" The interval does the job the p-value cannot.\nMemory hook: p answers \"is something there\", not \"how big is it\" and not \"how likely is my hypothesis\".","wrongs":["This is the most common misreading of a p-value. p = .03 is the probability of data this extreme GIVEN that H0 is true. It is not the probability that H0 is true, and nothing here gives Ha a 97 per cent probability.","Self-contradictory. A p-value below .05 is evidence AGAINST H0, so it cannot be a reason that H0 must be true. You reject H0, not Ha.","","The decision is right and the conclusion is the exact opposite of what it means. Rejecting the null of \"no change\" means there probably IS an effect. This option is picked by matching the words \"null hypothesis\" with \"no effect\" without tracking the rejection."],"trap":"Turning p into the probability that the null is true. It is the probability of the data given the null, and those two are not interchangeable.","tri":"Symptom: a p-value, an alpha, and interpretations on offer. -&gt; Diagnosis: what a significant p-value does and does not mean. -&gt; Treatment: p = .03 &lt; .05, so reject H0; significance is about existence, not size.","src":"Practice set 2, question 10"},{"n":11,"q":"When conducting a repeated-measures ANOVA, researchers also use Mauchley’s test for sphericity. Based on this output, what can we conclude about the assumption of sphericity?","o":["The sphericity assumption is violated so we have to use the Greenhouse-Geisser correction.","The sphericity assumption is not violated and we can assume variances are equal.","The sphericity assumption is not violated and we have to use the Huynh-Feldt correction.","The sphericity assumption is violated so we cannot assume variances are equal."],"a":1,"pct":50,"typ":"sphericity","theme":0,"spot":"HINT. The words \"Mauchly\" and \"sphericity\" with an output to read. Mauchly is an ASSUMPTION check, so the logic runs backwards from a normal test: a significant result is bad news and a non-significant result means you may carry on as normal. Go straight to the Sig. column.","plain":"In plain words: repeated measures ANOVA assumes the differences between every pair of conditions are about equally variable. Mauchly checks that. Here it finds nothing wrong, so no correction is needed.","work":"Step 1. Read the output. Sig. = .216.\nStep 2. Compare with .05. .216 is larger, so do not reject the null of the Mauchly test. The null of THIS test is that sphericity holds, which is the opposite arrangement from the tests you are used to. Here, failing to reject is the good outcome.\nStep 3. Draw the conclusion. Sphericity is not violated, so the variances of the differences can be treated as equal and the ordinary \"sphericity assumed\" row of the ANOVA table is the one to report. No correction. Option b.\nStep 4. The elimination argument, which is worth more than the table. Look at the four options as two pairs. Options a and d both begin \"the assumption is violated\", and both of their second halves are true statements about a violation: you would use Greenhouse-Geisser, and you could not assume equal variances. If the output were significant, a and d would both be correct, and a multiple-choice question cannot have two right answers. So the output must be non-significant. That leaves b and c, and c contradicts itself by applying a correction after saying none is needed. Only b survives.\nStep 5. Keep the trick. When two options would both be true under one reading of an output, that reading is the wrong one. This works even when you cannot see the output.\nMemory hook: Mauchly significant means trouble. Non-significant means carry on. Corrections exist for the trouble case only.","wrongs":["This reads the output as significant. Sig. = .216 is above .05, so there is nothing to correct. Greenhouse-Geisser is the right correction for the wrong situation.","","Self-contradictory, and that is why it can be eliminated without reading the output at all. If sphericity is not violated there is nothing for the Huynh-Feldt correction to fix. Corrections only follow a violation.","Same misreading as option a, with the consequence stated instead of the fix. And note that a and d say essentially the same thing, which is the clue that \"violated\" cannot be the right reading."],"trap":"Reading Mauchly like an ordinary test, where significant means good news. It is an assumption check: a small p-value here means your assumption has FAILED.","tri":"Symptom: a Mauchly output and a question about sphericity. -&gt; Diagnosis: assumption check, read the Sig. value. -&gt; Treatment: Sig. above .05 means sphericity holds and you read the uncorrected row; Sig. below .05 means it is violated and you use Greenhouse-Geisser or Huynh-Feldt.","src":"Practice set 2, question 11","recon":"Reconstructed: the SPSS output was an image the text could not read, so I rebuilt it as a non-significant Mauchly test (W = .812, approximate chi-square = 3.06, df = 2, Sig. = .216, and chi-square 3.06 on df = 2 does give p = .216). A non-significant result is forced by the options: if sphericity were violated, options a and d would both be correct, and a multiple-choice question cannot have two right answers.","out":{"cap":"Reconstructed SPSS output: Mauchly's Test of Sphericity","h":["Within-subjects effect","Mauchly's W","Approx. chi-square","df","Sig."],"r":[["Condition",".812","3.06","2",".216"]]}},{"n":12,"q":"Which of the following statements are true about outliers in correlation analyses?","o":["An outlier can only strengthen the correlation coefficient.","An outlier can only weaken the correlation coefficient.","An outlier can both weaken or strengthen the correlation coefficient.","Correlation coefficients are generally resistant to outliers."],"a":2,"pct":50,"typ":"outliers","theme":0,"spot":"HINT. Three options contain the word \"only\" or \"resistant\", and one allows both directions. With outliers and correlation, the honest answer is almost always \"it depends where the outlier sits\", so be suspicious of any option that forbids one direction.","plain":"In plain words: one odd point can drag the line towards itself. If it sits along the trend it makes the pattern look tighter. If it sits off to the side it makes the pattern look like a mess. Both are possible.","work":"Step 1. See how one point can strengthen r. Take a shapeless blob of points with r close to 0, then add one point far away up and to the right. Now the cloud plus that point looks like a rising line, and r jumps up. The outlier created a trend that was not really there.\nStep 2. See how one point can weaken r. Take points sitting almost perfectly on a rising line, then add one point far away in the bottom right. The tidy pattern is broken and r falls towards 0, or even changes sign.\nStep 3. Conclude. Both are possible, so option c.\nStep 4. Why r is so vulnerable. r is built from products of deviations from the means. A point far from both means contributes a huge product, so it can outweigh many ordinary points on its own. The bigger the leverage, the fewer points it takes to move r.\nStep 5. What to do about it in practice. Look at the scatterplot before you trust any r. If one point is driving the result, say so, and report r with and without it.\nMemory hook: one stubborn point can invent a relationship or destroy one. r never resists, it reacts.","wrongs":["Half the story. An outlier along the direction of the trend does inflate r, but one sitting against the trend pulls r down. \"Only\" is what makes this wrong.","The other half of the same story. Outliers are often met as the thing that spoils a correlation, which is why this option feels right. It still forbids a real possibility.","","The opposite of the truth. The MEDIAN is resistant to outliers. Means, standard deviations, correlations and regression slopes are all built from squared or product terms, which makes them sensitive by construction."],"trap":"Assuming an outlier must spoil a correlation. It can just as easily manufacture one, which is the more dangerous case because the result looks good.","tri":"Symptom: outliers and the correlation coefficient. -&gt; Diagnosis: r is highly sensitive to single extreme points, in either direction. -&gt; Treatment: say an outlier can raise or lower r depending on its position, and always look at the scatterplot.","src":"Practice set 2, question 12"},{"n":13,"q":"Bruno performed a multiple regression analysis for a data set with 3 different predictors. The F-test resulted in a P-value of 0.03. What does this mean?","o":["none of the slopes in the model are equal to zero.","at least one of the slopes in the model do not equal zero.","all of the slopes in the model are not equal to zero.","none of the slopes in the model are significant."],"a":1,"pct":50,"typ":"overall_f","theme":0,"spot":"HINT. The OVERALL F-test of a multiple regression, with a p-value, and options that differ only in how much they claim. The overall F is a single test on all the predictors at once, so it can only ever deliver \"at least one\". Pick the modest option.","plain":"In plain words: the model as a whole explains more than nothing. Somewhere among the three predictors there is at least one real slope. Which one it is, the F-test does not say.","work":"Step 1. Write the null of the overall F. H0: beta1 = beta2 = beta3 = 0. None of the three predictors helps. Ha: at least one beta is not 0.\nStep 2. Decide. p = .03 is below .05, so reject H0.\nStep 3. State exactly what that buys you. The negation of \"all three slopes are zero\" is \"at least one is not zero\". Option b. It is not \"all three are non-zero\", any more than a significant one-way F means all group means differ.\nStep 4. Say what comes next. Each predictor has its own t test, t = b / SE(b), on df = n - p - 1 = n - 4. Those t tests tell you which predictors are carrying the model. It is quite normal to have a significant F with only one significant t.\nStep 5. Sense check on the mirror-image case. A non-significant F does not prove every slope is exactly zero either. It just means the model has not shown its worth.\nMemory hook: the overall F is a doorbell for the whole model. It says someone is there, not who.","wrongs":["This is \"all slopes are non-zero\" in disguise: \"none are equal to zero\" means every single one differs from zero. The overall F cannot license that. One strong predictor out of three can make F significant on its own.","","Same over-claim, stated directly. The null being rejected is the conjunction \"all are zero\", and breaking a conjunction only needs one counter-example. Individual t tests are the only way to say which slopes are non-zero.","The opposite of the result. p = .03 is significant, so the model as a whole does pass. This option reads a small p-value as a failure."],"trap":"Upgrading \"at least one slope is not zero\" into \"all the predictors matter\". The overall F tests the model as a package and never names a member of it.","tri":"Symptom: a significant overall F in multiple regression. -&gt; Diagnosis: what the overall F tests. -&gt; Treatment: reject H0 that all slopes are zero, so at least one slope is not zero. Individual t tests say which.","src":"Practice set 2, question 13"},{"n":14,"q":"Which of the following is not a characteristic associated with a chi-square research design?","o":["within-subjects","between-groups","categorical variables","none of the above"],"a":0,"pct":50,"typ":"chi_design","theme":0,"spot":"HINT. The word \"not\" in the stem. These questions are won by answering the POSITIVE question first: list the features a chi-square design does have, then the odd one out is whatever is left. Also note that \"none of the above\" here would mean all three features fit.","plain":"In plain words: a chi-square test counts separate people in separate boxes. Each person appears once. So a design where the same people are measured again and again is not what chi-square is for.","work":"Step 1. List what a chi-square design does need. Variables measured in categories, counts rather than scores, and each person contributing to exactly one cell. That last point is the independence requirement.\nStep 2. Check the options one by one. Between-groups fits: different people in different categories. Categorical variables fits: that is the whole point of counting.\nStep 3. Check within-subjects. It means the same people are measured more than once, so their observations are linked. That breaks the independence of the cells, because one person would land in two cells. So within-subjects is the feature that does not belong. Option a.\nStep 4. Handle the \"none of the above\" option. It would only be right if all three features fitted a chi-square design. One of them clearly does not, so it is wrong here.\nStep 5. Useful footnote. There IS a chi-square style test for paired categorical data, the McNemar test, which exists precisely because the ordinary chi-square cannot handle repeated measurements of the same people.\nMemory hook: chi-square counts PEOPLE IN BOXES, one person one box. Repeated measures puts one person in several boxes, which is why it does not fit.","wrongs":["","Between-groups is a genuine feature of a chi-square design: separate, independent people in each category. The question asks for the feature that does NOT belong, so this is the wrong direction.","Categorical variables are the defining feature of chi-square. Both variables are made of categories and the data are counts. This one fits best of all.","\"None of the above\" would mean every listed feature belongs to a chi-square design. Within-subjects does not, so this cannot be right. Save this option for when you have ruled the others out."],"trap":"Losing track of the word \"not\" and picking the feature that fits best. Answer the positive question first, then take the leftover.","tri":"Symptom: which feature does NOT belong to a chi-square design. -&gt; Diagnosis: chi-square needs independent cases and categorical variables. -&gt; Treatment: within-subjects is the feature that does not fit, because it means repeated measurements of the same people.","src":"Practice set 2, question 14"}]},{"id":"mock","name":"Final mock exam 2026","short":"Final mock exam 2026","blurb":"The capstone paper. Forty questions, in the order last year moved through the course: confidence intervals for proportions, then chi-square, then correlation and regression, then inference for regression, then one-way ANOVA, then two factors at once, then repeated measures, with the design, power and ethics items dropped in among the later themes exactly as the real paper does. Seventeen of the forty are calculations, the same share as last year. Sit it in one go, in order, before you look at a single explanation. Then read the explanations for everything you got wrong AND for everything you guessed.","theme":0,"icon":"🏁","group":"paper","questions":[{"n":1,"q":"Marisol Ibarra compares two museum branches. With the shop by the exit, 126 of 280 visitors bought something; by the entrance, 102 of 260. Her 95 per cent interval for the difference in proportions, exit minus entrance, runs from -0.025 to 0.141. What may she conclude?","o":["The exit layout sells more, because most of the interval lies above zero.","The two proportions are equal, because zero sits inside the interval.","The data do not establish a difference, because the interval covers zero.","For 95 per cent of visitors the exit sells 2.5 points less to 14.1 points more, as 95 per cent are covered."],"a":2,"pct":75,"typ":"ci2p_interpret","theme":0,"spot":"HINT. An interval for a DIFFERENCE is already printed and you are asked what it means. Do not recompute anything. Ask one question only: does the interval contain zero? Zero means no difference, so an interval that reaches across zero has not ruled no difference out.","plain":"In plain words: the interval is the set of differences that would not surprise her. It runs from a small advantage for the entrance branch to a bigger advantage for the exit branch, and passes straight through nought on the way. So she cannot even say which branch is ahead.","work":"Step 1. Find the test value. For a DIFFERENCE between two proportions the value that means \"no difference\" is 0.\nStep 2. Look at the two ends of the interval. The lower end is -0.025, the upper end is 0.141.\nStep 3. Ask whether 0 lies between them. It does, because -0.025 is below 0 and 0.141 is above it.\nStep 4. Translate. The plausible differences run from the entrance branch being 2.5 percentage points ahead to the exit branch being 14.1 percentage points ahead. Nought is among them.\nStep 5. State the verdict in the careful form: the study does not show a difference. That is not the same as showing there is none.\nMemory hook: zero inside, nothing decided. Zero outside, you have a direction.\nExplain it to a friend: the interval is a list of answers she cannot rule out, and \"no difference at all\" is still on that list.","wrongs":["This is the one-sided reading of a two-sided interval. It is true that more of the printed interval sits above zero than below it, but a confidence interval is not a vote and you do not count how much of it falls on each side. As soon as zero is inside, neither branch can be called ahead.","An interval that contains zero does not say the difference IS zero. It says zero is one of the values still compatible with the data, along with 0.10 and 0.14. Absence of evidence for a difference is not evidence of equality.","","This reads a statement about one difference between two percentages as if it described individual visitors. The interval is about the gap between the two population proportions, not about the range of behaviour of 95 per cent of shoppers. The 95 per cent describes how often the method captures the true difference."],"trap":"Looking at where the middle of the interval sits instead of asking whether zero is inside. The centre, 0.058, is positive and tempting. It is not the verdict.","tri":"Symptom: a confidence interval for p1 - p2 is printed, and the question asks for a verdict. -&gt; Diagnosis: reading a CI for a difference. -&gt; Treatment: check whether zero is inside. Zero inside means you do not reject the hypothesis of no difference. Zero outside, and entirely on one side, means you do."},{"n":2,"q":"Ewan Macleod has records from 140 primary schools. Library visits per pupil per year correlate .52 with average reading score. His press office wants to write that visits raise scores. What should he tell them?","o":["The claim goes beyond his data: reading could just as easily drive the visits.","The claim is safe, since .52 across 140 schools can be read as an effect.","The claim is safe, since with 140 schools .52 is significant beyond .05, ruling chance out.","The claim is safe once squared, since .27 of reading variation is then down to visits."],"a":0,"pct":50,"typ":"causation","theme":0,"spot":"HINT. A correlation between two things nobody assigned, followed by a sentence with a verb like raises, boosts, improves or causes. That is the causation question. The answer is never about how big r is.","plain":"In plain words: he watched what schools already do. He did not send some schools to the library and keep others away. So he cannot say which way the arrow points, or whether something else is behind both.","work":"Step 1. Ask what the design was. He read off existing records. Nobody allocated library visits.\nStep 2. Write down the reverse-direction story. A school whose pupils already read well may enjoy library trips more and so go more often. The correlation would look exactly the same.\nStep 3. Write down the third-variable story. A school with a generous budget can afford both the coach to the library and smaller classes. Again the same correlation appears, with the budget doing the work.\nStep 4. Notice that no value of r distinguishes these stories from the causal one. A correlation of .52 and a correlation of .92 are equally silent about cause.\nStep 5. Say what would settle it: assign the visits at random, so that the two kinds of school differ only by the toss of a coin.\nMemory hook: the size of r tells you HOW CLOSELY two things move together. The DESIGN tells you whether one moves the other. Those are separate questions.","wrongs":["","A big sample and a strong correlation make the correlation itself precise. Neither of them tells you which variable moves which, so neither of them licenses the word effect. A perfectly measured correlation from 140,000 schools would still be a correlation.","Significance answers the question \"could this correlation have come out of a population where there is none?\" It never answers \"what produced it?\" Chance is not the only rival explanation on offer: ruling out chance and ruling out confounding are two different jobs.","Squaring r changes the units of the claim, not its logic. R squared of .27 says that 27 per cent of the variation in reading goes with variation in visits. Goes with, not responsible for, and not caused by."],"trap":"Letting the strength of r stand in for the strength of the design. Examiners build these options so that every wrong one is about the number and the right one is about how the data were collected.","tri":"Symptom: a correlation from observed data, plus a causal word in the proposed conclusion. -&gt; Diagnosis: correlation read as causation. -&gt; Treatment: name the two alternatives that an observational design cannot rule out. One, reverse direction: maybe good readers get taken to the library more. Two, a third variable: maybe well-resourced schools do both. Only random assignment removes these."},{"n":3,"q":"Teodor Marinescu checks food-waste sorting in two Romanian towns. In the first, 180 of 300 households sorted correctly; in the second, 125 of 250. Build a 95 per cent interval for the difference in proportions, first minus second. (3 decimals)","o":["0.030 to 0.170","0.017 to 0.183","0.045 to 0.155","-0.017 to 0.217"],"a":1,"pct":50,"typ":"ci2p_calc","theme":0,"spot":"HINT. Two separate groups, a count of successes in each, and the word difference. That is the two-proportion interval. You need three things: the two sample proportions, one standard error that adds BOTH groups in, and z* = 1.96.","plain":"In plain words: work out the percentage in each town, subtract, then put a cushion either side of that gap. The cushion has to allow for the wobble in both towns at once, so both towns contribute to it.","work":"Step 1. The two proportions. 180/300 = 0.60 and 125/250 = 0.50.\nStep 2. The difference. 0.60 - 0.50 = 0.10.\nStep 3. The standard error, adding one term per town. 0.60 x 0.40 / 300 = 0.000800 and 0.50 x 0.50 / 250 = 0.001000. These add to 0.001800.\nStep 4. Take the square root of the SUM, not the sum of the roots. sqrt(0.001800) = 0.042426.\nStep 5. The margin of error. 1.96 x 0.042426 = 0.083156, call it 0.083.\nStep 6. The interval. 0.10 - 0.083 = 0.017 and 0.10 + 0.083 = 0.183. So 0.017 to 0.183.\nStep 7. Read it back. Zero is NOT in the interval, so the first town really does sort better, by somewhere between 1.7 and 18.3 percentage points.\nMemory hook: add the variances, then one square root over the lot. Think of it as \"square, add, root\", never \"root, add\".","wrongs":["This uses z* = 1.645, the 90 per cent multiplier, in a 95 per cent interval. 0.10 plus and minus 1.645 x 0.042426 = 0.10 plus and minus 0.070. For 95 per cent the multiplier is 1.96. Learn the pair: 1.645 for 90, 1.96 for 95.","","This builds the standard error from the first town only: sqrt(0.60 x 0.40 / 300) = 0.028284, giving a margin of 0.055. The second town has sampling wobble too, and it has to be added in before the square root.","This takes the square root of each term first and then adds: 0.028284 + 0.031623 = 0.059907, giving a margin of 0.117. Standard errors do not add. Variances add, and the root comes last, which is why the true margin (0.083) is smaller than this one."],"trap":"Rooting too early. sqrt(A) + sqrt(B) is always bigger than sqrt(A + B), so this mistake always gives you an interval that is too wide, and here it is wide enough to swallow zero and flip your conclusion.","tri":"Symptom: two independent samples, a count and a total for each, a request for an interval for the difference. -&gt; Diagnosis: confidence interval for p1 - p2. -&gt; Treatment: (p1 - p2) plus and minus z* times sqrt(p1(1-p1)/n1 + p2(1-p2)/n2). For 95 per cent, z* = 1.96. Proportions always use z*, never t*."},{"n":4,"q":"Nadiya Kovalenko asks a random sample of 480 garden visitors whether an online post brought them; 168 say yes. What is the margin of error of her 95 per cent interval for the proportion of all visitors? (3 decimals)","o":["0.022","0.036","0.045","0.043"],"a":3,"pct":50,"typ":"moe","theme":0,"spot":"HINT. One sample, one proportion, and the words margin of error. That is the cushion only, not the whole interval. Do not add it to anything and do not report two numbers.","plain":"In plain words: how far either side of her 35 per cent does she have to reach to be 95 per cent confident of catching the true figure? That distance is the margin of error.","work":"Step 1. The sample proportion. 168/480 = 0.35.\nStep 2. The quantity inside the root. 0.35 x 0.65 = 0.2275, and 0.2275 / 480 = 0.00047396.\nStep 3. The standard error. sqrt(0.00047396) = 0.021771.\nStep 4. The margin of error. 1.96 x 0.021771 = 0.042671, so 0.043.\nStep 5. Sense check. She could report 0.35 plus and minus 0.043, that is 30.7 to 39.3 per cent. Roughly four points either side on a sample of 480 is about right. If you ever get a margin bigger than about 5 points from a sample of 400 or more, go back and look for a dropped square root.\nMemory hook: SE is the step, z* times SE is the stride. The question asked for the stride.","wrongs":["This hands in the standard error, 0.0218, where the margin of error was asked for. The SE is the raw wobble of one sample proportion. The margin of error is the SE stretched by 1.96 so that it covers 95 per cent of the possibilities.","This uses z* = 1.645, which belongs to a 90 per cent interval: 1.645 x 0.021771 = 0.0358. A 95 per cent interval has to be wider, so it uses 1.96.","This replaces the observed 0.35 with 0.5, the value that makes p(1 - p) as big as it can be: sqrt(0.25/480) = 0.022822, and 1.96 x 0.022822 = 0.0447. That is the worst-case margin you use when PLANNING a sample and you have no estimate yet. Here she has her data, so she uses 0.35.",""],"trap":"Reporting the standard error. Read the last four words of the question before you write anything: standard error, margin of error and confidence interval are three different answers to three different questions, and all three are usually on offer in the options.","tri":"Symptom: one proportion, one sample size, the phrase margin of error. -&gt; Diagnosis: margin of error for a single proportion. -&gt; Treatment: p-hat first, then SE = sqrt(p-hat(1 - p-hat)/n), then multiply by z* = 1.96. The SE on its own is NOT the margin of error."},{"n":5,"q":"At a South African driving school 30 per cent of candidates pass first time. Thandiwe Ncube takes a random sample of 150. What is the chance that more than 36 per cent of them passed first time? (3 decimals)","o":["0.054","0.063","0.448","0.946"],"a":0,"pct":50,"typ":"phat_dist","theme":0,"spot":"HINT. A proportion that is known for the whole population, a sample size, and a question about the chance of a sample coming out above or below some figure. That is the sampling distribution of p-hat, not a confidence interval. The giveaway is that the population value is given to you.","plain":"In plain words: if the true pass rate is 30 per cent, samples of 150 will not all come out at exactly 30 per cent. They scatter around it. How often does a sample land above 36 per cent?","work":"Step 1. Write the centre and the spread. Centre p = 0.30. SE = sqrt(0.30 x 0.70 / 150) = sqrt(0.0014) = 0.037417.\nStep 2. Standardise the cut point. z = (0.36 - 0.30) / 0.037417 = 0.06 / 0.037417 = 1.6036.\nStep 3. Decide which tail. The question says MORE than 36 per cent, so you want the area to the RIGHT of z = 1.60.\nStep 4. Look it up. The area to the left of 1.60 is about 0.9456, so the area to the right is 1 - 0.9456 = 0.054.\nStep 5. Sense check. 36 per cent is about one and a half standard errors above the centre, and a bit over 5 per cent of samples sit that far out on one side. That feels right.\nMemory hook: draw the curve, mark the centre at p, shade the side the words ask for, THEN compute. The picture stops you returning the wrong tail.","wrongs":["","This builds the SE from 0.36, the cut point, instead of from 0.30, the population value: sqrt(0.36 x 0.64 / 150) = 0.039192, giving z = 1.53 and a tail of 0.063. The sampling distribution is centred on the POPULATION proportion and its spread is built from the same p. The 0.36 is only the line you are measuring to.","This forgets to divide by n: sqrt(0.30 x 0.70) = 0.458258, giving z = 0.13 and a tail of 0.448. Without the n, this is the spread of a single pass or fail, not of an average over 150 of them. Dividing by n is what makes a sample more stable than one person.","This is the left-hand tail, the area BELOW 36 per cent. The z was right, but the final subtraction from 1 was never done. The question asked for more than 36 per cent, which has to be the smaller of the two pieces because 36 is above the centre."],"trap":"Reading 0.36 as the new centre. Copy p and n out on their own line before you do anything: the centre and the SE both come from p, and the other number is just the cut point.","tri":"Symptom: a population proportion p is given, plus n, plus a probability question about p-hat. -&gt; Diagnosis: Normal approximation to the sampling distribution of p-hat. -&gt; Treatment: the centre is p, the spread is sqrt(p(1 - p)/n). Turn the cut point into z = (p-hat - p) / SE, then look up the tail the question asks for. Build the SE from p, the POPULATION value, not from the cut point."},{"n":6,"q":"Annika Rautio draws a random sample of 1,200 adults from the Finnish population register and records weekly volunteering hours and life satisfaction. Volunteers report higher satisfaction. What conclusion does her design support?","o":["Both kinds, since a random draw makes the comparison causal as well as representative.","Neither kind, since without a treatment a survey can only describe her 1,200 respondents.","She can generalise the association to Finnish adults, but not say volunteering raises satisfaction.","She can say volunteering raises satisfaction, but only within her 1,200 respondents."],"a":2,"pct":50,"typ":"inference_type","theme":0,"spot":"HINT. The stem tells you carefully HOW people got into the study and whether anyone decided who got what. Two different words do two different jobs: random SAMPLING buys you generalisation, random ASSIGNMENT buys you cause. Look for each one separately.","plain":"In plain words: she picked her people fairly out of the whole country, so what she sees in them is a fair picture of the country. But she let people choose for themselves whether to volunteer, so she cannot know whether volunteering lifts mood or cheerful people volunteer.","work":"Step 1. Find the sampling. A random sample of 1,200 drawn from the national register. That is as good as sampling gets, so box one is ticked.\nStep 2. Find the assignment. There is none. People volunteer or they do not, by their own choice. Box two is empty.\nStep 3. Put the two together. A real association, fairly measured, that can be quoted for Finnish adults in general. But the reason behind it is unknown.\nStep 4. Name what could be behind it. Healthier people have the energy to volunteer, so health could cause both. Or satisfied people reach outwards, which is the arrow pointing the other way.\nStep 5. Write the two-part sentence that earns the mark: generalisable, not causal.\nMemory hook: sampling is about WHO is in the study, assignment is about WHAT happens to them. Two questions, two answers, never one.","wrongs":["Random sampling fixes who is in the study. It does nothing about why volunteers and non-volunteers differ, because the people themselves decided which group they were in. A perfectly drawn sample of a self-selected comparison is still a self-selected comparison.","There is plenty to generalise: the association itself. Observational studies on good samples are how most of what we know about populations was found. A random draw from the register is precisely what lets the association be quoted beyond the 1,200 who answered. What is missing is only the causal reading.","","This gets both halves backwards. The random sample is exactly what lets her speak beyond her 1,200 respondents, and the lack of assignment is exactly what blocks the causal claim."],"trap":"Treating \"random\" as one word that buys everything. The exam separates the two randoms on purpose, and the options are built so that you have to notice which one the stem actually gave you.","tri":"Symptom: a study described with detail about sampling and about assignment. -&gt; Diagnosis: which type of inference the design supports. -&gt; Treatment: tick two boxes separately. Box one, random sampling from a defined population: yes means the result generalises to that population. Box two, random assignment to conditions: yes means a causal claim is allowed. Here box one is ticked and box two is not."},{"n":7,"q":"Casper Mortensen gives three teams of 100 drivers a different route-planning app each and records whether every parcel arrived on time. His counts are below. What is the test statistic for an association between app and on-time delivery? (2 decimals)","o":["1.00","4.80","12.91","12.00"],"a":3,"pct":75,"typ":"chi_calc","theme":0,"spot":"HINT. A table of COUNTS, rows and columns both standing for categories, and a question about an association. That is chi-square. Six cells here, so six little calculations and then one sum.","plain":"In plain words: if the app made no difference, each team would have the same on-time rate as the depot overall. Work out what each cell would hold in that world, see how far each real count is from it, and add up the misses.","work":"Step 1. Read the margins off the table. Each row total is 100. The column totals are 180 on time and 120 late. N = 300.\nStep 2. Expected counts. Every row has 100 drivers, so for the on-time column 100 x 180 / 300 = 60, and for the late column 100 x 120 / 300 = 40. Same for all three apps, because the rows are equal.\nStep 3. Cell by cell, (O - E) squared over E.\n   App 1 on time: (72 - 60)^2 / 60 = 144/60 = 2.40\n   App 1 late:    (28 - 40)^2 / 40 = 144/40 = 3.60\n   App 2 on time: (60 - 60)^2 / 60 = 0\n   App 2 late:    (40 - 40)^2 / 40 = 0\n   App 3 on time: (48 - 60)^2 / 60 = 144/60 = 2.40\n   App 3 late:    (52 - 40)^2 / 40 = 144/40 = 3.60\nStep 4. Add them. 2.40 + 3.60 + 0 + 0 + 2.40 + 3.60 = 12.00.\nStep 5. The df, so you know what to compare it with. (3 - 1)(2 - 1) = 2. The critical value for 2 df at .05 is 5.99, so 12.00 is well past it: app and on-time delivery are associated.\nMemory hook: App 2 contributes nothing at all, because it landed exactly on its expected counts. Cells that match expectation add zero, and that is a useful arithmetic check.","wrongs":["This forgets to square the gaps: 12/60 + 12/40 + 0 + 0 + 12/60 + 12/40 = 1.00. Without the squaring, gaps above and below expectation would not even be treated alike, and the statistic would not grow the way chi-square does.","This uses the on-time column only: 2.40 + 0 + 2.40 = 4.80. A chi-square needs every cell in the table. The late column carries the same information in mirror image, and leaving it out throws away half the statistic.","This divides each squared gap by the OBSERVED count instead of the expected one: 144/72 + 144/28 + 0 + 0 + 144/48 + 144/52 = 12.91. The denominator is always E. The expected count is the yardstick; the observed count is what you are measuring against it.",""],"trap":"Rounding the expected counts, or dividing by O. Here the expected counts come out as whole numbers so the rounding trap is hidden, but the O-instead-of-E answer (12.91) sits deliberately close to the right one so that a careless division still looks plausible.","tri":"Symptom: a two-way table of frequencies, a question about association. -&gt; Diagnosis: chi-square test of independence. -&gt; Treatment: for every cell, expected = row total x column total / grand total. Then chi-square = sum of (O - E) squared over E, with df = (r - 1)(c - 1). Never round an expected count on the way.","out":{"cap":"On-time delivery by route-planning app","h":["App","All on time","At least one late","Row total"],"r":[["App 1","72","28","100"],["App 2","60","40","100"],["App 3","48","52","100"],["Column total","180","120","300"]]}},{"n":8,"q":"Agnieszka Lis manages a Polish park with four entrances. Past records split arrivals 40, 30, 20 and 10 per cent. This season she logged 400 hikers, below. What is the test statistic for whether this season matches that split? (2 decimals)","o":["0.40","3.45","3.33","69.20"],"a":1,"pct":50,"typ":"gof_stat","theme":0,"spot":"HINT. ONE row of categories, not a two-way table, and a set of shares the counts are supposed to match. That is goodness of fit. The expected counts are handed to you as percentages: turn each one into a count by multiplying by N.","plain":"In plain words: the park expects 40 per cent at the North gate, so out of 400 hikers it expects 160 there. Do that for all four gates, compare with what actually turned up, and add up the misses.","work":"Step 1. Turn the percentages into expected counts, using N = 400.\n   North 0.40 x 400 = 160, East 0.30 x 400 = 120, South 0.20 x 400 = 80, West 0.10 x 400 = 40. They add to 400, which is the check that you have done it right.\nStep 2. Cell by cell, (O - E) squared over E.\n   North: (148 - 160)^2 / 160 = 144/160 = 0.90\n   East:  (132 - 120)^2 / 120 = 144/120 = 1.20\n   South: (74 - 80)^2 / 80 = 36/80 = 0.45\n   West:  (46 - 40)^2 / 40 = 36/40 = 0.90\nStep 3. Add them. 0.90 + 1.20 + 0.45 + 0.90 = 3.45.\nStep 4. The df. Four categories, so 4 - 1 = 3. The critical value at .05 on 3 df is 7.81, and 3.45 is well short of it, so this season fits the usual pattern.\nStep 5. Sense check. No single gate is dramatically off, and the statistic is small. That hangs together.\nMemory hook: for goodness of fit you SUBTRACT ONE FROM THE NUMBER OF BOXES. The (r - 1)(c - 1) rule belongs to a table with rows AND columns, and this has only one row of boxes.","wrongs":["This forgets to square: 12/160 + 12/120 + 6/80 + 6/40 = 0.40. The squaring is what makes a shortfall and an excess count the same way and what makes a big miss count far more than a small one.","","This divides by the observed count instead of the expected one: 144/148 + 144/132 + 36/74 + 36/46 = 3.33. The answer lands close to the right one here, which is exactly why the mistake survives. The denominator is always E.","This ignores the historical shares and assumes the four gates should be equal, 400/4 = 100 each: 23.04 + 10.24 + 6.76 + 29.16 = 69.20. Equal expected counts are only correct when the question says the categories should be equally likely. Here the stem gives you 40, 30, 20 and 10."],"trap":"Using equal expected counts out of habit. Goodness of fit does NOT mean \"test for an even spread\". It means \"test against the claimed spread\", and here the claim is printed in the right-hand column.","tri":"Symptom: one set of categories, observed counts, and a claimed set of proportions. -&gt; Diagnosis: chi-square goodness of fit. -&gt; Treatment: expected count for each category = N x the claimed proportion. Then sum (O - E) squared over E, with df = number of categories - 1. Note the df rule here is DIFFERENT from the two-way table rule.","out":{"cap":"Hikers logged at each entrance this season","h":["Entrance","Hikers this season","Share expected from past years"],"r":[["North","148","40 per cent"],["East","132","30 per cent"],["South","74","20 per cent"],["West","46","10 per cent"],["Total","400","100 per cent"]]}},{"n":9,"q":"Hiroshi Watanabe has a two by two table: course version by whether students finished. His two-proportion test gives z = -2.35. What will the test for association on the same table give, and on how many degrees of freedom? (2 decimals for the statistic)","o":["5.52 on 1 degree of freedom","2.35 on 1 degree of freedom","4.70 on 1 degree of freedom","5.52 on 3 degrees of freedom"],"a":0,"pct":50,"typ":"chi_z","theme":0,"spot":"HINT. A 2 x 2 table and BOTH tests mentioned, one given and one asked for. That is the z squared question. There is nothing to recompute from counts: the link is arithmetic.","plain":"In plain words: on a two-by-two table the two tests are the same test wearing different clothes. Square the z and you have the chi-square. And a table with two rows and two columns always has one degree of freedom.","work":"Step 1. Square the z. (-2.35) x (-2.35) = 5.5225, so 5.52.\nStep 2. Deal with the sign. A square is never negative, and a chi-square statistic is a sum of squares, so it cannot be negative either. The minus sign simply tells you which version had the lower finishing rate; it does not survive the squaring.\nStep 3. The df. A 2 x 2 table has r = 2 and c = 2, so (2 - 1)(2 - 1) = 1 x 1 = 1.\nStep 4. Check that the two tests agree. A two-sided z of 2.35 has p = .019, and a chi-square of 5.52 on 1 df also has p = .019. Same p value, as it must be.\nStep 5. Note the direction of the equivalence: it holds only for 2 x 2. On a 3 x 2 table there is no single z to square.\nMemory hook: two by two means one df and chi-square = z squared. As soon as the table grows, the shortcut is gone.","wrongs":["","This quotes the z itself with the minus sign dropped. 2.35 is the z, not the chi-square. The two statistics live on different scales: one is roughly a number of standard errors, the other is that number squared.","This doubles 2.35 instead of squaring it. Doubling and squaring happen to be close for numbers near 2, which is why the mistake slips through. 2.35 squared is 5.52, not 4.70.","The statistic is right but the df is wrong. 3 comes from counting the four CELLS and subtracting one, which is the goodness-of-fit rule. For a two-way table the rule is (r - 1)(c - 1), and here that is 1."],"trap":"Using categories minus one for the df of a two-way table. Four cells tempt you into 3. Ask yourself whether the table has rows AND columns: if it does, multiply the two \"one less\" numbers.","tri":"Symptom: a 2 x 2 table, a z from a two-proportion test, a chi-square asked for (or the other way round). -&gt; Diagnosis: chi-square = z squared on a 2 x 2 table. -&gt; Treatment: square the z, ignoring its sign, and set df = (2 - 1)(2 - 1) = 1. To go backwards, take the square root of the chi-square, and remember the z could be either sign."},{"n":10,"q":"Ilaria Bruni sorts 360 walking club members by age band and preferred time of day. Only the totals are printed, below. What is the expected count for ages 40 to 59 preferring the afternoon, and the degrees of freedom? (2 decimals; df a whole number)","o":["30.00 and 6 df","33.44 and 11 df","33.44 and 6 df","33.44 and 5 df"],"a":2,"pct":50,"typ":"expected_df","theme":0,"spot":"HINT. The cells are blank and only the margins are printed. That is always an expected-count question, because the margins are all an expected count needs. If the df is asked for in the same breath, count the rows and the columns and keep the two numbers apart.","plain":"In plain words: if age had nothing to do with the time people prefer, each age band would spread itself across the four times in the same proportions as the club as a whole. How many of the 140 middle-aged members would that put in the afternoon column?","work":"Step 1. Pick the right row and the right column. The row for 40 to 59 has a total of 140. The afternoon column has a total of 86. The grand total is 360.\nStep 2. Apply the formula. 140 x 86 / 360 = 12040 / 360 = 33.4444, so 33.44.\nStep 3. Sanity check it against the logic. The afternoon takes 86/360 = 23.9 per cent of the whole club, and 23.9 per cent of 140 is 33.4. Same answer, arrived at the long way round, which is why the formula works.\nStep 4. The df. Three age bands and four times of day, so (3 - 1)(4 - 1) = 2 x 3 = 6.\nStep 5. Note that expected counts are allowed to be fractions. Nobody is half a person; the expected count is an average over many repeats, not a headcount.\nMemory hook: expected = YOUR ROW times YOUR COLUMN over EVERYBODY. And for the df, minus one, minus one, then multiply.","wrongs":["This splits the grand total evenly over the twelve cells, 360/12 = 30.00. Equal cells would only be expected if both the age bands and the four times of day were equally popular, and the printed margins show they are not.","The count is right but the df comes from counting the twelve cells and taking one away. That is the goodness-of-fit rule. A table with rows and columns uses (r - 1)(c - 1).","","The count is right but the df has been ADDED rather than multiplied: (3 - 1) + (4 - 1) = 5. The formula is a product. Say it out loud as \"two times three\", not \"two and three\"."],"trap":"Rounding the expected count to 33. Expected counts stay as decimals all the way through the chi-square sum, because rounding them early shifts the statistic and can flip a borderline decision.","tri":"Symptom: a two-way table with margins given and cells asked about. -&gt; Diagnosis: expected count, and df, under independence. -&gt; Treatment: expected = row total x column total / grand total. Keep the decimals, never round. df = (r - 1)(c - 1), with r = number of rows and c = number of columns.","out":{"cap":"Row and column totals only: age band by preferred time of day","h":["Age band","Early morning","Late morning","Afternoon","Evening","Row total"],"r":[["Under 40","","","","","115"],["40 to 59","","","","","140"],["60 and over","","","","","105"],["Column total","98","92","86","84","360"]]}},{"n":11,"q":"Dounia Cherif records, for each of 300 Moroccan pupils, which of three after-school clubs they joined and whether they live in the town or outside it. Which pair of hypotheses is correct?","o":["H0: the three clubs are equally popular. H1: they are not equally popular.","H0: every cell has the same mean. H1: at least one cell mean differs.","H0: club choice and where a pupil lives are associated in the population. H1: they are not, as H0 carries her claim.","H0: club choice and where a pupil lives are not associated in the population. H1: they are associated."],"a":3,"pct":50,"typ":"chi_hyp","theme":0,"spot":"HINT. Two categorical variables recorded on the same people and a request for hypotheses. No numbers needed. Check three things in each option: is it about an association (not about means, not about equal popularity), is the NO-association version in H0, and is it about the population rather than the sample.","plain":"In plain words: the dull hypothesis is that where a pupil lives tells you nothing about which club they pick. The interesting one is that it tells you something. The dull one always goes first.","work":"Step 1. Name the two variables. Club choice, with three categories. Where the pupil lives, with two. Both are categories, not measurements.\nStep 2. Decide what the dull hypothesis is. H0 is always the hypothesis of no effect, no difference, no relationship. Here that is: knowing where a pupil lives tells you nothing about the club they choose.\nStep 3. Write it as independence. H0: club choice and residence are independent, that is, not associated, in the population of pupils.\nStep 4. Write H1 as the plain opposite: they are associated. Do NOT add a direction, because the chi-square test cannot test one. It only asks whether the table departs from independence.\nStep 5. Check the word population appears somewhere. Hypotheses are always claims about the population; the 300 pupils are the evidence, not the subject.\nMemory hook: H0 is the boring one. For chi-square the boring sentence is \"these two have nothing to do with each other\".","wrongs":["These are goodness-of-fit hypotheses, about one variable on its own. They would suit a question like \"are the three clubs equally popular?\". They ask only whether the pupils spread themselves evenly, and say nothing about whether club choice depends on where a pupil lives, which is what the table is for.","There are no means here. The cells hold COUNTS of pupils, not averages of a measured score, so there are no cell averages to compare. Hypotheses about means belong to t tests and ANOVA, and those need a numerical outcome variable, which this study does not have.","The two hypotheses have been swapped, and the claim she is making has been put in H0. H0 has to be the hypothesis of no relationship, because the whole logic of the test is to compute what the table would look like IF there were none and then measure the distance from that. You cannot compute expected counts from \"they are associated\".",""],"trap":"Putting the interesting claim in H0 because it is the one the researcher cares about. H0 is chosen for what you can calculate from, not for what you hope.","tri":"Symptom: two categorical variables, one sample, hypotheses asked for. -&gt; Diagnosis: hypotheses for a chi-square test of independence. -&gt; Treatment: H0 says the two variables are NOT associated (independent) in the population. H1 says they ARE associated. Nothing about means, nothing about equal category sizes, and no direction in H1."},{"n":12,"q":"Matteo Lombardi measures curiosity and visit minutes for 210 science centre visitors. The correlation is .42. Curiosity has a standard deviation of 8.5 points and visit length 24.0 minutes. What is the slope predicting minutes from curiosity? (2 decimals)","o":["0.15","1.19","0.42","0.50"],"a":1,"pct":50,"typ":"slope_from_r","theme":0,"spot":"HINT. An r and TWO standard deviations, and a slope wanted. That is the one formula. The only thing that can go wrong is which SD goes on top, so settle that before you touch the calculator.","plain":"In plain words: the correlation says how tightly the two move together, but it has no units. The slope has to be in minutes per curiosity point, so it needs the spread of minutes on top and the spread of curiosity underneath.","work":"Step 1. Name y and x. The sentence says predicts MINUTES from CURIOSITY, so y = minutes and x = curiosity.\nStep 2. Put the right SD in the right place. s(y) = 24.0 minutes on top, s(x) = 8.5 points underneath.\nStep 3. Compute. 0.42 x 24.0 = 10.08, and 10.08 / 8.5 = 1.1859, so 1.19.\nStep 4. Check the units. The answer is minutes per curiosity point. One extra point of curiosity goes with about 1.19 extra minutes in the building. That reads sensibly.\nStep 5. A quick way to catch a flipped fraction: s(y) is nearly three times s(x) here, so the slope must be bigger than r, not smaller. 1.19 is bigger than 0.42, so the fraction is the right way up.\nMemory hook: the SD of the thing you are PREDICTING goes on TOP. Predicted on top, predictor on the bottom.","wrongs":["This has the two SDs the wrong way up: 0.42 x 8.5 / 24.0 = 0.1488. That number is the slope of the line predicting curiosity from minutes, which is a different line and answers a different question.","","This hands in r itself as the slope. They are only the same number when both variables have been standardised to the same SD. Here one is in points and one in minutes, so r on its own cannot be a slope: it has no units.","This uses r squared in place of r: 0.1764 x 24.0 / 8.5 = 0.4981. R squared belongs in a sentence about explained variation, never in the slope formula."],"trap":"Writing the formula as r times s(x) over s(y) from memory. Anchor it with the units instead: the slope says \"how much y per one x\", so y is on top.","tri":"Symptom: r given, both SDs given, slope wanted. -&gt; Diagnosis: slope from r. -&gt; Treatment: b = r x s(y) / s(x), where y is the thing being PREDICTED and x is the thing predicting. Decide which is which from the words \"predicts y from x\"."},{"n":13,"q":"Selma Gunnarsdottir records, for 96 Icelandic greenhouse growers, weekly hours spent on record-keeping and yield per square metre. The correlation is .35. Which statement is correct?","o":["More record-keeping tends to go with higher yield, and about 12 per cent of the variation in yield goes with it.","About 35 per cent of yield variation is accounted for, since .35 states that share.","One more hour a week can mean .35 more yield, since .35 is the rise per unit.","Keeping better records produces a higher yield, since the sign shows which variable pushes."],"a":0,"pct":50,"typ":"corr_interpret","theme":0,"spot":"HINT. A single r, and four sentences claiming to say what it means. Run each option past three checks: does it confuse r with r squared, does it treat r as a slope, does it slide into cause? Whatever survives all three is the answer.","plain":"In plain words: .35 means they drift upwards together, not very tightly. Square it to talk about explained variation, and never turn it into a cause or into a \"per hour\" figure.","work":"Step 1. Read the sign. Positive, so more record-keeping goes with more yield.\nStep 2. Read the strength. .35 is moderate: a visible upward drift in a scatterplot with plenty of scatter around it.\nStep 3. Square it if you want to talk about variation. 0.35 x 0.35 = 0.1225, about 12 per cent. So roughly an eighth of the spread in yield lines up with the spread in record-keeping, and seven eighths does not.\nStep 4. Refuse the slope reading. To say how much yield per extra hour she would need s(yield) and s(hours), which the stem does not give.\nStep 5. Refuse the causal reading. Nobody assigned the hours. Careful growers may do several careful things, and the records may be a symptom of care rather than its cause.\nMemory hook: r for direction and strength, r squared for share of variation, slope for per-unit change, random assignment for cause. Four questions, four different tools.","wrongs":["","This quotes r where r squared belongs. The share of variation that goes together is 0.35 squared = 0.1225, that is 12 per cent, not 35 per cent. This is the single most common slip on correlation questions, and 35 per cent overstates the relationship by almost three times.","This reads r as a slope. A slope carries units, here yield per hour, and r carries none: it would be .35 whether the hours were recorded in hours or in minutes. Converting r into a slope needs both standard deviations.","This is the causal jump. The sign of a correlation gives the direction of the trend, never the direction of influence. A positive correlation in observed data is compatible with record-keeping lifting yield, with high yield making records worth keeping, and with a third thing such as experience lifting both."],"trap":"Forgetting to square before talking about explained variation. If an option uses the words \"variation\", \"accounted for\" or \"explained\", the number in it must be r SQUARED.","tri":"Symptom: one correlation, four interpretations. -&gt; Diagnosis: interpreting r. -&gt; Treatment: r gives direction and strength only. For explained variation, square it. For a per-unit change, you need a slope, which needs the two SDs. For cause, you need random assignment, which an observational study does not have."},{"n":14,"q":"Arjun Bhatt finds a correlation of .58 between parcel weight in kilograms and delivery charge in rupees, across 140 parcels. Which change to his data would leave the correlation at exactly .58?","o":["Dropping the ten heaviest parcels, since r is standardised and cases do not matter.","Adding a very heavy parcel charged almost nothing, since one case among 140 cannot move r.","Replacing each parcel with its district average, since averaging leaves r unchanged.","Recording weights in grams and charges in euros, since only the scale changes."],"a":3,"pct":50,"typ":"r_invariance","theme":0,"spot":"HINT. The words \"would leave r unchanged\" or \"exactly the same\". That is the invariance question. One option will be a pure change of units or a shift by a constant; the others will all change WHICH points are in the data or HOW SPREAD OUT they are.","plain":"In plain words: r is a number about the pattern, not about the measuring stick. Change the stick and r stays put. Change which parcels are in the pile and r moves.","work":"Step 1. Recall what r is built from. Each value is turned into a z score, how many SDs it sits from its own mean, and r is the average product of those z scores.\nStep 2. Apply a change of units. Multiply every weight by 1000 to get grams. The mean multiplies by 1000, the SD multiplies by 1000, so every z score is exactly what it was. The same goes for converting rupees into euros.\nStep 3. Conclude: the z scores are untouched, so r is untouched. Still .58.\nStep 4. Check the others quickly. Dropping the heaviest parcels narrows the range of x, which pulls r down. Adding a strange point moves r, usually a lot in a sample this size. Averaging to districts throws away within-district variation and usually pushes r up.\nStep 5. Note the one caveat: multiplying by a NEGATIVE constant flips the sign of r while keeping its size.\nMemory hook: r does not care what ruler you used. It cares a great deal which points you measured.","wrongs":["This is range restriction. Standardising removes the units, not the cases. Taking out the ten heaviest parcels shrinks the spread of weights, and a narrower spread of x almost always lowers r even when the underlying relationship is unchanged.","This is the outlier effect. One point that breaks the pattern can shift r noticeably, and with 140 points a single extreme pair that contradicts the trend will pull r down.","This is aggregation. Averaging the parcels within each district removes the variation between parcels inside a district and keeps only the variation between districts, which usually makes r larger. It would not stay at .58.",""],"trap":"Thinking that \"fewer points\" or \"more points\" is the same kind of change as \"new units\". Changing the measuring scale is cosmetic. Changing the set of cases is a change to the data.","tri":"Symptom: a given r and four proposed changes to the data. -&gt; Diagnosis: what leaves r unchanged. -&gt; Treatment: r survives any LINEAR rescaling of either variable, that is multiplying by a positive constant or adding a constant, because r is computed from standardised scores and standardising undoes the change. r does NOT survive adding or removing cases, restricting the range, or averaging cases together."},{"n":15,"q":"An entrance test correlates .55 with first-year marks across all 900 applicants to a conservatoire. Using only the 180 admitted, all in the top band of the test, Rosalind Ayers gets .21. Which explanation fits best?","o":["The test works less well for strong candidates, so the relationship genuinely weakens and .21 is honest.","With 180 instead of 900 the drop from .55 to .21 is sampling error; another group could exceed .55.","The admitted group covers only a narrow slice of test scores, and restricted range pulls r towards zero.","The .21 is the more trustworthy figure, as it comes from students who took the course."],"a":2,"pct":50,"typ":"restriction","theme":0,"spot":"HINT. The same two variables, two correlations, and one of them computed on a SELECTED subgroup, usually the top scorers, the admitted, the shortlisted or the hired. That is range restriction every time.","plain":"In plain words: once you keep only the high scorers, the test scores are all bunched together. With almost no spread left in the test, there is almost nothing for it to line up with, so the correlation falls even though nothing about the students has changed.","work":"Step 1. Picture the full scatterplot of 900 applicants: a broad upward cloud from low scores and low marks to high scores and high marks.\nStep 2. Now cover up everything except the right-hand edge, the top band. What is left is a short, tall, nearly vertical slab of points.\nStep 3. Ask what r measures in that slab. The test scores inside it barely differ, but the marks still vary a lot, for all the other reasons marks vary. So the points look almost unrelated, and r collapses.\nStep 4. Notice what has NOT changed. The line through the cloud has the same slope. The relationship is the same; only the visible spread of x has shrunk.\nStep 5. Draw the practical lesson. To judge whether the entrance test predicts success she needs the correlation over the full range of applicants, .55, not the one computed on the people the test already filtered.\nMemory hook: no spread, no correlation. Cut the spread of x and you cut r, whatever the truth underneath.","wrongs":["This would be a real possibility if the relationship genuinely bent or weakened at the top. But the restricted r is lower for a purely mechanical reason, so there is no need to invent a change in the test to explain it, and nothing here is evidence for one.","Sampling error moves r up as often as down, and a drop from .55 to .21 is far too large and far too predictable for noise. Restriction of range produces a drop in one direction every time, which is the clue that something systematic is at work.","","It is the less useful of the two for the question she is asking. The whole point of an entrance test is to tell apart people across the full range of ability. Judging it only on the people it already selected is like testing a sieve on the grains that got through."],"trap":"Reading the lower correlation as the honest one because its sample feels more real. Which correlation answers your question depends on which population you want to talk about, and for a selection test that is all applicants.","tri":"Symptom: r computed on a subgroup selected on one of the two variables, and a drop in r to be explained. -&gt; Diagnosis: restriction of range. -&gt; Treatment: say that r depends on the spread of the variables, that selecting on x removes most of that spread, and that the correlation in the restricted group therefore understates the relationship in the full applicant pool. The full-range figure, .55, is the one that describes the test as a selection tool."},{"n":16,"q":"Bogdan Ivanov has 3,000 Bulgarian pupils from 60 schools. Across the 3,000, weekly hours of music practice correlate .18 with concentration. Averaging within each school and correlating the 60 school means gives .62. What is going on?","o":["The .62 is the better estimate for a pupil, since averaging removes measurement error.","Averaging smooths away differences between pupils, so the school-level correlation is inflated.","Only one of .18 and .62 can be right, since the same data cannot give two values.","The jump from .18 to .62 shows a causal effect once the noise between pupils is removed."],"a":1,"pct":50,"typ":"aggregation","theme":0,"spot":"HINT. Two correlations from the SAME data, one at the level of individuals and one at the level of groups, schools, countries, districts or regions, with the group one much larger. That is the aggregation question.","plain":"In plain words: when you replace 50 pupils by one school average, you throw away all the disagreement inside the school and keep only the tidy differences between schools. Tidy data correlates better. That tells you about schools, not about pupils.","work":"Step 1. Count what is thrown away. Within a school, pupils differ enormously in both practice and concentration. Averaging replaces all of that with a single point.\nStep 2. Count what is kept. Only the differences BETWEEN schools, and those are far more orderly, because the things that make a school different, its intake, its music programme, its neighbourhood, tend to move both variables at once.\nStep 3. See why r rises. The scatter in the plot was mostly within-school scatter. Remove it and the 60 remaining points lie much closer to a line.\nStep 4. Decide which r he should quote. His question is about pupils, so .18 is his answer. The .62 is a true fact about schools, and he may report it as such, but it must not be presented as a pupil-level finding.\nStep 5. Note that this is not a mistake in the arithmetic. Both numbers are correct; they are correlations between different things.\nMemory hook: averages are tidier than people. The higher the level you aggregate to, the better the correlation looks and the less it says about individuals.","wrongs":["Averaging does reduce measurement error, but that is not what is driving the jump from .18 to .62: it mainly removes real variation between pupils. And the result cannot be a better estimate of a pupil-level relationship, because it is no longer about pupils at all.","","Both correlations are computed correctly, so neither of them is the one to discard. They are simply correlations between different sets of numbers: 3,000 pupil pairs in one case, 60 school pairs in the other. There is no arithmetic to fix.","Nothing here was assigned, so there is still no causal footing, and the rise in r is a consequence of averaging rather than of noise being cleaned away. A bigger correlation is never evidence of cause."],"trap":"Quoting the group-level correlation in a sentence about individuals. This is the ecological fallacy, and the exam tests it by making the aggregated figure look impressively large.","tri":"Symptom: an individual-level r and a much higher group-level r from the same dataset. -&gt; Diagnosis: aggregation, sometimes called the ecological fallacy. -&gt; Treatment: say that averaging removes within-group variation, which is most of the scatter, so the group-level r is systematically higher. Then say which r answers which question: .18 is the one about pupils, .62 is the one about schools."},{"n":17,"q":"Tomoko Shimizu has 28 participants and a correlation of .66 between minutes walked and mood. One participant sits far out, with the most walking and the best mood. Without that person the correlation falls to .12. What is the sensible reading?","o":["A single point can carry a correlation, so she should report it with and without that participant.","Nothing much, since with 28 participants one point cannot move r, so .66 to .12 has another cause.","The outlier confirms the relationship, since a point in the same direction can only strengthen it.","She has to keep the .66, since removing a real observation is never allowed and all 28 count."],"a":0,"pct":50,"typ":"outliers","theme":0,"spot":"HINT. A correlation quoted twice, with and without one unusual case, and a big gap between the two. That is the outlier question. The answer is never \"ignore it\" and never \"delete it quietly\".","plain":"In plain words: almost the whole correlation came from one person. The other 27 barely show a relationship at all. That is a fragile finding, and she has to say so rather than pick whichever number she prefers.","work":"Step 1. Measure the damage. r goes from .66 to .12. In terms of shared variation, from 44 per cent down to under 2 per cent. Almost all of it rested on one person.\nStep 2. Understand the leverage. A point far from both means contributes a large product of z scores, and with only 28 cases each contribution is a big slice of the average.\nStep 3. Decide what to do. Check first whether it is an error, a typing slip or a different measurement unit. If it is a genuine person, she may not simply bin them for being inconvenient.\nStep 4. Report honestly. Give .66 for all 28, give .12 for the 27, and let the reader see that the result depends on one case.\nStep 5. Draw the lesson for the next study. With a wider sample she would find out whether this person is unusual or the start of a real pattern at high walking levels.\nMemory hook: in a small sample, one point can be most of the finding. Always look at the scatterplot before you trust an r.","wrongs":["","The opposite is true. The smaller the sample, the more leverage a single point has, and 28 is small. The drop from .66 to .12 is itself the proof that one point moved r a great deal.","Sitting in the same direction as the trend does not make a point supportive. It makes the point the main source of the trend. The remaining 27 participants, who are the actual body of evidence, show almost nothing.","Removing a case is allowed, so long as you say that you did it and why. What is not allowed is removing it silently, or reporting only whichever of the two correlations suits the story."],"trap":"Choosing one of the two numbers and reporting it alone. The exam answer is always the transparent one: report both and name the point.","tri":"Symptom: r with and without one point, and a large difference. -&gt; Diagnosis: the influence of an outlier on r. -&gt; Treatment: recognise that a point far out along both variables has a large lever on r, especially in a small sample. Report both values, be transparent about the exclusion, and treat the finding as provisional until more data arrive."},{"n":18,"q":"Pavel Horak regresses running cost on kilometres driven for 220 vans, then plots residuals against predicted costs. The cloud stays centred on the zero line with no bend, but is narrow at low predicted cost and fans out at high. What does that tell him?","o":["The relationship is curved, so a straight line is the wrong shape here, and the widening is the curve pulling away.","The residuals are not centred on zero, so the slope is biased and the widening shows it.","There is one influential outlier at the high-cost end, and one far-out van widens the band.","The line still fits the average, but error spread grows with predicted cost, so standard errors cannot be trusted."],"a":3,"pct":50,"typ":"resid_plot","theme":0,"spot":"HINT. Residuals plotted against predicted values, with the SHAPE of the band described in words. Two different faults look different: a BEND in the middle of the band means the wrong shape of line; a FAN, narrow at one end and wide at the other, means unequal spread. Read the description for which one it is.","plain":"In plain words: the line itself is fine, because the points sit around zero all the way across. What is not fine is that the errors are small for cheap vans and large for expensive ones. The model is more precise in some places than others, and the printed standard errors assume it is equally precise everywhere.","work":"Step 1. Check the first thing a residual plot is for: is the band centred on zero across the whole range? Yes. So the straight line is capturing the average correctly and there is no systematic under or over prediction.\nStep 2. Check for a bend. The stem says there is none. So curvature is ruled out.\nStep 3. Check the width. Narrow on the left, wide on the right, growing steadily. That is the fan shape, and it means the typical size of an error depends on how large the prediction is.\nStep 4. Work out what breaks. The slope estimate itself is still unbiased. What breaks is everything built on a single error variance: the standard errors, so the t tests, so the confidence intervals and prediction intervals.\nStep 5. Say what he can do. Model the log of cost instead of cost, or use robust standard errors, then look at the residual plot again.\nMemory hook: a BEND is about the shape of the line. A FAN is about the size of the errors. Centred and fanning means trust the line, distrust the p values.","wrongs":["Curvature shows up as a band that drifts above zero in the middle and below it at the ends, or the other way round. The widening here is a change in the spread of the errors, not in the shape of the line, and the stem says explicitly that the band stays centred and has no bend.","The stem says the cloud stays centred on the zero line. In least squares the residuals always average to zero anyway, so this can never be the reading of a residual plot.","One outlier is a single isolated point, not a steady widening of the whole band. The description is of every van at the high end having a bigger error, which is a pattern across many cases rather than one odd case.",""],"trap":"Jumping to \"the model is wrong, use a curve\" whenever a residual plot looks untidy. Untidy in the middle means wrong shape. Untidy at one end only means unequal spread, and those have different consequences and different fixes.","tri":"Symptom: a residual plot described as centred but widening, a trumpet or a fan. -&gt; Diagnosis: unequal spread of the errors, that is, the constant-variance assumption has failed. -&gt; Treatment: say that the fit of the LINE is acceptable because the band is centred on zero and has no bend, but the inference is not, because the formulas for standard errors, t tests and intervals all assume one constant error spread. Usual fixes: transform the response, for example take logs, or use standard errors that do not assume constant spread."},{"n":19,"q":"Zainab Osei predicts weekly hours of unpaid care for 84 Ghanaian adults from three household measures. R squared = .231; the MS and F columns are cut off, below. What is the F for the model? (2 decimals)","o":["0.10","0.30","8.00","8.30"],"a":2,"pct":50,"typ":"f_from_output","theme":0,"spot":"HINT. A printed table with SS and df but no MS and no F, and one F wanted. Two divisions then one division. Never divide an SS by an SS.","plain":"In plain words: each sum of squares has to be turned into an average first, by dividing by its own degrees of freedom. Then F is one average divided by the other, model over error.","work":"Step 1. MS for the regression row. 540.0 / 3 = 180.0.\nStep 2. MS for the residual row. 1800.0 / 80 = 22.5.\nStep 3. F. 180.0 / 22.5 = 8.00.\nStep 4. Check the table hangs together. 540 + 1800 = 2340, the total SS, and 3 + 80 = 83, the total df. Both tie up, so the numbers are as printed.\nStep 5. Check it against R squared. 540 / 2340 = 0.2308, printed as .231. Consistent.\nStep 6. Confirm the df and the verdict. F(3, 80) = 8.00 has p = .0001, so the three predictors together do far better than nothing. Note that df error here is n - p - 1 = 84 - 3 - 1 = 80, which is what the table prints.\nMemory hook: SS to MS to F. Three steps, in that order, and each SS meets only its own df.","wrongs":["This divides MS regression by the residual SS instead of the residual MS: 180.0 / 1800.0 = 0.10. Both rows have to be turned into MS before you divide. Half-converting is worse than not converting at all, because the answer looks like a real F.","This divides SS by SS: 540.0 / 1800.0 = 0.30. An F is a ratio of two average squares, not of two totals, and the df are what turn the totals into averages. An F near 0.3 would also mean the model explains less than chance, which does not match R squared of .231.","","This divides the residual SS by the TOTAL df: 1800.0 / 83 = 21.6867, giving F = 180.0 / 21.6867 = 8.30. Each row uses its own df. The total df belongs to the total row and is never a denominator."],"trap":"Dividing SS by SS because both numbers are sitting there in the same column. The df column is in the table for a reason: use it twice before you divide once.","tri":"Symptom: a regression ANOVA table with SS and df, F asked for. -&gt; Diagnosis: F from regression output. -&gt; Treatment: MS = SS/df in each row, then F = MS regression / MS residual. Use each row own df, and never the total df.","out":{"cap":"Regression ANOVA table, three predictors, MS and F columns missing","h":["Source","SS","df"],"r":[["Regression","540.0","3"],["Residual","1800.0","80"],["Total","2340.0","83"]]}},{"n":20,"q":"Lukas Prochazka finds an old printout predicting the resale value of a used tractor from four features. The df column is lost, below. How many tractors were in the sample? (whole number)","o":["100","95","96","99"],"a":0,"pct":50,"typ":"backout_n","theme":0,"spot":"HINT. A table with a hole in it and a question about the SAMPLE SIZE. That is the work-backwards question. Nothing new to learn: use MS = SS/df in reverse to get each df, then the df error formula in reverse to get n.","plain":"In plain words: he can rebuild the missing degrees of freedom by asking what number each sum of squares must have been divided by to give the printed average. Then he turns the error df back into a headcount.","work":"Step 1. Recover the regression df. 1680.0 / 420.0 = 4. That matches the four features, which is the check that this is the right approach.\nStep 2. Recover the residual df. 2850.0 / 30.0 = 95.\nStep 3. Write the error df formula for multiple regression. df error = n - p - 1, with p = 4 predictors.\nStep 4. Solve for n. 95 = n - 4 - 1, so n = 95 + 5 = 100.\nStep 5. Check the whole table. Regression df 4 plus residual df 95 is 99, which is n - 1 = 99, the total df. And 1680 + 2850 = 4530, the total SS. Everything ties up.\nMemory hook: df error = n - p - 1, so n = df error + p + 1. Count the predictors, then add ONE MORE for the intercept the model also had to estimate.","wrongs":["","This hands in the residual df, 95, as the sample size. The residual df is what is LEFT after the model has spent some df on estimating things. Five df were spent here, four on the features and one on the intercept.","This adds back only the intercept: 95 + 1 = 96. That is the formula for a regression with no predictors at all. Each of the four features also costs one df.","This adds back only the four predictors: 95 + 4 = 99. It is the commonest slip on this question, because people remember n - p and forget the intercept. Notice that 99 is in fact the TOTAL df, n - 1, which is another way to see that it cannot be n."],"trap":"Forgetting the intercept. Write the formula out in full before you substitute: n - p - 1, with THREE terms, not two.","tri":"Symptom: a regression table with df missing and n wanted. -&gt; Diagnosis: recover n from the error df. -&gt; Treatment: df = SS / MS for each row. Then, since df error = n - p - 1 for a regression with p predictors, n = df error + p + 1. The plus one is the intercept, and it is the step everybody drops.","out":{"cap":"Regression output with the df column lost","h":["Source","SS","df","MS"],"r":[["Regression","1680.0","?","420.0"],["Residual","2850.0","?","30.0"],["Total","4530.0","?",""]]}},{"n":21,"q":"Lucinda Barros predicts drying time from tile thickness. At 12 mm her line predicts 46.0 minutes, and her software gives two intervals at the same confidence level: 44.1 to 47.9 and 38.6 to 53.4. Which is which?","o":["44.1 to 47.9 is for one new 12 mm batch, 38.6 to 53.4 for the mean of all 12 mm batches.","44.1 to 47.9 is for the mean of all 12 mm batches, 38.6 to 53.4 for a single new batch.","Both are for a single new batch, the wider one at a higher confidence level.","The wider interval must be a software error: a prediction at 12 mm is never less certain than an average."],"a":1,"pct":50,"typ":"two_intervals","theme":0,"spot":"HINT. Two intervals at the same x value, same confidence, different widths. That is always the confidence interval for the MEAN response against the PREDICTION interval for ONE case. You never have to compute: the wider one is always the prediction interval.","plain":"In plain words: asking where the average drying time of 12 mm tiles sits is an easier question than asking how long the next batch will take. Averages are steadier than single cases, so the interval for the average is the narrow one.","work":"Step 1. Check the centres. 44.1 and 47.9 average to 46.0. 38.6 and 53.4 average to 46.0. Both sit on the predicted value, as they must.\nStep 2. Compare the half-widths. The first is 46.0 plus and minus 1.9. The second is 46.0 plus and minus 7.4, almost four times as much.\nStep 3. Ask what each question has to allow for. For the MEAN of all 12 mm batches, only one thing is unknown: exactly where the true line passes. For ONE new batch, two things are unknown: where the line is, and how far this particular batch will fall from it.\nStep 4. Match them up. Two sources of uncertainty must give the wider interval, so 38.6 to 53.4 is the prediction interval and 44.1 to 47.9 is the CI for the mean.\nStep 5. Notice what happens with more data. Collect thousands of batches and the line becomes almost perfectly known, so the CI for the mean shrinks towards zero width. The prediction interval does not: individual batches still scatter, and that scatter is a fact about tiles, not about her sample size.\nMemory hook: averages are calmer than individuals. Mean interval narrow, prediction interval wide, both on the same centre.","wrongs":["This has the two the wrong way round. An average is steadier than a single case, not shakier: that is the whole reason we take averages.","","Both intervals are at the same confidence level, as the stem says. The difference in width comes from what they are intervals FOR, not from how confident they are.","The wider interval is exactly what good software should print. A prediction for one case is genuinely less certain than a statement about an average, so a prediction interval is always wider than the corresponding CI for the mean, at every value of x."],"trap":"Reading the wide interval as a sign that something is wrong. It is the honest one: predicting a single future case is a harder job than locating an average.","tri":"Symptom: two intervals at one x, same confidence, one wider. -&gt; Diagnosis: CI for the mean response against prediction interval for an individual. -&gt; Treatment: the narrow one is the CI for the mean, which carries only the uncertainty in where the LINE is. The wide one is the prediction interval, which carries that same uncertainty PLUS the natural scatter of individual batches about the line. Both are centred on the same predicted value."},{"n":22,"q":"Mirela Popescu predicts books read per year by a Romanian household from income in thousands of lei and the main reader's education. Output below, with R squared = .330 and F(2, 57) = 14.04, p &lt; .001. Which statement is NOT true?","o":["With education held constant, one thousand lei more income predicts 0.062 more books a year.","There were 60 households: the error df of 57 in F(2, 57) is n less two less one.","Education has a t of 3.500 because 0.315 divided by 0.090 is 3.5, a coefficient over its standard error.","Because R squared is .330, the correlation between actual and predicted books is also .330."],"a":3,"pct":50,"typ":"read_output","theme":0,"spot":"HINT. Printed output and the words NOT true, false or incorrect. Three statements will be plain readings of the table and one will swap two quantities that look alike. Check each option against the table rather than against your memory, and watch for r against r squared and for b against t.","plain":"In plain words: three of these sentences are things you can read straight off the output. One of them quietly treats R squared as if it were a correlation. Correlations and squared correlations are not the same number.","work":"Step 1. Take the income coefficient. b = 0.062 with education held constant. That is exactly what a coefficient in a multiple regression means. True.\nStep 2. Take the sample size. df error = 57 is printed inside F(2, 57), and n = df error + p + 1 = 57 + 2 + 1 = 60. True.\nStep 3. Take the t for education. 0.315 / 0.090 = 3.5, which is what the table prints. True, and worth checking on the other rows too: 1.840 / 1.250 = 1.472 and 0.062 / 0.025 = 2.480. The table is internally consistent.\nStep 4. Take the last statement. R squared = .330 is the SHARE of variation explained. The correlation between observed and predicted books is the multiple R, which is sqrt(0.330) = 0.574, not 0.330. So this statement is false, and it is the answer.\nStep 5. Note the size of the error. .330 against .574 is not a rounding matter; it understates how closely the predictions track reality.\nMemory hook: R squared is the SQUARE. To get back to a correlation you take the root. R squared .33 goes with R .57, never with R .33.","wrongs":["True, and it is the standard reading of a coefficient in a model with more than one predictor: the effect of income on books among households that match on education.","True. The second number inside F(2, 57) is the error df, and for a regression with p predictors df error = n - p - 1. So n = 57 + 2 + 1 = 60.","True, and it is worth checking because every t in regression output is its own coefficient divided by its own standard error. 0.315 / 0.090 = 3.500, exactly as printed.",""],"trap":"Letting .330 do duty as both the explained share and the correlation. They are the same quantity on two different scales, and the exam puts both numbers within reach so that only a square root tells them apart.","tri":"Symptom: printed regression output, one false statement to be found. -&gt; Diagnosis: reading regression output. -&gt; Treatment: verify each claim against the table. Each t must equal its own b over its own SE. n must equal df error + p + 1. A coefficient is a change in y per one unit of x with the other predictors held constant. R squared is a share of variation, and the multiple R, the correlation between observed and predicted y, is its SQUARE ROOT.","out":{"cap":"Regression output: books read per year","h":["Term","b","SE","t"],"r":[["(Constant)","1.84","1.25","1.472"],["Income (thousands of lei)","0.062","0.025","2.48"],["Education (years)","0.315","0.09","3.5"]]}},{"n":23,"q":"Anders Kjellberg compares sight-reading scores for six studios. Instead of one overall test he compares every pair separately at the .05 level. What is the chance that at least one is significant if the studios are really the same? (3 decimals)","o":["0.265","0.750","0.537","0.785"],"a":2,"pct":50,"typ":"familywise","theme":0,"spot":"HINT. The words every possible pair, each at .05, and a chance of at least one. That is the family-wise error rate. Two steps only: count the pairs, then 1 - .95 to the power of that count.","plain":"In plain words: every test he runs is another lottery ticket for a false alarm. With fifteen tickets the chance of winning at least one false alarm is better than even, which is why one overall test comes first.","work":"Step 1. Count the comparisons. With k = 6 studios, m = 6 x 5 / 2 = 30 / 2 = 15 pairs. Check it by hand if you like: studio 1 against five others, studio 2 against four more, then 3, 2 and 1, which is 5 + 4 + 3 + 2 + 1 = 15.\nStep 2. Probability one test gives no false alarm. 1 - .05 = .95.\nStep 3. Probability all fifteen give no false alarm. .95^15 = 0.4633.\nStep 4. Probability at least one does. 1 - 0.4633 = 0.5367, so 0.537.\nStep 5. Read it back. More than half the time he would find a \"significant\" difference in a school where every studio is identical. That is why a single overall F test comes first, and why follow-up comparisons are corrected.\nMemory hook: count the pairs with k(k - 1)/2, then 1 minus .95 to that power. The answer is always bigger than you expect, and it never goes above 1, which is the check on the multiplying mistake.","wrongs":["This uses m = 6, the number of GROUPS, instead of 15, the number of PAIRS: 1 - .95^6 = 0.265. He is running one test per pair, not one per studio.","This multiplies instead: 15 x .05 = 0.750. Multiplying is a rough upper bound that works for small m but overstates the rate, and with enough tests it would give a probability above 1, which is impossible. The exact figure uses powers of .95.","","This uses m = k(k - 1) = 30, forgetting to halve: 1 - .95^30 = 0.785. The pair studio 1 against studio 2 is the same comparison as studio 2 against studio 1, so each pair must be counted once. That is what the division by 2 does."],"trap":"Forgetting the division by two in k(k - 1)/2. Write the pairs out for a small case once, three groups give three pairs, and the formula will stick.","tri":"Symptom: several groups, all pairwise tests, each at .05, chance of at least one false positive wanted. -&gt; Diagnosis: family-wise error rate. -&gt; Treatment: m = k(k - 1)/2 pairs. Each test individually avoids a false alarm with probability .95. All m avoid one with probability .95 to the power m. So at least one false alarm has probability 1 - .95^m."},{"n":24,"q":"Farida Nasser times serving at five branches of an Egyptian pharmacy chain, 13 customers each. Part of her output is below. What is her estimate of the standard deviation of serving times within a branch? (2 decimals)","o":["6.50","6.29","42.25","50.35"],"a":0,"pct":50,"typ":"sqrt_mse","theme":0,"spot":"HINT. An ANOVA table and a question about a standard deviation, a typical spread, or how far scores sit from their group mean. The error row holds the answer, but only after you turn SS into MS and then take a square root.","plain":"In plain words: the error row measures how much customers differ from the average at their own branch. Divided by its df it becomes a variance, and the square root of a variance is a standard deviation, which is what she was asked for.","work":"Step 1. Check the design against the table. Five branches with 13 customers each is N = 65, so df error = N - k = 65 - 5 = 60 and total df = N - 1 = 64. Both match what is printed.\nStep 2. MS error. 2535.0 / 60 = 42.25.\nStep 3. Take the square root. sqrt(42.25) = 6.50.\nStep 4. Read the units. MS error is in squared seconds; 42.25 cannot be a standard deviation. Only after the root are you back in seconds.\nStep 5. Sense check. A typical customer is served about 6.5 seconds either side of the average for their branch. A plausible figure, and it is the number that would go into any effect size or post hoc comparison.\nMemory hook: s = sqrt(MSE). MSE is a VARIANCE, and variances are squared things. Whenever a question wants a standard deviation, look for a square root at the end.","wrongs":["","This divides the error SS by the TOTAL df, 64 instead of 60: 2535.0 / 64 = 39.6094, and the root of that is 6.29. The printed total df of 64 sits right there in the table to tempt you. Each row divides by its own df.","This hands in MS error, 42.25, where the standard deviation was asked for. That is the variance. It is in squared seconds and is about six and a half times too big.","This takes the square root of the SS rather than of the MS: sqrt(2535.0) = 50.35. The df step cannot be skipped, because an SS grows with the sample size while a variance does not."],"trap":"Stopping at the MS. Underline the words standard deviation in the stem, then underline the square root in your own working, so the two match before you pick an option.","tri":"Symptom: ANOVA output, a standard deviation wanted. -&gt; Diagnosis: the pooled within-group SD. -&gt; Treatment: MS error = SS error / df error, then s = sqrt(MS error). Two steps, and the second one is the one people forget. Use the ERROR df, never the total df.","out":{"cap":"One-way ANOVA: serving time at five branches","h":["Source","SS","df"],"r":[["Between branches","507.0","4"],["Within branches (error)","2535.0","60"],["Total","3042.0","64"]]}},{"n":25,"q":"Minseo Kang compares Korean characters written per minute across four groups of learners using different workbooks, of 18, 22, 16 and 24 learners. What degrees of freedom does her overall F test have? (whole numbers)","o":["4 and 76","3 and 76","3 and 79","76 and 3"],"a":1,"pct":50,"typ":"df_oneway","theme":0,"spot":"HINT. Several groups, a group size for each, one outcome measured, and df wanted. That is the one-way F. Two numbers only: how many groups, and how many people altogether. The group sizes being unequal changes nothing.","plain":"In plain words: the first df counts the groups minus one, because once you know three of the four group means and the overall mean, the fourth is fixed. The second df is everybody, minus one for each group mean that had to be estimated.","work":"Step 1. Count the groups. Four workbooks, so k = 4.\nStep 2. Count the people. 18 + 22 + 16 + 24 = 80, so N = 80.\nStep 3. First df, the numerator. k - 1 = 4 - 1 = 3.\nStep 4. Second df, the denominator. N - k = 80 - 4 = 76.\nStep 5. Write the pair in the right order. F(3, 76). The small number always comes first, because the model df can never exceed the error df in a study worth running.\nStep 6. Check by addition. 3 + 76 = 79, which is N - 1 = 79, the total df. That addition check catches almost every df slip.\nMemory hook: groups minus one, then everybody minus the groups. And the two must add up to N - 1.","wrongs":["This uses k rather than k - 1 for the numerator. Four group means are not four free numbers: once the overall mean is fixed, only three of them can move independently. Also notice that 4 + 76 = 80, not 79, so the addition check fails.","","This uses N - 1 = 79 where N - k belongs. N - 1 is the TOTAL df for the whole analysis. The error df has to give up one df for each of the four group means that were estimated. And 3 + 79 = 82, which is not 79.","The two numbers are right but written the wrong way round. F(76, 3) is a different distribution with a quite different critical value, and reporting it this way round would make a significant result look non-significant."],"trap":"Reaching for N - 1 because that is the df you learned first, with the t test. In ANOVA the error df pays for every group mean, so it is N - k.","tri":"Symptom: k groups, N people, a one-way F. -&gt; Diagnosis: df for a one-way ANOVA. -&gt; Treatment: the pair is (k - 1, N - k), model first and error second. Add the group sizes to get N. Unequal group sizes do not change the formula."},{"n":26,"q":"Chidi Eze compares minutes a day on bookkeeping for three groups of 25 Nigerian market traders and reports F(2, 72) = 5.84, p = .004. What is he entitled to say?","o":["All three means differ, because a significant F rejects equality for every pair at once.","The largest and smallest mean differ, but nothing can be said about the middle group.","The three means are equal, because the F test compares variances rather than means.","Not all three means are equal, so he needs a follow-up comparison."],"a":3,"pct":75,"typ":"anova_conclude","theme":0,"spot":"HINT. A significant F with three or more groups and the words what can he conclude. The right answer is always the modest one: not all means are equal, and post hoc tests come next. Any option that names which groups differ is going further than the F test can.","plain":"In plain words: the overall test has told him the three groups are not all the same. It has not told him who differs from whom. That is a second question and it needs a second set of tests.","work":"Step 1. Write down what H0 said. All three population means are equal.\nStep 2. Compare p with .05. p = .004 is smaller, so reject H0.\nStep 3. Write the alternative carefully. H1 is NOT \"all three differ\". It is \"the three are not all equal\", which is satisfied if just one group sits apart and the other two are identical.\nStep 4. Notice what is still unknown. The F statistic is one number built from all three means at once. It cannot tell you whether group 1 differs from group 2, or only group 3 from the other two.\nStep 5. Say what comes next: pairwise comparisons with a correction, because three groups means three pairs and three uncorrected tests inflate the error rate.\nMemory hook: F says SOMEWHERE. Post hoc says WHERE. Never let F answer the second question.","wrongs":["This is the classic over-reading. A significant F rejects equality overall, not pair by pair, and \"not all equal\" is not \"all different\". Two of the three groups could have identical means and the F would still come out significant on the strength of the third.","This names a specific pair, which no overall F can do. It is also possible for F to be significant when the largest and smallest means are not significantly different from each other once a correction is applied.","Rejecting H0 is the opposite of concluding equality. And although the F statistic is built as a ratio of two variances, the hypotheses it tests are about MEANS: the between-groups variance is just a way of measuring how far apart the means are.",""],"trap":"Promoting \"at least one differs\" into \"they all differ\". In exam answers the cautious wording is the correct wording, and the examiner always offers the confident version next to it.","tri":"Symptom: a significant one-way F, three or more groups, a conclusion wanted. -&gt; Diagnosis: what a significant F licenses. -&gt; Treatment: reject H0 that all the population means are equal. Conclude that AT LEAST ONE differs, which is the same as \"not all are equal\". Then run corrected pairwise comparisons, for example Tukey, to locate the differences."},{"n":27,"q":"Hedda Lindholm asks whether three shop-floor layouts differ in staff tiredness. For each of 90 assistants she has the layout, a tiredness rating from 1 to 10, staff numbers and the region. Which variable is the response?","o":["The layout, because that is the variable her whole question is built around.","The number of staff in the shop, because it is the only variable that is a count.","The tiredness rating, because it is the measured outcome she compares.","The region, because it has to be held constant for the comparison to be fair."],"a":2,"pct":50,"typ":"anova_design","theme":0,"spot":"HINT. A study described with several variables listed and a question using the words response, outcome or dependent variable. Ask one question: which variable is MEASURED ON EACH PERSON and is the thing she wants to compare? That is the response. The variable that splits people into groups is the factor.","plain":"In plain words: she wants to compare tiredness between layouts. So tiredness is what gets measured and compared, and layout is the label that says which group a person belongs to.","work":"Step 1. Write the research question as a sentence. Does tiredness differ between the three layouts?\nStep 2. Fill the two slots. The thing that differs is tiredness; the thing it differs between is layout. So response = tiredness, factor = layout.\nStep 3. Check the type of each. Tiredness is a number from 1 to 10, which can be averaged, so it can be a response. Layout has three unordered categories, which cannot be averaged, so it has to be the factor.\nStep 4. Deal with the leftovers. Staff numbers and region are extra variables she has recorded. They might be useful as covariates or for checking confounding, but neither is what she set out to compare.\nStep 5. Name the analysis. A one-way ANOVA on tiredness with layout as the factor, df (3 - 1, 90 - 3) = (2, 87).\nMemory hook: the response is the thing you MEASURE, the factor is the thing you SORT BY. You can take a mean of a response; you cannot take a mean of a layout.","wrongs":["Layout is what she is interested in, but interest does not make something the response. Layout is categorical, so it has no mean to compare. It is the factor that defines the three groups.","Being a count does not make a variable the response, and the thing being averaged here is tiredness, not staff numbers. Staff numbers is not what she wants to compare between layouts; it is background information about each shop.","","Region is a possible confounder, something she might want to control or balance, not the outcome she is measuring. A variable that needs holding constant is by definition not the thing being compared."],"trap":"Choosing the variable the researcher cares most about. The response is the one that gets MEASURED and AVERAGED, which is almost never the one named in the headline.","tri":"Symptom: several variables listed, response or outcome asked for. -&gt; Diagnosis: identifying the response and the factor in a one-way ANOVA. -&gt; Treatment: the RESPONSE is the numerical variable measured on every case, the thing whose means you compare. The FACTOR is categorical and defines the groups. Rewrite the research question as \"does RESPONSE differ across levels of FACTOR?\" and the two slots fill themselves."},{"n":28,"q":"Valentina Esposito compares three label lengths (none, short, long) in two kinds of visitor (first-time, returning), 9 different visitors per combination, recording seconds spent. Only SS and df are printed, below. What is the F ratio for the label by visitor test? (2 decimals)","o":["3.00","0.33","3.31","6.00"],"a":0,"pct":50,"typ":"ms_from_ss","theme":0,"spot":"HINT. A two-way table with SS and df printed and no MS or F column, and one F wanted. Fill in only the two rows you need: the effect you were asked about, and the error row. Everything in a two-factor table divides by the SAME error MS.","plain":"In plain words: turn the interaction SS into an average by dividing by its own df, do the same for the error SS, then divide one average by the other. That ratio is the F.","work":"Step 1. Check the design against the table. a = 3 label lengths, b = 2 visitor types, so ab = 6 cells and N = 6 x 9 = 54. Interaction df = (3 - 1)(2 - 1) = 2 and error df = N - ab = 54 - 6 = 48. Both match the print-out, and the total df is 2 + 1 + 2 + 48 = 53 = N - 1.\nStep 2. MS for the interaction. 72.0 / 2 = 36.0.\nStep 3. MS for the error. 576.0 / 48 = 12.0.\nStep 4. F for the interaction. 36.0 / 12.0 = 3.00.\nStep 5. Sense check it. The critical F at .05 on (2, 48) is about 3.19, so F = 3.00 is just short of significance. If you want the other two: label length gives (96/2)/12 = 4.00 and visitor type gives (60/1)/12 = 5.00, both with the same denominator.\nStep 6. Check the SS column adds up. 96 + 60 + 72 + 576 = 804, the printed total.\nMemory hook: in a two-factor table every F shares one denominator. Work out MS error once and reuse it.","wrongs":["","This is F upside down, MS error over MS interaction: 12.0 / 36.0 = 0.33. The effect always goes on top and the error underneath, because F asks how big the effect is COMPARED WITH the noise.","This divides the error SS by the TOTAL df, 53 instead of 48: 576.0 / 53 = 10.8679, giving F = 36.0 / 10.8679 = 3.31. The total df belongs to the total row. In a two-factor design the error df is N - ab, which the table prints as 48.","This divides the interaction SS straight by the error MS: 72.0 / 12.0 = 6.00, skipping the step that turns the interaction SS into an MS. Both rows have to become MS values before the division."],"trap":"Dividing an SS by an MS. Fill in the whole MS column first, even the rows you were not asked about: it costs a few seconds and it makes a mixed-up division impossible.","tri":"Symptom: a two-way SS and df table, one F wanted. -&gt; Diagnosis: MS then F in a two-factor ANOVA. -&gt; Treatment: MS = SS / df in each row. Then F for any effect = MS for that effect / MS error. The error row is the denominator for all three F values in the table.","out":{"cap":"Two-way ANOVA: label length by visitor type","h":["Source","SS","df"],"r":[["Label length","96.0","2"],["Visitor type","60.0","1"],["Label x Visitor","72.0","2"],["Error","576.0","48"],["Total","804.0","53"]]}},{"n":29,"q":"Raul Betancourt tests two walking aids in mild and in severe joint pain, measuring walking distance. Both main effects are significant, and so is the aid by severity test. What does the interaction add?","o":["Nothing new, since both main effects were significant and the interaction only confirms it.","That the gap between the two aids differs between mild and severe pain, so each main effect needs reading in that light.","That the kind of aid is related to the severity of pain, a link between the factors.","That neither main effect can be trusted now, since the interaction cancels them."],"a":1,"pct":50,"typ":"interaction_interp","theme":0,"spot":"HINT. Two factors and a significant interaction, with the words what does this tell him or what does it add. Nothing to compute. The answer is always a sentence of the form \"the effect of ONE factor depends on the level of the OTHER\", followed by what to do about it.","plain":"In plain words: the two aids are not simply one better than the other. One of them helps mild patients more, or severe patients more. So a single average difference between the aids hides two different stories, and he should quote them separately.","work":"Step 1. Say what an interaction is. The two factors do not simply add. What one factor does changes across the levels of the other.\nStep 2. Put it in his terms. The advantage of one aid over the other is one size in mild pain and a different size in severe pain. It could even reverse.\nStep 3. Work out the consequence for the main effect. The main effect of aid is a single average across both severity groups. If the real difference is large in one and small in the other, that average belongs to neither.\nStep 4. Say what to report. Lead with the interaction, then give the aid comparison separately for mild and for severe patients. That is the answer a clinician needs.\nStep 5. Note that a significant main effect is still a real fact about the average. It is just a less useful fact once the interaction is there.\nMemory hook: interaction means IT DEPENDS. If the honest answer to \"which aid is better?\" is \"depends how bad the pain is\", you have an interaction.","wrongs":["The interaction adds a great deal. The main effects told him that the aids differ on average and that severity matters on average. Only the interaction tells him that the choice of aid should depend on the severity.","","An interaction is not a correlation between the two factors. In this design patients were allocated to an aid within each severity group, so the two factors are deliberately unrelated to each other. An interaction is about the OUTCOME behaving differently in different combinations.","This goes too far. The main effects remain valid statements about averages, and they are normally still reported. What changes is that they must not be quoted as if they described every severity group equally."],"trap":"Treating the interaction and the main effects as rivals, where only one can be true. They answer different questions. The interaction tells you whether you are entitled to quote a main effect as a single number.","tri":"Symptom: a significant interaction between two factors. -&gt; Diagnosis: interpreting an interaction. -&gt; Treatment: state that the effect of factor A depends on the level of factor B. Then give the consequence: a main effect is an average over levels where the effect is not the same size, so it describes neither level well. The fix is simple effects, that is, comparing the aids within mild pain and within severe pain."},{"n":30,"q":"Gabriel Weiss rewards children with praise or a sticker per puzzle solved, in three age groups. The six means are below. He plots them with age along the bottom, one line per reward. What will that plot show?","o":["Two lines crossing between 8 and 10 years, so the sticker is ahead at one age and behind at another.","Two flat lines on top of each other, so neither factor matters and all six means are alike.","Two rising lines growing steadily further apart with age, so there is an interaction.","Two rising lines, the sticker six puzzles above praise at every age, so no interaction."],"a":3,"pct":50,"typ":"read_plot","theme":0,"spot":"HINT. A small table of cell means and a question about what the plot of them shows. Do not estimate anything by eye. Subtract one column from the other, row by row. If the differences are all the same, the lines are parallel and there is no interaction. If they change, there is one.","plain":"In plain words: work out the sticker advantage at each age. If it is the same number every time, the two lines run side by side and the reward effect does not depend on age. If it grows or shrinks or flips, it does.","work":"Step 1. The sticker advantage, age by age. 18 - 12 = 6, then 22 - 16 = 6, then 26 - 20 = 6. The same every time.\nStep 2. Conclude on the interaction. A constant gap means the two lines are parallel, so there is no sign of an interaction.\nStep 3. Check the age main effect. Average the two rewards at each age: (12 + 18)/2 = 15, (16 + 22)/2 = 19, (20 + 26)/2 = 23. Rising by 4 each step, so age matters.\nStep 4. Check the reward main effect. Average down each column: praise (12 + 16 + 20)/3 = 16, sticker (18 + 22 + 26)/3 = 22. A gap of 6, so reward matters.\nStep 5. Describe the picture in words: two straight lines climbing from left to right, the sticker line six puzzles above the praise line all the way across, never touching and never converging.\nMemory hook: PARALLEL means no interaction, whatever else the plot does. Lines can both rise steeply and still be parallel.","wrongs":["Crossing lines would mean the sticker is better at one age and praise at another. Here the sticker is ahead by exactly 6 at every age, so the lines never come near each other.","Flat lines on top of each other would mean every one of the six means was about the same. These means run from 12 to 26, and both the row averages and the column averages differ, so both factors matter.","This is the right direction but the wrong gap. \"Further apart with age\" would need the difference to grow, for example 4, then 6, then 8. The differences here are 6, 6 and 6, so the lines stay the same distance apart.",""],"trap":"Reading \"both lines rise steeply\" as an interaction. Rising together is a main effect of the other factor. Only a CHANGE in the gap between the lines is an interaction, so always subtract before you describe.","tri":"Symptom: cell means given, the shape of the interaction plot asked for. -&gt; Diagnosis: reading an interaction plot from the means. -&gt; Treatment: compute the simple difference between the two levels of one factor at each level of the other. Constant difference means parallel lines and no interaction. Then check each factor separately for a main effect: do the row averages change, and do the column averages differ?","out":{"cap":"Mean puzzles solved in each combination","h":["Age group","Praise","Sticker"],"r":[["6 years","12.0","18.0"],["8 years","16.0","22.0"],["10 years","20.0","26.0"]]}},{"n":31,"q":"Esperanza Vidal uses four warm-ups and three floor surfaces on a balance task, 7 different volunteers per combination. What are the degrees of freedom for the warm-up by surface test and for the error term? (whole numbers)","o":["6 and 72","6 and 77","6 and 83","12 and 72"],"a":0,"pct":75,"typ":"df_interaction","theme":0,"spot":"HINT. Two factors, the number of levels of each, a number of people per combination, and df wanted. Count exactly three things and nothing else: a, the levels of the first factor; b, the levels of the second; and N, the total number of people.","plain":"In plain words: how many numbers are free to move in the interaction test, and how many are left over for the error. The first is a product of two small numbers, the second is everybody minus the number of cells.","work":"Step 1. Read off a and b. Warm-ups: 4 levels, so a = 4. Surfaces: 3 levels, so b = 3.\nStep 2. Count the cells. ab = 4 x 3 = 12 combinations.\nStep 3. Count the people. 7 in each of 12 cells, so N = 12 x 7 = 84.\nStep 4. Interaction df. (a - 1)(b - 1) = (4 - 1)(3 - 1) = 3 x 2 = 6.\nStep 5. Error df. N - ab = 84 - 12 = 72.\nStep 6. Addition check. The main effects take 3 and 2 df, so 3 + 2 + 6 + 72 = 83 = N - 1. The whole table accounts for exactly 83 df, which confirms both numbers.\nMemory hook: minus one, minus one, then MULTIPLY. 4 x 3 = 12 is the design; (4 - 1)(3 - 1) = 6 is the df.","wrongs":["","The interaction df is right, but this uses N - a - b = 84 - 4 - 3 = 77 for the error. The formula takes away the PRODUCT of the level counts, 12, not their sum, 7. One df goes for every cell mean estimated, and there are 12 cells.","The interaction df is right, but 83 is N - 1, the TOTAL df for the whole analysis. The error row still has to give up one df for each of the 12 cells, so it ends at 72.","12 is the number of CELLS, not the interaction df. This is the trap the examiner plants every time: 4 x 3 = 12 looks like an answer, but you subtract one from each factor FIRST, which gives 3 x 2 = 6."],"trap":"Writing the number of cells where the product of the \"one less\" numbers belongs. Say it out loud: minus one, minus one, THEN multiply.","tri":"Symptom: two factors with their level counts, a number per cell, and a request for df. -&gt; Diagnosis: df in a two-factor ANOVA. -&gt; Treatment: interaction df = (a - 1)(b - 1). Error df = N - ab, where ab is the number of cells and N is the total number of people. Multiply the two \"one less\" numbers; never report the number of cells."},{"n":32,"q":"Noelia Sandoval times each of 15 lifeguards on the same course with three board designs, every lifeguard using all three. The design means are below. What is the sum of squares for the design effect? (whole number)","o":["18","252","270","810"],"a":2,"pct":50,"typ":"rm_ss","theme":0,"spot":"HINT. Condition means and nothing else, plus a number of PEOPLE, and an SS for the condition effect wanted. The recipe is the same as for the between-groups SS: squared distances of the condition means from the grand mean, each multiplied by how many scores went into that mean.","plain":"In plain words: see how far each design mean sits from the overall average, square those distances so they cannot cancel, add them up, then scale by the number of lifeguards, because each design mean was built from 15 times.","work":"Step 1. The grand mean. (24.0 + 30.0 + 27.0) / 3 = 81.0 / 3 = 27.0. A tidy check: Design C is exactly on it.\nStep 2. The deviations and their squares. 24 - 27 = -3, squared 9. 30 - 27 = +3, squared 9. 27 - 27 = 0, squared 0.\nStep 3. Add the squares. 9 + 9 + 0 = 18.\nStep 4. Multiply by the number of persons. Each design mean came from 15 lifeguards, so SS design = 15 x 18 = 270.\nStep 5. The df, for completeness. k - 1 = 3 - 1 = 2, so MS design = 270 / 2 = 135.\nStep 6. Sense check. The design SS cannot be bigger than the total SS, and with means only 3 seconds apart on either side of 27 a value in the hundreds is about right for 45 timings.\nMemory hook: squared gaps, times the number of PEOPLE, not the number of scores. Each condition mean rests on n persons.","wrongs":["This stops at the sum of squared deviations, 18, without multiplying by n. An SS has to grow with the amount of data behind it: the same three means from 500 lifeguards would be much stronger evidence and must give a much bigger SS.","This multiplies by n - 1 = 14: 14 x 18 = 252. The n - 1 correction belongs to computing a variance from scores, not to this formula. Here n counts the scores that went into each condition mean, and there are 15 of them, not 14.","","This multiplies by N = 15 x 3 = 45, the total number of timings: 45 x 18 = 810. Each condition mean was built from 15 timings, not 45, so the multiplier is n, the number of persons."],"trap":"Multiplying by the total number of scores instead of by the number of persons. In a repeated-measures design one person supplies several scores, so n and N are different numbers, and this formula wants n.","tri":"Symptom: condition means, the number of persons, an SS for the condition effect wanted. -&gt; Diagnosis: SS for the within-subject factor. -&gt; Treatment: grand mean first. Then SS condition = n x sum of (condition mean - grand mean) squared, where n is the number of PERSONS, because in a repeated-measures design n scores went into every condition mean.","out":{"cap":"Mean seconds by board design, 15 lifeguards each using all three","h":["Board design","Mean seconds"],"r":[["Design A","24.0"],["Design B","30.0"],["Design C","27.0"]]}},{"n":33,"q":"Imran Qureshi asks each of 40 volunteers to taste the same three brands of tea, in a different order each, and rate each out of 20. He wants to know whether average rating differs between brands. Which analysis does his design call for?","o":["A one-way ANOVA with 2 and 117 degrees of freedom, treating every rating as independent.","A repeated-measures ANOVA, because every volunteer rates all three brands.","A chi-square test of independence between volunteer and brand, sorting the ratings into a table.","Three separate paired comparisons, one per pair of brands, each at the .05 level."],"a":1,"pct":50,"typ":"which_test","theme":0,"spot":"HINT. Ask one question of the design: does each person appear in one condition, or in all of them? Phrases like the same volunteers, each participant tried all, before and after, and every person rated each mean the measurements are LINKED, which means repeated measures.","plain":"In plain words: the three sets of ratings are not three separate groups of people. They are the same 40 people three times over, so a volunteer who is generous with every brand makes all three of his or her ratings high together. The analysis has to know that.","work":"Step 1. Count the people and the conditions. n = 40 volunteers, k = 3 brands.\nStep 2. Ask whether the observations are independent. They are not. The three ratings from one volunteer share whatever makes that volunteer generous or harsh, so they are correlated.\nStep 3. Rule out the between-subjects F. A one-way ANOVA assumes 120 independent ratings. Here there are only 40 independent people, and treating them as 120 would understate the uncertainty.\nStep 4. Name the right test and its df. Repeated-measures ANOVA with df model = k - 1 = 2 and df error = (40 - 1)(3 - 1) = 39 x 2 = 78. So F(2, 78).\nStep 5. Note the bonus the design brings. Because each volunteer is their own comparison, the steady differences between volunteers come out of the error term and the test is more sensitive than a between-subjects version with the same number of ratings.\nMemory hook: same people in every condition means repeated measures, and the error df is (n - 1)(k - 1) with n counting PEOPLE.","wrongs":["This treats the 120 ratings as 120 independent observations and gives df = (3 - 1, 120 - 3) = (2, 117). The 120 ratings come from only 40 people, so they are not independent. The correct error df is (40 - 1)(3 - 1) = 78, a good deal smaller.","","Chi-square needs counts in categories, and a rating out of 20 is a number he wants to average, not a category to be tallied. There is nothing here to sort into a table of frequencies.","Three paired comparisons would at least respect the pairing, but running all three at .05 with no overall test and no correction inflates the error rate: with three tests it is 1 - .95^3 = 0.143. The overall repeated-measures F comes first, and corrected pairwise comparisons follow only if it is significant."],"trap":"Counting the measurements instead of the people. The number that drives the error df in a repeated-measures design is n, the number of persons, which here is 40, not 120.","tri":"Symptom: one group of people, each measured under every condition, means to be compared. -&gt; Diagnosis: a within-subject design, so repeated-measures ANOVA. -&gt; Treatment: use the repeated-measures F, with df model = k - 1 and df error = (n - 1)(k - 1) where n counts PERSONS. The person-to-person differences come out of the error term, which is where the extra power comes from."},{"n":34,"q":"Clemence Moreau tests whether a breathing exercise lowers stage fright, 14 musicians per group, and finds a difference in the expected direction, p = .19. Her power to detect the effect size she expected was .28. How should she write this up?","o":["The exercise does not work, since p is well above .05.","The exercise does work, since a power below .50 means p should be halved before comparing with .05.","The result is inconclusive, since the effect she found is too small to matter.","Her study was too small, so this is not enough evidence rather than no effect."],"a":3,"pct":50,"typ":"power","theme":0,"spot":"HINT. A non-significant result, a small sample, and a power figure well below .80. That is the power question, and the answer is never \"the effect does not exist\". It is \"this study could not have found it\".","plain":"In plain words: with 14 people per group and a power of .28 she would have missed a real effect of the size she expected about seven times out of ten. So failing to find it tells her almost nothing about whether it is there.","work":"Step 1. Translate the power figure. Power .28 means that IF the effect she expected is really there, a study like hers finds it 28 times in 100. It misses it 72 times in 100.\nStep 2. Ask what her p = .19 can rule out. Very little. A miss was the most likely outcome even in a world where the exercise works exactly as she hoped.\nStep 3. Say what failing to reject H0 means. Not enough evidence to reject it. Never evidence that it is true. You cannot prove the absence of an effect by failing to find it with a blunt instrument.\nStep 4. Give the fix. More musicians per group is the main lever. Measuring each musician before and after, so that each is their own comparison, would also raise power, as would a less noisy measure of stage fright.\nStep 5. Write the honest sentence: the study was consistent with an effect of the expected size and also with no effect, and it cannot separate the two.\nMemory hook: low power turns a null result into a shrug, not into a no. Absence of evidence is not evidence of absence.","wrongs":["This is accepting H0, which no significance test licenses, and here it is especially unsafe because the study was almost certain to miss the effect even if it was real.","There is no rule anywhere that allows a p value to be halved because of low power. Low power affects the chance of DETECTING an effect; it does not change the p value you actually got, and it certainly does not turn a null result into a positive one.","The problem is not that the effect was too small to matter. She has no reliable estimate of the size of the effect at all, because with 14 per group her estimate is very imprecise. Practical importance is a separate judgement from statistical sensitivity.",""],"trap":"Reading p = .19 as a finding of no difference. Power is what turns a null result into information, and .28 is far too low to turn this one into anything.","tri":"Symptom: p above .05, small n, low power reported. -&gt; Diagnosis: a non-significant result from an underpowered study. -&gt; Treatment: do not accept H0. Say that the study lacked the sensitivity to detect the effect, that the result is therefore uninformative rather than negative, and that the remedy is a bigger sample (or a within-subject design, or a more precise measure)."},{"n":35,"q":"Folake Adeyemi records words recalled by each of 12 participants after each of three kinds of break, all taking all three. The residual row is blank, below. What is the residual sum of squares, and its degrees of freedom?","o":["276.0 with 22 df","276.0 with 33 df","444.0 with 22 df","672.0 with 22 df"],"a":0,"pct":50,"typ":"rm_partition","theme":0,"spot":"HINT. A repeated-measures table with the residual row missing. The whole question is the partition: the total splits into exactly three pieces here, persons, conditions and residual, so the missing piece is whatever is left. The df works the same way, and (n - 1)(k - 1) is the formula to confirm it.","plain":"In plain words: the total variation in all 36 scores is made of three things. Some people simply remember more than others. Some breaks work better than others. Whatever is left over, the bit that is neither a steady person difference nor a steady break difference, is the residual.","work":"Step 1. Confirm the design from the table. Between-persons df = 11 means n - 1 = 11, so n = 12 persons. Break-type df = 2 means k - 1 = 2, so k = 3 conditions. Total df = 35 means N - 1 = 35, so N = 36 scores, and 12 x 3 = 36. Everything agrees.\nStep 2. Subtract to get the residual SS. 840.0 - 396.0 - 168.0 = 276.0.\nStep 3. Get the residual df from the formula. (n - 1)(k - 1) = (12 - 1)(3 - 1) = 11 x 2 = 22.\nStep 4. Check it by subtraction too. 35 - 11 - 2 = 22. The two routes agree, which is the strongest check you can run on this question.\nStep 5. Carry on one step if you want the test. MS residual = 276.0 / 22 = 12.5455, MS break = 168.0 / 2 = 84.0, so F(2, 22) = 84.0 / 12.5455 = 6.70.\nMemory hook: THREE pieces in a repeated-measures table, not two. Persons come out first, and that is exactly why this design is more sensitive than a between-subjects one.","wrongs":["","The SS is right but the df is not. 33 is N - k = 36 - 3, which is the error df you would use if these were three separate groups of people. In a repeated-measures design 11 df have already gone to the person differences, so the residual gets (n - 1)(k - 1) = 22. Notice also that 11 + 2 + 33 = 46, which is more than the 35 df available.","This subtracts only the person SS: 840.0 - 396.0 = 444.0, leaving the break effect inside the residual. All three rows have to come out of the total, and the residual is what is left after BOTH.","This subtracts only the condition SS: 840.0 - 168.0 = 672.0, leaving the person differences inside the residual. That is the between-subjects error term, and keeping it would throw away the whole advantage of measuring the same people repeatedly."],"trap":"Treating the repeated-measures table as if it had only two pieces, model and error. There are three. Write SST = SS persons + SS conditions + SS residual before you substitute anything.","tri":"Symptom: a repeated-measures table with SS total, SS persons and SS conditions printed and the residual missing. -&gt; Diagnosis: partitioning SS total. -&gt; Treatment: SS residual = SS total - SS persons - SS conditions. df residual = (n - 1)(k - 1) with n = PERSONS, and it must be whatever is left of the total df once the other rows have taken theirs.","out":{"cap":"Repeated-measures ANOVA: words recalled after three kinds of break","h":["Source","SS","df"],"r":[["Between persons","396.0","11"],["Break type","168.0","2"],["Residual","?","?"],["Total","840.0","35"]]}},{"n":36,"q":"Veronika Halas compares how long people take to finish a crossword at three difficulty levels. She can give every solver all three or each solver one. What is the main statistical advantage of giving every solver all three?","o":["It removes the need for random assignment, since there are no groups to assign.","It makes the three sets of times independent, which is what the F test requires.","Each solver is their own comparison, so solver differences come out of the error term.","It guarantees that the three sets of times will be normally distributed, since each solver contributes to all three."],"a":2,"pct":50,"typ":"within_between","theme":0,"spot":"HINT. Two ways of running the same study, one with everybody in every condition and one with each person in a single condition, and a question about the advantage. The answer is always about the ERROR TERM: a within-subject design takes the person-to-person differences out of it.","plain":"In plain words: people differ a lot in how fast they do crosswords, and that difference is just noise when you want to compare difficulty levels. If each person does all three, you can compare each person with themselves and that noise disappears from the comparison.","work":"Step 1. Think about where the noise comes from in the between-subjects version. Any difference between the three groups mixes the difficulty effect with the fact that different people are in each group.\nStep 2. Now the within-subject version. Each solver gives three times, so you can look at how much slower THIS solver was on the hard crossword than on the easy one. Their general speed cancels out.\nStep 3. See it in the table. The repeated-measures analysis pulls out a between-persons row with n - 1 df before the error is computed, so the residual carries only (n - 1)(k - 1) df of genuinely unexplained variation.\nStep 4. Follow the consequence. A smaller MS error means a larger F for the same difference between means, which means more power from fewer people.\nStep 5. Name the price. Doing three crosswords in a row brings practice and fatigue, so the order has to be varied across solvers. That is counterbalancing, and it is the standard safeguard.\nMemory hook: your own baseline is the best baseline. Removing person differences shrinks the error term, and a smaller error term is a bigger F.","wrongs":["Random assignment is still needed, but of the ORDER rather than of the condition. Each solver still has to meet the three crosswords in an order decided at random or systematically counterbalanced, or practice effects will masquerade as difficulty effects.","This is backwards. Giving every solver all three makes the three sets of times DEPENDENT, since each solver appears in all of them. That dependence is exactly why the repeated-measures F is used instead of the ordinary one-way F.","","Nothing about the design makes the data normal. Normality is a property of the measurements, and the repeated-measures F assumes it just as the between-subjects F does. What the design buys is precision, not a free assumption."],"trap":"Thinking independence is the advantage. Independence is what the design gives up. What it gains is precision, by taking the differences between people out of the error term.","tri":"Symptom: a choice between a within-subject and a between-subject version of the same study. -&gt; Diagnosis: why a within-subject design is more powerful. -&gt; Treatment: say that between-person variation is removed from the error term, so MS error is smaller, so F is larger for the same real difference, so fewer participants are needed. Then name the cost: order effects, which is what counterbalancing is for."},{"n":37,"q":"Nikolaj Sorensen wants to write that standing desks reduce afternoon sluggishness. He has a clear correlation across 300 staff who chose whether to take one, the desks came first, and the link survives adjustment for age and job type. What is still missing for a causal claim?","o":["Nothing, because a correlation, the right time order and adjustment are the three criteria he has met.","He has not ruled out other ways staff who chose a standing desk differ.","A larger sample, because 300 staff is too few for a causal conclusion.","A smaller p value, because a causal claim needs p below .001."],"a":1,"pct":50,"typ":"causal_criteria","theme":0,"spot":"HINT. A researcher who has ticked several boxes and wants to claim cause. Look for what is NOT in the list, and it is almost always random assignment. Adjusting, controlling and matching are all second-best substitutes for it.","plain":"In plain words: he has shown the two go together, in the right order, and that three or four obvious explanations do not account for it. But the staff chose whether to take a standing desk, and people who choose one may differ in a dozen ways he has not measured.","work":"Step 1. Credit what he has. A real association, measured after the desks arrived, that is not explained by age or job type.\nStep 2. Ask who decided who got a standing desk. The staff themselves. That is the weak point.\nStep 3. Name the kind of confounder that is left. Staff who ask for a standing desk may be more health-conscious, may sleep better, may drink less at lunch, may have more control over their day. None of these is in his adjustment.\nStep 4. See why adjustment cannot finish the job. You can only adjust for what you measured. There is no way to adjust for a variable you never thought of, and there is always at least one.\nStep 5. Say what would settle it. Allocate the desks at random. Then the two groups differ only by chance, in everything measured and unmeasured alike, and a difference in sluggishness can be put down to the desks.\nMemory hook: adjustment fixes the confounders you LISTED. Randomisation fixes the ones you never thought of. Only one of those is a short list.","wrongs":["Those three are a good start but they are not sufficient. The missing condition is that no OTHER systematic difference between the groups can account for the result, and statistical adjustment only covers the variables he happened to measure.","","Sample size changes how precise his estimate is, not whether it is causal. Three thousand staff who chose their own desks would give a tighter correlation and exactly the same confounding problem.","There is no p value small enough to turn an observational comparison into a causal one. The p value answers a question about chance; causation is a question about design."],"trap":"Treating statistical adjustment as equivalent to random assignment. The exam makes the adjustment list look impressive on purpose. Ask what is not on it.","tri":"Symptom: an observational study with time order and statistical adjustment, and a causal claim proposed. -&gt; Diagnosis: the criteria for a causal claim. -&gt; Treatment: association and time order are necessary but not sufficient. The remaining requirement is that the groups do not differ systematically in any other relevant way, and only RANDOM ASSIGNMENT delivers that, because it balances the variables nobody measured as well as the ones they did."},{"n":38,"q":"Salim Benkirane measured five outcomes in a study-skills trial. One came out at p = .03, the other four around .40. His supervisor suggests building the paper on that one, as the planned outcome. What is the right response?","o":["Report all five and say the highlighted one was picked after the results, which makes a .05 test generous.","Follow the supervisor, because the data show which outcome matters and the other four only distract.","Abandon the study, because one significant result out of five can never be published.","Report all five but divide every p value by five, the repair a .05 test needs."],"a":0,"pct":50,"typ":"ethics","theme":0,"spot":"HINT. Several outcomes tested, one of them significant, and a suggestion to present it as the plan. That is the research integrity question. The answer is always full disclosure, never a clever repair and never abandoning the work.","plain":"In plain words: if you look in five places, finding one surprise is close to expected. Pretending you only ever looked in one place makes a lucky result sound like a discovery. The honest fix is to say what you did.","work":"Step 1. Work out how surprising one hit really is. Five independent tests at .05 give a false-alarm chance of 1 - .95^5 = 0.226, roughly one chance in four. One \"significant\" result among five is close to what you would expect from nothing at all.\nStep 2. See what the selective write-up destroys. The reader is told the chance of a false alarm was 5 per cent, when it was more like 23 per cent. The p value has been quietly stripped of its meaning.\nStep 3. Identify the integrity breach. It is not the analysis, it is the concealment. Presenting a post hoc choice as a pre-planned one misreports how the research was done.\nStep 4. State the fix. All five outcomes in the paper, a plain sentence saying the highlighted one was chosen after seeing the data, and the finding labelled as exploratory. A correction such as Bonferroni may be applied as well, but the disclosure is the part that cannot be skipped.\nStep 5. Note the better habit for next time. Decide and register the primary outcome BEFORE collecting data. Then one test genuinely is one test.\nMemory hook: you may analyse the data any way you like. You may not hide how many ways you tried.","wrongs":["","The data cannot tell you which outcome mattered in advance, which is the claim the paper would be making. And the other four results are not a distraction: they are precisely what a reader needs in order to judge the fifth.","One result in five may well be publishable, as an exploratory finding, honestly labelled, with the other four reported alongside it. Abandoning the work hides the evidence just as effectively as the supervisor plan does.","This misremembers the correction. Bonferroni divides the SIGNIFICANCE LEVEL by the number of tests, comparing each p with .05/5 = .01, which makes the test stricter. Dividing the p VALUES makes them smaller and the test more generous, which is backwards. And no arithmetic repairs the failure to disclose."],"trap":"Looking for a technical fix for an honesty problem. A correction can be part of the answer, but the examiner is testing whether you say what was actually done.","tri":"Symptom: multiple outcomes, one significant, pressure to report it as planned. -&gt; Diagnosis: selective reporting, sometimes called p-hacking or HARKing. -&gt; Treatment: report every outcome that was measured, state that the highlighted one was selected after the results were seen, and either apply a correction for multiple testing or present the finding as exploratory and needing replication."},{"n":39,"q":"Zhen Liu has every participant try all four seating positions in a simulator, each position coming first for a quarter of the participants, second for another quarter, and so on. What does that achieve?","o":["It makes the four conditions independent of one another, since no position sits in a fixed place in the schedule.","It removes the need to measure every participant in every condition.","It raises the effective sample size from n participants to 4n.","It stops practice and fatigue building up on one position, spreading any order effect evenly."],"a":3,"pct":50,"typ":"counterbalancing","theme":0,"spot":"HINT. A within-subject design plus a description of how the ORDER was varied. That is counterbalancing, and the thing it protects against is order effects: practice, fatigue, boredom, learning. It never changes the sample size and never makes the conditions independent.","plain":"In plain words: whoever goes last is tired, and whoever goes first has never done it before. If one seat were always last, that tiredness would look like a fact about the seat. Rotating the order spreads the tiredness over all four seats equally.","work":"Step 1. Name the threat. Doing four runs in a row changes the driver. Early runs are unpractised; late runs are tired.\nStep 2. See what goes wrong without counterbalancing. If seat D were always last, every score for seat D would carry the fatigue of a fourth run. Seat D would look worse, and she could never tell the seat from the fatigue.\nStep 3. See what the rotation does. Seat D is first for a quarter of the participants, second for a quarter, and so on. The fatigue is still there, but it now falls equally on all four seats.\nStep 4. State the result precisely. Order is no longer confounded with condition. The order effect has not been removed from the data; it has been made the same for every condition, so it cannot create a difference between them.\nStep 5. Note what it does not do. There are still n participants, the four sets of scores are still linked person by person, and the repeated-measures analysis is still the one to use.\nMemory hook: counterbalancing does not remove the order effect. It shares it out fairly.","wrongs":["The conditions remain dependent, because the same people are in all four. That is a feature of the design, not something the ordering can change, and it is why the analysis has to be a repeated-measures one.","Counterbalancing is only possible BECAUSE every participant does every condition. It is a way of arranging a within-subject design, not a way of avoiding one.","There are still n participants. Each gives four scores, but those four are linked, so they do not count as four independent observations. This is exactly the mistake that produces the wrong error df in a repeated-measures ANOVA.",""],"trap":"Saying that counterbalancing removes the order effect. It balances it. The practice and fatigue are still in the scores; they are just no longer sitting on one condition.","tri":"Symptom: every participant in every condition, with the order deliberately varied. -&gt; Diagnosis: counterbalancing against order effects. -&gt; Treatment: say that practice and fatigue grow with position in the sequence, that an unvaried order would confound position in the sequence with the condition, and that counterbalancing makes the order effect equal across conditions so it no longer masquerades as a condition effect."},{"n":40,"q":"Siobhan Doherty measures reaction time in the same 26 participants under four levels of background chatter. Her assumption check reports a statistic of .56 with p = .018. What should she do, and why?","o":["Nothing, because a p below .05 on this check means the assumption has been met, so the uncorrected F row can stand.","Switch to a one-way ANOVA treating the four levels as independent groups.","The variances of the differences between conditions are not equal, so she should read the Greenhouse-Geisser row.","The check says her data are not normally distributed, so she should use a rank test."],"a":2,"pct":50,"typ":"sphericity","theme":0,"spot":"HINT. Repeated measures with three or more conditions, and an assumption check reported with a W-like statistic and a p value. That is the sphericity check. Two things to get right: a SMALL p means the assumption has FAILED, and the fix is a correction to the df, not a different kind of test.","plain":"In plain words: the repeated-measures F assumes that the differences between any two conditions are about equally spread out. Her p of .018 says they are not. The cure is to read the same F against adjusted degrees of freedom, which makes the test a little more cautious.","work":"Step 1. Write down what the check is testing. H0: the variances of the differences between every pair of conditions are equal, which is what sphericity means.\nStep 2. Read the p value against .05. .018 is smaller, so reject H0. Sphericity does NOT hold.\nStep 3. Understand why it matters. The uncorrected repeated-measures F uses df (k - 1) and (n - 1)(k - 1), here 3 and 75. Those df are only right under sphericity. Without it the test rejects H0 too readily.\nStep 4. Apply the fix. Multiply both df by the correction factor and read the F against the smaller df. In the output that is the Greenhouse-Geisser line. The F value is unchanged; only the df and so the p value move.\nStep 5. Say what to report. The corrected df, usually with one decimal because they are no longer whole numbers, and the corrected p.\nMemory hook: on an assumption check, a SMALL p is BAD NEWS. You wanted this one to come out non-significant.","wrongs":["This reads the p value backwards, and the uncorrected row is exactly the one she should not read here. The null hypothesis of the check is that sphericity holds, so a p below .05 means it has been rejected. On assumption checks you are hoping for a LARGE p.","A between-subjects one-way ANOVA would be wrong for this design whatever the assumption check said, because the same 26 people provide all four sets of times. Abandoning the model is not a way round one of its assumptions: treating them as independent groups would throw away the pairing and inflate the error term.","","This check is not about normality. It is about whether the differences between pairs of conditions are equally variable. Normality is a separate assumption with separate checks, and a departure from it would call for a different remedy."],"trap":"Reading the assumption check like a result, where a small p is a success. Here a small p means the F you were about to read is not trustworthy as printed.","tri":"Symptom: repeated measures, k of 3 or more, an assumption check with a small p. -&gt; Diagnosis: sphericity has been violated. -&gt; Treatment: the null hypothesis of this check is that sphericity HOLDS, so p = .018 rejects it. Use a corrected F: Greenhouse-Geisser or Huynh-Feldt multiply both df by a factor below 1, which raises the critical value and raises the p value of the main test. The F statistic itself does not change."}]}];
